{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_0","type":"agent_message","text":"I’ll rewrite the five design documents around the in-instance engine and dao.stream binding, preserving the register semantics and conformance fixtures. I’ll check the stream outcome contract before defining buffer handling, so the binding doesn’t reintroduce the dissolved service protocol.\n"}}
{"type":"item.started","item":{"id":"item_1","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/zsh -lc \"wc -l docs/design/{datom.world,yang.wasm,yin.vm.engine,yin.vm.debruijn.register,yin.vm.rust-kernel}.md; sed -n '34,180p' docs/design/dao.stream.md; rg -n '\"'^#{1,4} |''^'\"\\\\|.*(full|blocked|refused)|buffer|size' docs/design/dao.stream.md | head -100; rg --files collab | rg '1792353000000.*(findings|report)'\"","aggregated_output":"","exit_code":null,"status":"in_progress"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_1","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/zsh -lc \"wc -l docs/design/{datom.world,yang.wasm,yin.vm.engine,yin.vm.debruijn.register,yin.vm.rust-kernel}.md; sed -n '34,180p' docs/design/dao.stream.md; rg -n '\"'^#{1,4} |''^'\"\\\\|.*(full|blocked|refused)|buffer|size' docs/design/dao.stream.md | head -100; rg --files collab | rg '1792353000000.*(findings|report)'\"","aggregated_output":"     236 docs/design/datom.world.md\n     565 docs/design/yang.wasm.md\n    1302 docs/design/yin.vm.engine.md\n    1738 docs/design/yin.vm.debruijn.register.md\n    1338 docs/design/yin.vm.rust-kernel.md\n    5179 total\nThe Invariants below are these denials in operational form.\n\nThe design separates five concepts:\n\n1. A **creation specification** is data for creating a new logical stream.\n2. A **handle** is a host-local operational stream value — the\n   implementation of the `dao.stream` protocols.\n3. A **logical-stream identity** is transport-independent plain data shared by\n   every handle and descriptor for one stream.\n4. A **portable descriptor** is reachability data used to attach to an existing\n   stream; it includes the logical-stream identity but is not that identity.\n5. A **cursor** is immutable interpreter-owned observation state for one\n   logical stream.\n\nCreation is not attachment. A descriptor is not a creation specification or an\nidentity. A handle is not portable identity. A cursor is not handle-owned\nstate. A cursor is a value and has no identity of its own: a VM that lets\nprograms advance a cursor keeps it in a mutable **cursor cell**, a box that\nhas identity and is named by a sealed **cursor-ref** (`yin.vm`; the four-word\nvocabulary cursor, cursor cell, cursor-ref and cell is defined in\n`yin.vm.universal-continuation-format.md` §7.5.3).\n\nSeven operations range over them, each returning an outcome map (see\nResult Convention):\n\n| Operation    | Consumes               | Yields             |\n|--------------|------------------------|--------------------|\n| `create!`    | creation specification | handle outcome     |\n| `attach!`    | portable descriptor    | handle outcome     |\n| `descriptor` | handle                 | descriptor outcome |\n| `cursor`     | handle, anchor         | cursor outcome     |\n| `next`       | handle, cursor         | read outcome       |\n| `append!`    | handle, value          | write outcome      |\n| `close!`     | handle                 | close outcome      |\n\nA handle implements the subset of the **handle surface** its transport\ndeclares (see Surfaces). `create!` and `attach!` are not part of it: they\nconsume no handle and produce one.\n\n## Invariants\n\n- Stream elements are data. DaoStream assigns them no intrinsic meaning.\n- Reading is non-destructive. A reader advances its own cursor, not the stream.\n- Multiple cursors may observe the same retained value independently.\n- Interpreters own cursors and decide when to call `next`.\n- A runtime may drive an interpreter, but DaoStream does not require a runtime.\n- Closing a stream changes future availability but does not itself erase\n  retained history.\n- Retention may be bounded. A cursor over evicted history receives a gap\n  outcome as data; the reader decides what missing data means.\n- All expected operational outcomes are data, not exceptions or callbacks.\n- No operation waits. Every operation returns what is true at the moment it is\n  called; an answer not yet knowable arrives later as data.\n- No hidden global state. DaoStream keeps no registry of streams, handles, or\n  observers, and no operation consults ambient state.\n- A logical stream has an identity that is plain data, the same through every\n  handle on it, stable across attachment lifecycles and across serialization.\n  Projecting that identity never fails.\n\nEvery clause below must be consistent with these invariants, and most derive\nfrom one directly. Where a clause is instead an interoperability decision — the\ndescriptor's properties, the result keyword namespace, the anchor set — it is\nstated as a decision rather than dressed as a deduction. A proposed addition\nthat contradicts an invariant does not belong in this contract; one that merely\ncannot be deduced from them must say which decision it is and why.\n\n## Result Convention\n\nEvery DaoStream operation returns data.\n\n- Each operation's set of outcomes is **exhaustive**: a transport may produce\n  fewer of them, never any outside the set. Which subset it produces is part of\n  its declared nature (see Surfaces). An outcome is a map whose\n  `:dao.stream/outcome` key names the outcome with a qualified keyword. Every\n  operation returns such a map — including projections like `descriptor` and\n  `cursor`, whose success map carries the projected value.\n- `:dao.stream/outcome` is present in every result map and is never repeated\n  in the per-outcome tables below; the keys those tables name are required in\n  addition to it.\n- Result maps are **open**: the contract fixes which keys are required per\n  outcome, and a consumer must ignore keys it does not understand. This is\n  what allows future extensions (for example readiness notification) to be\n  added without breaking existing consumers.\n- Each outcome set is exhaustive **for this version of the contract**. A\n  consumer that receives an outcome it does not recognize treats it as: not\n  `ok`; nothing observed; for a write, effect unknown and no automatic\n  retry; and not retryable unless the result map carries\n  `:dao.stream/retry? true`. It does not throw, because an unrecognized\n  outcome is a newer contract, not a defect in this host's assembly. A\n  transport may carry `:dao.stream/retry? true` on any non-`ok` outcome to\n  say that asking again is the right response.\n- `:dao.stream/refused` is an outcome `cursor`, `next` and `append!` may\n  produce: a policy composed on this handle declined the operation; nothing\n  was observed and nothing was appended; not retryable unless the map\n  carries `:dao.stream/retry? true`. `descriptor` and `close!` never produce\n  it: a name is not gated and a lifecycle transition is not declined. What\n  the policy is, and how it is composed, is outside this contract.\n- Exceptions are reserved for defects in the host's own assembly of\n  DaoStream, detected before any operation runs. Everything observable at an\n  operation returns data: closed, full, blocked, end, gap, not found,\n  malformed input, and transport defects are all outcomes, never exceptions\n  and never callbacks.\n- All qualified keywords in results live under `:dao.stream/…`.\n\n## The IO Model\n\nThree models are available for expressing IO. DaoStream is deliberately the\nthird.\n\n**Blocking (synchronous) IO.** The operation parks until it has an answer,\nthen returns it. Control flow stays sequential and the call site reads as\nordinary code, which is the whole of its appeal. The cost is that something\nmust wait — a thread per pending operation, held for its duration — and it\nrequires a host that can park at all.\n\n**Callback (asynchronous) IO.** The operation registers a continuation and\nreturns at once; the runtime invokes the continuation when the answer\narrives. Nothing waits, so one thread carries many pending operations: this\nis the model that scales. Its cost is inverted control. The caller no longer\ndecides when it observes the answer, and the inversion is viral — on a host\nwithout blocking, a promise cannot be absorbed inside a function, because\nawaiting returns to the caller at the first suspension point and makes the\ncaller's own result a promise in turn. Whatever is reached from a callback\nis reached from a callback.\n\n**Non-blocking polling.** The operation returns immediately with whatever is\ntrue now, including the answer \"nothing yet, ask again.\" Nothing parks and\nnothing is inverted: the caller keeps control and decides when to ask again.\nThis is POSIX `O_NONBLOCK` without `select`, and it is DaoStream's model.\n\n`next` is total and non-blocking (see Reading): every state of the stream\nhas a defined data answer, and `:dao.stream/blocked` is `EAGAIN` — this\nposition holds nothing yet. `append!`'s `:dao.stream/full` is the same\nanswer for writes. No operation in this contract parks, and none takes a\ncallback; the Result Convention's \"never exceptions and never callbacks\" is\nthis model stated as a rule about results.\n\nThe rule generalizes past `next`: **no operation waits.** An operation that\ncannot yet know its answer returns the outcome true at the moment it is\ncalled, and the eventual answer arrives the way every other fact does — as\ndata, observed later, through an ordinary operation or on a stream. A\nDaoStream operation is never a request for something to happen and be\nreported back. It is a question about now.\n\n### Why this model and not the other two\n\nThe hosts do not offer the same choices. The JVM has both blocking and\n1:# DaoStream Design\n7:## Purpose\n73:## Invariants\n100:## Result Convention\n138:## The IO Model\n178:### Why this model and not the other two\n194:### What it costs\n204:### The readiness extension\n220:## Creation and Attachment\n259:somewhere with nothing behind it — a ring buffer's descriptor on any other\n263:### Envelopes\n271:(for example a ring buffer's capacity), and validated by the selected handler\n373:### Host Dispatch\n381:{:dao.stream/ringbuffer {:dao.stream/create ringbuffer/create!\n382:                         :dao.stream/attach ringbuffer/attach!}\n409:## Surfaces\n477:reports `gap`); another policy answers the same condition, named (a ring buffer\n483:### Where the asynchrony goes\n501:## Cursors\n569:## Reading\n578:| `:dao.stream/blocked`         | Nothing at this position yet; retry later.                                    | —                                                                                       |\n583:| `:dao.stream/refused`         | A policy composed on this handle declined the read; nothing was observed.     | —                                                                                       |\n592:## Writing\n599:| `:dao.stream/full`            | The transport does not accept the value; nothing was appended. Whether this is transient or permanent is the transport's declared nature (see Retention and Gaps). | —             |\n602:| `:dao.stream/refused`         | A policy composed on this handle declined the append; nothing was appended.                                                                          | —             |\n607:ring buffer it is the logical stream itself, so `ok` places the value in it; for\n614:retry, buffer, or notify on its behalf. The three in-memory transports\n615:(`ringbuffer`, `memory-log`, `ws`) declare clean failure: their\n627:## Close\n677:## Concurrency\n700:## Retention and Gaps\n704:them through its creation specification. A ring buffer retains a bounded window and evicts\n706:buffer refuses transiently. Which outcomes a given stream can produce —\n729:### Complete history\n789:## Composition\n809:## Explicitly Absent\n828:  local ring buffer.\n840:## Open Decisions\n849:transports (`ringbuffer`, `memory-log`, `ws`), and their consumers.\n858:### OD-4. A logical stream can close but cannot end\n898:### OD-5. \"No operation waits\" binds the seven operations and nothing above them\n934:  framebuffer as over a socket.\n990:### Recorded, not proposed\ncollab/1792353000000-architect-one-abi-ruling.deepseek-v4-pro.findings.md\ncollab/1792353000000-architect-one-abi-ruling.claude-fable-5-1.findings.md\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
{"type":"item.started","item":{"id":"item_2","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/zsh -lc \"cat collab/1792353000000-architect-one-abi-ruling.deepseek-v4-pro.findings.md; cat collab/1792353000000-architect-one-abi-ruling.claude-fable-5-1.findings.md; rg --files collab | rg '1792353000000.*argon'\"","aggregated_output":"","exit_code":null,"status":"in_progress"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_2","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/zsh -lc \"cat collab/1792353000000-architect-one-abi-ruling.deepseek-v4-pro.findings.md; cat collab/1792353000000-architect-one-abi-ruling.claude-fable-5-1.findings.md; rg --files collab | rg '1792353000000.*argon'\"","aggregated_output":"# Round-2 answer — the one-ABI ruling (deepseek-v4-pro)\n\n## Q1 — Does the ruling change my round-1 position?\n\nYes, and in a way I should state plainly rather than paper over: **round-1 me was\nright about the diagnosis and wrong about the category.**\n\nWhat survives:\n\n- The *diagnosis* of the two opposed properties survives, but downgraded from\n  \"two different kinds of boundary\" to \"one boundary placed where crossing is\n  synchronous and state-local.\" The synchronous/state-local vs async/canonical\n  contrast, the one-privileged-reader vs no-privileged-reader contrast — all of\n  it is still the correct *cost accounting* of *why* the seam, as currently\n  drawn, is expensive. It is no longer a proof that the seam *must* be a\n  different kind of thing.\n- The *value-representation point* survives intact, and the ruling actually\n  strengthens it (see Q3): in-machine handles vs canonical bytes, the three\n  encoding domains, tag-13, non-canonicalizable continuations. This is a *hard\n  lower bound* that is orthogonal to the one-ABI/two-ABI question and does not\n  dissolve at the node lane.\n- The red-herring status of \"one `exchange` vs N typed imports\" survives: the\n  import-surface shape was never doctrine, and the ruling confirms it by\n  locating all the *doctrinal* weight in the one-ABI principle instead.\n- My round-1 identification of \"move the interpreter loop / compile the engine\n  into the guest\" as the *only real alternative axis* is now **vindicated** — the\n  ruling names exactly that (the engine-in-guest node lane) as the destination.\n\nWhat I now revise:\n\n- **I retract \"no design in which guest-host is dao.stream.\"** That was the\n  category error. The correct statement is \"no design in which guest-host is\n  `dao.stream` *while the engine lives in the host*.\" Once the engine is\n  compiled into the guest, the synchronous services (`:load-free`, primitive\n  apply, GC materialization, closure minting) *cease to be boundary crossings at\n  all* — they become in-machine operations — and the only thing left crossing\n  the boundary is `dao.stream`. My round-1 \"indispensable reason\" was really a\n  description of *why the line is expensive to draw where the current design\n  draws it*; I elevated an economics fact to a kind-necessity. The ruling is\n  correct that this was wrong.\n- Correspondingly, I demote my \"indispensable reason\" from \"the seam is a\n  different kind of boundary\" to \"the seam, as currently placed, has\n  per-instruction synchronous crossings whose cost is the justification for the\n  second ABI.\" That is a substantive concession, and it is the right one.\n\nNet: round-1's mechanism survives, round-1's metaphysics does not. The ruling\nmoves the argument from \"kinds\" to \"placement,\" and on that axis the ruling wins.\n\n## Q2 — Critique of the ruling: is \"performance is the sole valid justification\" correct?\n\n**The conclusion is correct, and I want to say that without hedging, because the\ncandidate \"other reasons\" are mostly either ABI-agnostic or actually cut the\n*other* way. But the ruling's stated form is incomplete in two specific places,\nand I'll name them.**\n\nThe cleanest way to see the ruling's truth is to notice what the second ABI has\nthat `dao.stream` lacks: **synchrony, and task-local (non-canonical) value\nidentity.** Those two things, jointly, *are* the second ABI. Every candidate\njustification must either reduce to those two, or fail. Going through them:\n\n- **Capability / security granularity.** Real, but **reducible**. The narrow,\n  checked, versioned surface that prevents the guest from forging closures,\n  continuations, or typed effects is a property of the *host-side checks*\n  (custody gates, check-then-commit), not of the medium. `dao.stream` already\n  enforces the same thing — effects are plain data, custody gates check them,\n  an unimplemented effect yields a qualified `unsupported` result. The handle\n  mechanism (tag-13) does let the second ABI pass *non-portable* capabilities\n  (a live cursor, a just-minted closure) to the guest without canonicalizing\n  them — but that is the *canonicalization cost* term wearing a capability hat:\n  the reason you pass a handle rather than a descriptor is that re-attaching to\n  a descriptor per instruction is absurd, i.e. *performance*. The fact that a\n  handle *happens to be* non-portable (and therefore can't leak as authority) is\n  a consequence, not a motivation. Reducible.\n\n- **Determinism / cross-embedder parity.** This is the one the question lists as\n  a possible justification that is in fact a **reason to prefer `dao.stream`,\n  not a second ABI**. Determinism is guaranteed by the *shared transition\n  implementation* (single-engine-port, one-transition-two-dispatchers), not by\n  having two surfaces — and it is *strictly harder* on the private ABI. §E.4.3\n  (the deterministic-traversal prerequisite, minted `c-N` ids, sorted map/set\n  walks) exists *only because* the private ABI carries unordered in-machine\n  values and mints ids. At the `dao.stream` floor, determinism reduces to\n  \"canonical encoding is total and ordered,\" which is comparatively trivial. So\n  determinism is a *cost the second ABI imposes*, not a justification for it.\n  The ruling would be *stronger* if it said this explicitly.\n\n- **Freeze-vs-extend of the ISA.** Real as an axis, but **reducible — it is the\n  placement axis itself, already covered by the ruling.** The frozen opcode set\n  (r2, 23 opcodes, \"no new guest register namespace\") vs the extensible service\n  operations is a genuine distinction, but it maps onto \"opcode vs effect,\" not\n  \"opcode vs second-ABI-operation\": a second-ABI operation (`exchange` for\n  `resolve-var`) is *also* extensible (versioned), and a `dao.stream` effect is\n  *also* extensible (additive data). So freeze-vs-extend does not distinguish a\n  second ABI from the stream; it is the *content* of the line-drawing decision\n  (the `:define` promotion is exactly a move along it).\n\n- **Failure isolation.** Real, but **reducible / ABI-agnostic**. Check-then-\n  commit atomicity, \"a refused transition leaves guest state unchanged,\" \"a\n  panic poisons only its machine,\" `BAD_MESSAGE` before semantic work — these\n  are properties of the host engine's implementation, satisfied identically\n  whether the medium is a mailbox or a stream (`dao.stream` has its own\n  qualified-unsupported / malformed-input failure model).\n\n- **Independent versioning.** **Reducible** — a *consequence* of having a second\n  surface (you must version it), not a reason for it. `dao.stream` effect\n  vocabularies version the same way (qualified envelopes).\n\nSo: every candidate is either ABI-agnostic (capability, freeze-vs-extend,\nfailure isolation, versioning) or actually a *cost* of the second ABI\n(determinism). The ruling's conclusion holds — and I'd sharpen it into a\nstatement the ruling itself gestures at but doesn't quite say: **the second ABI\nhas exactly one property `dao.stream` lacks — synchronous, task-local,\nnon-canonical identity — and that property is nothing other than an expression\nof per-instruction crossing cost. Performance is not merely \"a\" justification;\nit is the *only possible* one, because it is the only thing the second ABI is.**\n\nMy two corrections to the ruling's *form* (not its substance):\n\n1. **\"Performance\" is doing two jobs it should separate.** Crossing *frequency*\n   (how many synchronous crossings per instruction) and *canonicalization cost*\n   (how much must be serialized per crossing, and whether the value even *has* a\n   canonical form — closures, live cursors, reified continuations). The ruling\n   lumps them. They behave differently under the ruling's own logic: crossing\n   frequency dissolves at the node lane; canonicalization cost does *not* (the\n   node lane still appends its effects-out and reads its facts-in as canonical\n   `dao.stream` values). The ruling is about the first; the second is the\n   immovable-floor cost and belongs in Q3, not in \"performance.\"\n\n2. **The ruling lists the second ABI's benefit but not its own cost.** It names\n   \"crossing cost\" as the price of the second ABI, but the second ABI *also*\n   imposes a determinism/parity burden (§E.4.3) and a versioning surface that\n   `dao.stream` does not. Stating the second ABI's full cost — not just its\n   benefit's absence — makes the one-ABI case stronger than the ruling's own\n   phrasing does.\n\n## Q3 — Is the floor argument sound? Can the floor move?\n\n**The core is sound; the word \"immovable\" is overstated, and the question's\nparenthetical is the right instinct.** There are three distinct claims bundled\nunder \"the floor,\" and they need to be pulled apart.\n\n1. **The non-computability seam — sound, and immovable.** A shipped program\n   computes a function of its inputs; a \"world-fact\" (wall clock, socket bytes,\n   another machine's state, a sensor) is *not* a function of the machine's\n   state, because the machine is a closed computation and the world is outside\n   that closure. No relocation of the line makes the world computable. This is\n   the existential core, and it is immovable. Note the ruling states it\n   asymmetrically (\"answers are world-facts, in\"), but it is *symmetric*: the\n   machine's *effects out* are equally non-computable — an effect's *outcome*\n   (did the world accept the write?) is a world-fact. The floor is \"the\n   machine/world boundary, in both directions, is data,\" and `dao.stream` is the\n   minimum because it is the *only* thing that is simultaneously the effect-out\n   channel and the fact-in channel, unified as append/park/resume.\n\n2. **The frontier — movable, and the ruling already concedes this.** \"Which\n   operations are world-facts\" is a *design decision*, not a fact about the\n   world. The `:define` promotion (v4: host service → opcode) is exactly a\n   frontier move: `define` was recognized as a function of store state, not a\n   world side-effect, and pulled inside. So the floor is not a fixed *set* of\n   operations; it is a *frontier*, and the frontier is the design surface. The\n   ruling means to freeze only the seam; its language (\"the floor itself is not\n   movable\") risks freezing the frontier by accident. I'd rewrite it: **the seam\n   is immovable; the frontier is movable, and moving it is precisely the work.**\n\n3. **The parenthetical examples — real, but they don't dissolve the seam, they\n   relocate which facts count.** *Determinism / recorded worlds*: a recorded\n   world (replay log, prior-input snapshot) *is* computable — it's a function of\n   the recorded history, which lives in (or is retrievable from) the machine's\n   state. So a deterministic closed machine has a *trivially small* floor (its\n   only world-fact is the seed of its input tape). This genuinely moves the\n   floor *extensionally*. *Replicated agreement*: does **not** move the floor at\n   all — \"whatever the replicas agree\" is *itself* a world-fact (a process in\n   other machines), and its outcome enters your machine as data. Replication\n   moves the answer's *producer* further into the world and *adds* stream\n   traffic; it changes *which* facts are world-facts, not whether any exist.\n\nSo the synthesis — and it is the part that actually *strengthens* the ruling:\n**only the seam is immovable, in the existential sense; the frontier is movable,\nin the extensional sense.** That yields a clean partition that is the ruling's\nreal content: a *world-fact must* cross on `dao.stream` (it has nowhere else to\ngo); a *non-world-fact* sits on the synchronous ABI *only* for performance. Once\nyou see that, the one-ABI principle has more teeth than the ruling's own wording\ngives it: the second ABI is not \"justified by performance,\" it is \"reserved\nexclusively for non-world-facts whose stream-crossing is too expensive.\"\n\nOne further refinement that ties back to round 1: the canonicalization cost I\nflagged there is the *floor's* cost, not the second ABI's. Even the node lane —\nengine fully in-guest, only `dao.stream` crossing — still must encode its\neffects-out and decode its facts-in as canonical values, and must *refuse* to\ncanonicalize the non-canonicalizable (reified continuations). That cost is\npermanent, orthogonal to the one-ABI question, and the ruling should own it as a\n*floor* fact rather than silently folding it into \"performance.\"\n\n## Q4 — Consequences for the plan (W0–W6)\n\n**The ruling changes the plan's *teleology and accounting*, but not its\n*sequence*. I would not resequence to node-lane-first, and I'd argue against any\nreading that does. What I would change is concrete and mostly about making the\ncurrent plan *honest* under the ruling.**\n\nWhy not resequence: the node lane (engine-in-guest) is a *harder correctness\ntarget* — you must compile collection, custody, module stores, and the FFI\ntwo-step *into* wasm and get byte-identical parity with the host engine — and it\nis **unverifiable without the host engine's conformance oracle first** (the\nK0/W0 oracle, §E.5.1). The mailbox ABI is not merely the provisional thing to be\nshrunk; it is the *measurement instrument* and the *oracle* that the node lane's\ncorrectness depends on. You cannot dissolve a cost you have not yet quantified,\nand you cannot verify the dissolution without the reference. So W0–W5\nmailbox-first is **prerequisite**, not alternative, to the node lane.\n\nWhat I would change:\n\n1. **Flip the W6 attitude from \"fallback\" to \"destination.\"** Today engine-in-\n   guest / node-lowering is written as a contingency (\"*if* crossing cost\n   dominates, prototype the interpreter-loop variant,\" `yang.wasm.md` §6.1). The\n   ruling inverts this: the node lane is the *target*, the mailbox ABI is the\n   *provisional* thing. The *measurement* may still gate *when*, but not\n   *whether*. Keep \"no optimization before W5 correctness\" as a sequencing guard;\n   remove the implication that service-migration is optional.\n\n2. **Make service-migration-into-guest a hard, ruling-mandated W6 deliverable,\n   with a per-operation cost partition.** W6 should produce a table of the\n   required services (§E.3.4's list: handshake, value definition/lookup,\n   free-name/store/gensym, primitive apply, stream/effect/FFI, scheduler-round,\n   collect/pin, module attach/spawn) partitioned into three buckets:\n   (a) already-compiled pure control; (b) **compilable into the guest engine**\n   (resolve-var, primitive apply, closure/continuation minting, store ops, GC —\n   the engine crates compiled *into* the instance, argon's Option 1); and\n   (c) irreducibly world-fact (stream/effect/park/FFI) → `dao.stream`. The plan\n   currently treats (b) as a vague \"service migration\" footnote; the ruling makes\n   (b) *the* W6 program.\n\n3. **Add a W0 crossing-cost measurement fixture.** The ruling's governing number\n   is measured per-instruction crossing cost, and it must be *engine-independent*\n   (the economics only bind if the cost is identical across Node/Wasmtime/Wasmer).\n   W0 should include a micro-benchmark counting, per opcode and per required\n   service, serialized bytes + crossing count + codec time for a representative\n   instruction mix. This feeds the W6 \"dissolve or shrink\" decision from the\n   first slices instead of as a late afterthought.\n\n4. **Add a W0 required-operations *minimality audit*.** The ruling says every\n   addition to a process-local ABI is a cost to be justified. W0 should therefore\n   state, per required operation, *why it is not yet compilable into the guest*\n   — i.e. is it a world-fact (bucket c), or a not-yet-migrated service (bucket b)?\n   This makes the cost explicit from day one rather than discovering it at W6.\n\n5. **Correct the non-goal language.** §6.2 / §E.5.4 currently list \"native-to-\n   wasm migration of value/scheduler services\" and the interpreter-loop variant\n   among non-goals/later axes. Under the ruling, that migration is *the goal*. I'd\n   reframe the non-goal to \"no *silent second semantic implementation* during\n   migration\" (single-engine-port still binds) rather than \"no migration.\"\n\nBottom line: the ruling does **not** commit the project to building the node lane\nfirst. It commits the project to *treating the mailbox ABI as a cost curve with a\nknown, measured, and shrinking area, whose zero-point is the node lane* — and the\nmailbox ABI is the instrument you need to draw that curve. The single most\nimportant concrete change is item 2: make service-migration a *mandated* W6\ndeliverable with an explicit per-operation partition, and add the item-3 W0\nmeasurement that makes it a decision rather than a contingency.\n# One-ABI ruling, round 2 — Fable position\n\nRead: `datom.world.md` Host Boundaries → \"Owner ruling, 2026-10-19\"; the\nruling notes in `yin.vm.engine.md` §E.3 and `yang.wasm.md` §4; `yang.wasm.md`\n§1.1 (Rust kernel is design-only, not authorized) and §6.1 (W0–W6 gates). No\nother architect's round-2 answer was read.\n\n## 1. What the ruling changes in my position\n\n**Survives.** The mechanism: a process-local ABI traffics in references into a\nheap the composition owns, has one legitimate reader, is not history, and is\ncollectable. Those four properties are what *any* second ABI is, whichever\nreason you give for having one. They remain the spec of the shim, and they\nremain the reason the shim cannot be dao.stream *while it exists*. The node\nlane (engine-in-guest, dao.stream the only interface) as the place where the\nowner's instinct is correct also survives; the ruling adopts it as the\nstrategic endpoint.\n\n**Revised.** In round 1 I called shared custody \"the one indispensable reason\"\nfor a second ABI. That was a category slip and the ruling catches it. Shared\ncustody is not a reason to draw the line where it is; it is a *consequence*\nof drawing it there. The line between machine and world is chosen, and the\ncustody properties follow from the choice. So I withdraw \"indispensable\nreason\" and keep \"indispensable property\". I also withdraw the word\n\"load-bearing\": the shim is load-bearing for the body lane, and the body lane\nis itself provisional.\n\n**Not conceded.** \"Performance\" is the wrong word for what justifies the shim\ntoday (see §2), and the round-1 recommendation to shrink the shim by moving\npure work into emitted code was wrong under this ruling (see §4). I revise\nboth.\n\n## 2. Critique of the ruling\n\n**\"Performance is the sole valid justification\" is right in shape and wrong in\nword.** The shape is right: every candidate justification I can name for a\nsecond ABI evaporates in the node lane, because the node lane moves the whole\nmachine to one side of the line, and a reason that disappears when the line\nmoves is not a reason for a second ABI but a reason for where the line is.\nThat is the ruling's thesis and I now agree with it. But the thing that\njustifies the body-lane shim *today* is not per-instruction crossing cost. It\nis **availability**: the only engine the project has is CLJC, which cannot be\ncompiled into a wasm instance, and the Rust kernel that could be is\ndesign-only and unauthorized (`yang.wasm.md` §1.1). The body lane exists\nbecause lowering *images* is buildable now and lowering the *engine* is not.\nCrossing cost justifies the shim's *size*; it does not justify its\n*existence*. The ruling should say \"engineering cost\", and the cost ledger\nshould list \"engine not yet compilable into the guest\" as the first entry,\nbecause that is the entry whose removal dissolves the ABI.\n\nCandidate reasons the question names, and whether each is a genuine\nsecond-ABI reason:\n\n- **Capability/security granularity.** Not genuine. The body lane gets cheap\n  per-crossing checks because trust was established once at A admission and\n  the guest bytes are our compiler's output. That is amortization, which is\n  cost. ShiBi tokens on the stream give the same granularity in the node lane.\n- **Determinism and cross-embedder parity.** Not a second-ABI reason; it is a\n  constraint on either ABI. It actually argues *for* dissolution: one engine\n  binary running under Node, Wasmtime and Wasmer gives parity for free, while\n  the body lane has to prove parity between a CLJS service and a Rust service\n  of the same transitions (§E.4.2 \"one transition, two dispatchers\").\n- **Freeze-vs-extend of the ISA.** Half genuine. The register contract is\n  frozen at 23 opcodes; services are where semantics evolve without bumping\n  the ISA. But the extension point does not have to be a second ABI: the\n  effect vocabulary on the stream is also extensible. What the shim buys is\n  *freedom to churn* a vocabulary that is not history and need not be\n  canonical forever. That is canonicalization and versioning cost, so\n  reducible. I flag it as the one reduction with teeth: a wrongly frozen\n  stream vocabulary is permanent, so moving a service onto the stream early\n  carries a one-way risk the ruling's \"every removal is progress\" does not\n  price.\n- **Failure isolation.** Not genuine, and inverted. A wasm trap in the body\n  lane kills a task while the host keeps the heap; in the node lane a trap\n  loses the heap but stream history survives and the node replays. Node lane\n  isolates better.\n- **Independent versioning.** Reducible to the same cost as freeze-vs-extend.\n  The shim versions with the engine, the stream with the system; that is\n  convenient, not necessary.\n- **In-machine sharing and identity** (cells, `identical?`, shared\n  substructure). Not genuine either, for the same reason as all the others:\n  the node lane keeps the heap inside the guest and loses nothing.\n\nTwo things the ruling misses that are not reasons for a second ABI but are\ncorrections to how it will be applied:\n\n- **\"Every removal is progress\" needs a direction.** Removing a crossing by\n  re-implementing the service's semantics in the WAT emitter is not progress\n  toward one ABI; it is a third engine (the risk §6.1 already names). Progress\n  should be measured by *engine code reuse inside the guest*, not by crossing\n  count. A shim can shrink the wrong way.\n- **The endpoint is unreachable as planned.** The node lane needs an engine\n  that compiles to wasm32. Nothing in the authorized plan commissions one.\n  The ruling therefore implies a decision it does not state: either authorize\n  the Rust kernel crates for wasm32, or accept that the body-lane shim is\n  long-lived and the one-ABI state is aspirational. Review should surface\n  that, not let \"provisional by principle\" paper over it.\n\n## 3. Is the floor sound\n\nSound, with a sharper definition than the ruling gives. The machine is a\ndeterministic function of (shipped code, stream prefix). The floor is the set\nof operations whose outputs are *not* such a function. \"World-facts cannot be\ncomputed by shipped code\" is that statement; it is correct and it is the\nright floor. The stream is the floor because a stream prefix is the only form\nin which a world-fact can be an input to a deterministic function.\n\nCan the floor move? Downward, yes, and that is desirable; upward, no.\n\n- **Recorded worlds / replay** do not move the floor. The recording *is* the\n  stream; a replayer is just another host that answers from history. The\n  machine still reads facts off a stream. What replay changes is which host\n  answers, not where the line is.\n- **Replicated agreement** does not move it either. Consensus turns many\n  hosts' facts into one agreed fact that still arrives by append. It changes\n  the fact's authority, not its mechanism.\n- **Changing what counts as a fact** does move it, downward. Time, randomness,\n  scheduling order, allocation order: each can be a world-fact (floor) or a\n  function of history (machine) depending on design. Logical clocks, seeded\n  PRNGs fed by one stream fact, and deterministic scheduler rounds pull them\n  inside the machine. `:module/require` is already an effect, so module\n  resolution is correctly above the floor. The project should keep moving\n  things *into* the machine this way because every such move enlarges what\n  replay can reproduce.\n- **Upward movement would mean** shipped code computing a fact about the\n  world without reading it, which is a contradiction in terms. The one honest\n  near-exception is *assumptions*: code can embed a belief about the world\n  (a constant, a cached module) and act on it. That is not computing a fact;\n  it is risking a stale one, and the stream is still where it finds out it was\n  wrong.\n\nOne nit on the ruling's wording: \"a guest whose engine is compiled inside it\nneeds only `dao.stream`\" still imports memory and a function table and a\nstream-transport function. Those are not an ABI in the ruling's sense (they\ncarry no semantics), and the transport is the sanctioned function-shaped\nhandle operation. Say so explicitly, or someone will count the imports and\nclaim the node lane has two ABIs too.\n\n## 4. Consequences for the plan\n\nThe ruling commits the project to the node lane as destination. Body-lane\nfirst is still the right *order*, for the availability reason in §2, but\nseveral things in W0–W6 should change so that the body lane is built as a\nbridge to the node lane rather than as a permanent structure.\n\n1. **Decide the Rust-kernel question now, not at W6.** W6 currently says\n   \"if crossing cost dominates, prototype the self-contained interpreter-loop\n   variant\". Under the ruling that is backwards: the node lane is the endpoint\n   regardless of the measurement, and the measurement decides only *when*.\n   The owner should rule whether K-phase crates are authorized for a wasm32\n   target. If yes, items 2–4 follow. If no, the ruling should be amended to say\n   the shim is long-lived until that changes.\n2. **W2–W4: the Rust service crates must compile to wasm32 from the first\n   commit.** `no_std`-compatible, no WASI in the core, no host-pointer types\n   in the transition API. §1.1 already says the native embedder reuses \"the\n   same crates\"; make wasm32 compilability a gate, not a hope. This is the\n   single concrete step that turns the node lane from a W6 experiment into a\n   build target, and it costs almost nothing if done at the start and a\n   rewrite if done late.\n3. **Add a node-lane gate (call it N0) beside W5, not inside W6.** Engine\n   crate compiled to wasm, one stream-transport import, run the register\n   corpus under Node, Wasmtime and Wasmer. Pass/fail is parity with the CLJC\n   reference, not speed. This is where the ABI dissolves; it deserves its own\n   gate.\n4. **Compiled bodies survive into the node lane as an optimization.** An\n   engine-in-guest instance can still link per-image compiled body modules\n   into its own function table (§5's attach mechanism with the engine on the\n   inside). The body's \"service call\" then becomes an intra-instance call to\n   the engine's exported transitions over shared linear memory and the\n   engine's own Value layout, with no host crossing and no TLV. Design W5's\n   body modules so their service thunks are *replaceable* with direct calls:\n   keep the thunk as the one site that knows how a service is reached. That\n   is the cheapest way to make the emitter's output outlive the shim.\n5. **Mark §E.3 as bridge-era and stop investing in it beyond CLJS needs.**\n   Keep golden fixtures (they are cheap and protect CLJS/Rust parity), but do\n   not extend the envelope, do not add operations without a cost entry, and\n   do not let any other spec depend on it (the \"one home\" rule already\n   helps). The expanded-canonical-bytes ordering rule and tag-13 descriptors\n   are the parts most likely to grow; freeze them at W0's set.\n6. **Measure crossings from W1, not W6.** A counter of exchanges per guest\n   instruction and bytes per exchange costs nothing and makes \"shrink as\n   measurements allow\" an actual loop instead of a promise.\n7. **Withdraw my round-1 \"shrink the shim\" items** that moved engine\n   semantics into emitted code (constant minting, store-get of immutable\n   module stores, cached free-name resolution). Under the ruling those are\n   anti-progress: fewer crossings bought with a third implementation of\n   engine semantics. The only shrinking that counts is moving the engine into\n   the guest. The emitter should keep exactly what it must know to compile\n   control flow, moves and closure calls, and nothing it could get wrong\n   about the engine.\n\n**One-line answer.** The ruling is right that the boundary is drawn and that\nonly cost justifies a second ABI; it is wrong to name the cost \"performance\",\nbecause what justifies the shim today is that no engine exists that can live\ninside the guest, and the plan should be changed to build one, with the body\nlane explicitly engineered as scaffolding the node lane will reuse rather\nthan replace.\ncollab/1792353000000-architect-one-abi-ruling.gemini-4-argon.answer.md\ncollab/1792353000000-architect-one-abi-ruling.gemini-4-argon.stdout.log\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
{"type":"item.started","item":{"id":"item_3","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/zsh -lc \"sed -n '320,373p' docs/design/dao.stream.md; sed -n '501,639p' docs/design/dao.stream.md; sed -n '1,120p' docs/design/datom.world.md; sed -n '1250,1302p' docs/design/yin.vm.engine.md\"","aggregated_output":"","exit_code":null,"status":"in_progress"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_3","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/zsh -lc \"sed -n '320,373p' docs/design/dao.stream.md; sed -n '501,639p' docs/design/dao.stream.md; sed -n '1,120p' docs/design/datom.world.md; sed -n '1250,1302p' docs/design/yin.vm.engine.md\"","aggregated_output":"logical stream,\nwhere `attach!` on the reconstructed value attaches to the stream it names —\nprovided the receiving host can reach what it names at all (see Creation and\nAttachment on reachability), and allowing that a transport which cannot know\nthat at call time reports it later as data.\n\nBoth envelopes are plain data through and through: every value must survive\nthe host serialization codec structurally unchanged, so equality is\nstructural, and no key holds a function, a host object, a live handle, or\nidentity smuggled in metadata. Like every DaoStream map, envelopes are open —\na consumer ignores qualified keys it does not understand.\n\n`create!` outcomes:\n\n| Outcome                       | Meaning                                       | Required keys        |\n|-------------------------------|-----------------------------------------------|----------------------|\n| `:dao.stream/ok`              | A new logical stream exists, its identity settled at once. Implies not that every resource backing it has finished being acquired. | `:dao.stream/handle` |\n| `:dao.stream/invalid-spec`    | The creation specification is malformed.      | —                    |\n| `:dao.stream/not-found`       | No transport here matches `:dao.stream/type`. | —                    |\n| `:dao.stream/transport-error` | The handler failed.                           | —                    |\n\n`attach!` outcomes:\n\n| Outcome                          | Meaning                                                                             | Required keys        |\n|----------------------------------|-------------------------------------------------------------------------------------|----------------------|\n| `:dao.stream/ok`                 | A handle exists through which this attachment is operated. Implies neither that a far end has confirmed the stream it names nor that establishment has completed. Where either answer is one only the far end can give, it arrives later as data, on the channel the transport names (see Surfaces). | `:dao.stream/handle`, and `:dao.stream/attachment` where the transport distinguishes attachments |\n| `:dao.stream/invalid-descriptor` | The descriptor is malformed.                                                        | —                    |\n| `:dao.stream/not-found`          | The descriptor names no stream here, or no transport matches its type.              | —                    |\n| `:dao.stream/transport-error`    | The handler failed.                                                                 | —                    |\n\nWhere a transport acquires its medium through a process rather than at once —\nbinding a port, opening a handle through an API that only answers later — `ok`\nstill means the logical stream exists: its identity is minted, its sequence is\nempty, and the handle is operable. What it does not mean is that the stream can\nyet accept values. The handle reports that through its own operations, exactly\nas any other transient condition is reported, and the result of the acquisition\narrives as data.\n\nThis is permitted only where logical existence is genuinely independent of the\nresource. A transport that cannot mint identity and lifecycle before its\nacquisition completes is not creating a stream and deferring its medium; it is\ndeferring the creation itself, and `ok` would be false. Such a transport does\nnot implement `create!` — what it needs is a request whose result arrives\nlater, which is an ordinary interpreter over streams, not an operation here.\n\nCreation defers differently from attachment, and the difference is why this is\na caveat rather than a new outcome. `attach!` defers a **remote confirmation**:\nan answer only a far end can give, which is why it arrives as deposited data.\n`create!` has no far end. What it defers is **local acquisition**, whose\nfailure surfaces through the handle the caller already holds. A \"not yet\"\noutcome would have to return no handle — leaving the caller an identity it\ncannot operate, and nothing to retry against.\n\n### Host Dispatch\n## Cursors\n\nA cursor is an immutable value owned by the interpreter that holds it.\n\n- Cursors are minted by the stream: `cursor` on a handle takes an **anchor**\n  and returns `:dao.stream/ok` with `:dao.stream/cursor`. The contract\n  defines two anchors — `:dao.stream/oldest`, the earliest retained\n  position, and `:dao.stream/newest`, positioned after the newest appended\n  value so the first `next` observes the next value to arrive. A transport\n  whose medium is naturally addressable may accept additional\n  transport-owned anchors (a byte offset, a timestamp). An anchor that is\n  malformed or not honored by this transport yields\n  `:dao.stream/invalid-anchor`. On a handle whose attachment has closed,\n  `cursor` yields `:dao.stream/closed`; a handle on the logical stream\n  itself keeps minting cursors after close, so retained history remains\n  readable. Minting may have to consult the medium — locating the earliest\n  retained position on a durable log is itself a read — so where a\n  transport's reads can fail at runtime, minting can too, and `cursor`\n  yields `:dao.stream/transport-error` for it; a transport whose medium\n  cannot fail this way excludes the outcome like any other (see Surfaces).\n  `cursor`'s outcome set is therefore `:dao.stream/ok` (with\n  `:dao.stream/cursor`), `:dao.stream/invalid-anchor`,\n  `:dao.stream/closed`, `:dao.stream/refused`, and\n  `:dao.stream/transport-error`.\n- A composition that intends to observe events caused by an operation mints\n  its `:dao.stream/newest` cursor **before** invoking that operation. Minting\n  after `attach!`, endpoint bind, handoff installation, or any other operation\n  that can deposit would intentionally skip events deposited in between.\n- **A freshly minted `:dao.stream/oldest` is a position, not a claim about\n  completeness.** The anchor names the earliest *retained* position, which on\n  an evicting transport advances as values are lost. A `gap` is reported to a\n  cursor that spans an eviction; a cursor minted after one is a valid cursor\n  onto the surviving suffix and reports nothing, because nothing is wrong with\n  the cursor. Retained history is not complete history, and no anchor can make\n  it so. A consumer that requires complete history gets it from the transport's\n  declared retention (see *Retention and Gaps*), never from an anchor.\n- A composition on a transport that **can evict**, intending to detect whether\n  history was lost, mints an **origin cursor** — `:dao.stream/oldest` before\n  the first append — and keeps it, exactly as a composition that intends to\n  observe events caused by an operation mints `:dao.stream/newest` before\n  invoking it. A kept origin cursor converts a silent loss into a reported\n  `gap`. It cannot be minted afterwards and cannot be reconstructed: a consumer\n  that arrives later holds no evidence about what preceded it. On a transport\n  that declares complete retention this is unnecessary — a fresh `:oldest` is\n  the origin there, however late the consumer arrives.\n- Every valid cursor comes from the stream: minted by `cursor`, received as\n  a successor from `next`, or recovered from a gap outcome. Consumers never\n  construct cursor internals or fabricate positions — there is no `seek`. A\n  cursor's representation belongs to the transport.\n- A cursor binds to the logical stream, not to a handle: any handle on the\n  same logical stream accepts it, so a cursor handed to another interpreter\n  works with that interpreter's own handle. A cursor is plain data and\n  survives the host serialization codec structurally unchanged, under the\n  same rule as the envelopes. A cursor that outlives the stream it names is\n  a cursor like any other: `next` answers `cursor-mismatch` when the handle\n  it is given is on a different logical stream, never a defect. A descriptor\n  whose stream no longer exists is `attach!`'s own `not-found`, a separate\n  case that takes no cursor.\n- A cursor carries the logical-stream identity, not a reachability descriptor.\n  `cursor-mismatch` compares that identity, so handles reached through distinct\n  descriptors for the same logical stream accept the same cursor.\n- Advancing is receiving a successor cursor from `next`. The stream never\n  advances a reader; the stream holds no reader positions at all. Dropping a\n  cursor is how a reader detaches — there is nothing to unregister.\n- Because cursors are immutable values, a reader may retain an old cursor and\n  re-read retained history, or hand a cursor to another interpreter. What a\n  cursor can still observe is governed only by retention.\n\n## Reading\n\n`next` takes a handle and a cursor and returns an outcome immediately.\nIt is total and non-blocking: every state of the stream has a defined data\nanswer.\n\n| Outcome                       | Meaning                                                                       | Required keys                                                                           |\n|-------------------------------|-------------------------------------------------------------------------------|-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------|\n| `:dao.stream/ok`              | A value was observed.                                                         | `:dao.stream/value`, `:dao.stream/cursor` (successor)                                   |\n| `:dao.stream/blocked`         | Nothing at this position yet; retry later.                                    | —                                                                                       |\n| `:dao.stream/end`             | What this handle is on is closed and this position is past the retained tail. | —                                                                                       |\n| `:dao.stream/gap`             | This position was evicted.                                                    | `:dao.stream/cursor` (earliest retained position, or the tail when nothing is retained) |\n| `:dao.stream/cursor-mismatch` | The cursor was minted by a different logical stream.                          | —                                                                                       |\n| `:dao.stream/invalid-cursor`  | The value is not a cursor.                                                    | —                                                                                       |\n| `:dao.stream/refused`         | A policy composed on this handle declined the read; nothing was observed.     | —                                                                                       |\n| `:dao.stream/transport-error` | The transport failed to perform the read; nothing was observed.               | —                                                                                       |\n\nReading never mutates the stream. Two readers with cursors at the same\nposition observe the same retained value; neither can starve or affect the\nother. There is no destructive read anywhere in the contract — no drain, no\ntake. An operation that removes an element as it reads would make one\nreader's progress every other reader's data loss.\n\n## Writing\n\n`append!` takes a handle and a value and returns an outcome:\n\n| Outcome                       | Meaning                                                                                                                                              | Required keys |\n|-------------------------------|------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------|---------------|\n| `:dao.stream/ok`              | The value is accepted at the next position in the sequence this handle's writer surface is on. Implies neither local readability nor remote delivery. | —             |\n| `:dao.stream/full`            | The transport does not accept the value; nothing was appended. Whether this is transient or permanent is the transport's declared nature (see Retention and Gaps). | —             |\n| `:dao.stream/invalid-value`   | The transport cannot carry this value (for example, it cannot be encoded); nothing was appended.                                                     | —             |\n| `:dao.stream/closed`          | What this handle is on is closed to new appends; the value was not appended.                                                                         | —             |\n| `:dao.stream/refused`         | A policy composed on this handle declined the append; nothing was appended.                                                                          | —             |\n| `:dao.stream/transport-error` | The transport failed to perform the append. Whether the value was appended is unknown unless the transport declares that its failures are clean.     | —             |\n\nAcceptance is handle-relative, as close outcomes are. **Which sequence** a\nhandle's writer surface is on is part of the transport's declared nature: for a\nring buffer it is the logical stream itself, so `ok` places the value in it; for\na transport that carries values toward a stream elsewhere it is the ordered\noutbound path, and what becomes of the value at the far end is reported there,\nnot here. No remote transport can say more — under *no operation waits*, an\nanswer from the far end cannot arrive before `append!` returns.\n\nThe writer decides what to do about any non-`ok` outcome; DaoStream does not\nretry, buffer, or notify on its behalf. The three in-memory transports\n(`ringbuffer`, `memory-log`, `ws`) declare clean failure: their\n`transport-error` means nothing was appended.\n\n**Deduplication and correlation are the payload's.** A writer that must\nretry safely puts an identity in the value; a reader that must not act twice\ndeduplicates on it. The stream never promises exactly-once, and a\n`transport-error` whose effect is unknown is never retried automatically.\n\nA transport whose acceptance is not its final answer (durability reached,\nfar-end refusal) declares the channel on which that answer is deposited, as\n*Close* already requires for a failing flush.\n\n## Close\n\n`close!` takes a handle and returns a map whose outcome set is `{ok}` — no\nother outcome exists for it. It is idempotent: closing an already-closed scope\nis also `:dao.stream/ok`.\n\n`ok` is the local lifecycle transition, not a report on releasing host\nresources: what the handle is on is closed from that moment, irrevocably.\nA transport whose close must also flush or release something that can fail\ndeclares where that failure surfaces — an ordinary deposit on a channel it\nnames — because the outcome set has no room for it and the transition has\nalready happened.\n\n---\ndescription: Core philosophy, foundational axioms, invariants, primitives (tuples, streams, host boundaries), architectural commitments, design principles, code style, and development workflow for datom.world\n---\n\n# DATOM.WORLD ARCHITECTURAL FOUNDATIONS & DEVELOPMENT RULES\n\n## Core Philosophy\n\nEverything is data.\nInterpretation > abstraction.\nExplicit causality > implicit assumptions.\nRestrictions are a feature.\n\n### Foundational Axioms\n\n1. **Everything is a Stream**: All IO and data flow through append-only streams.\n2. **Interpretation Creates Semantics**: Data is syntax; semantics only emerge through interpretation (one truth, many perspectives).\n   At system scale, a \"system\" is an emergent property of interpreters of\n   pure data, not an object, service, or stored artifact. Find the\n   interpreters and the data they share to find the system.\n3. **Code and State are Datoms**: Code (Universal AST) and runtime execution state are structured datoms, enabling syntax independence and structural reflection.\n4. **Everything is a Continuation**: Computation is modeled as serializable, portable continuations that can pause, travel across streams, and resume anywhere.\n\n## Non-Negotiable Invariants\n\n- Do not introduce hidden global state.\n- Do not introduce implicit control flow.\n- Do not introduce callbacks; every callback is an event on a stream.\n- Do not introduce shared mutable state.\n- Do not collapse interpretation and execution into the same layer.\n- Do not assume graphs: graphs must be constructed explicitly from tuples.\n- Do not lose information (unitarity): streams are append-only and no datom is destroyed, only reinterpreted. The Universal AST is never lost: it is always retrievable wherever code is lowered, by address or by a query over its datoms. Every derived form (semantic vector, native images, indexes) is an interpretation of it and may drop facts only because the AST remains.\n\n## Tuples and Datoms\n\nTuples are elements in an open moduli space, graded by dimension $n$. A tuple can be any dimension/size.\nA datom is the canonical persistent tuple `[e a v t m]` (entity, attribute, value, transaction, metadata). Persistent facts are immutable datoms.\nSource scope belongs to the interpreter, never to an appended tuple slot. Queries keep physical sources as separate db-values and express union or joins explicitly.\n\nSee [`docs/design/datom.md`](./datom.md) for full specification.\n\n## Streams\n\nAll IO is modeled as a stream. Functions consume streams and produce streams.\nNo direct function-to-function coupling without a stream boundary.\nSide effects must appear as stream emissions.\nStreams are values that can be sent through streams.\nStreams carry whatever values the consumer needs: no datom requirement, no privileged payload shape.\n\nA stream has no privileged reader, and its meaning is not in it. Any number\nof interpreters may observe the same medium, and each constructs its own\nsemantics from the same batches: a `yin.vm` evaluator observing a program\nstream constructs CESK state and executes it; `dao.space.index` observing\nthe very same stream materializes covered indexes, so `dao.space.query/q`\nanswers Datalog over the program as it grows. Neither knows the other\nexists. The medium is their only coupling, and each observer's ignorance of\nthe other is load-bearing: the evaluator never consults an index to run,\nand the index never evaluates a form to index it. This is how axiom 2\nbecomes architecture: one stream, many interpretations, none of them\nthe stream's own.\n\nFunctions can take anything and return anything.\nAgents are functions, closures, or continuations in `yin.vm` that communicate with the outside world through `dao.stream`. Agent behavior is specified entirely through stream effects.\n\nSee [`docs/design/dao.stream.md`](./dao.stream.md) for full stream specification.\nSee [`docs/design/yin.vm.streams-all-the-way-down.md`](./yin.vm.streams-all-the-way-down.md) for VM architecture.\n\n### Host Boundaries\n\nA host boundary is a stream boundary, never a function call. The one\nsanctioned function-shaped contact is a `dao.stream` handle operation, whose\nexhaustive outcome map is stream protocol, not an application callback.\n\n**The system ABI.** `dao.stream` is the system's binary interface — the only\ncontract that crosses a trust domain. Nodes with no shared code (different\nruntimes, languages, or machines) interoperate purely through canonical stream\nvalues (`dao.jing` encodings, content-hash identities) and the\nappend/park/resume discipline. Interfaces below this line are shims, not peers:\na guest that cannot express a stream operation (a WebAssembly module's only\noutgoing capability is an imported function over linear memory) is served by a\nhost-local ABI — for the wasm lane, `yin.vm.engine.host.v1`\n([`yin.vm.engine.md`](./yin.vm.engine.md) extension §E) — that terminates at the\nembedding composition, which alone translates service requests into stream\noperations. Two consequences are doctrine. First, what crosses a trust domain\nis a strict subset of what exists inside a process: closures and continuations\ncross only as portable plain data; host types, resources, and code artifacts\nnever do — the custody gates mark exactly this seam\n([`yin.vm.linker.md`](./yin.vm.linker.md)). Second, every new guest substrate\nspecifies its boundary as such a shim to the composition, never as a second\nsystem ABI, and a process-local ABI's definitions have exactly one home in the\nspecification tree. Naming doctrine: \"the system ABI\" is reserved for\n`dao.stream`; a guest-host interface such as `yin.vm.engine.host.v1` is always\nqualified (the guest-host service ABI, the wasm service ABI), never bare \"the\nABI\" in a definitional position.\n\n**Owner ruling, 2026-10-19: the boundary is drawn, not found; one ABI is the\nprinciple.** Where the line between machine and world sits above the\n`dao.stream` floor is a design choice, and it has been redrawn before\n(contract v4 promoted `:define` from host service to opcode). The sole valid\njustification for a second, process-local ABI is **performance**: the measured\nengineering cost of per-instruction boundary crossings. It is therefore an\nengineering-cost measure, not doctrine — provisional by principle, to be\nshrunk as measurements allow and dissolved where a lane can sit at the floor\n(a guest whose engine is compiled inside it needs only `dao.stream`). The\nfloor itself is not movable: operations whose answers are facts about the\nworld cannot be computed by shipped code. Architecture review should treat\nevery addition to a process-local ABI as a cost to be justified, and every\nremoval as progress toward the one-ABI state.\n\n**Effects out.** Portable code appends an effect value describing what must\nhappen. A host interpreter consumes that stream, performs the operation, and\nappends the outcome. Portable code reads the outcome. Correlation is by\nidentity in the data; neither side holds a reference to the other. A host with\nno implementation for an effect emits a qualified unsupported result. That is a\ncorrect outcome, not a gap to be filled.\n\n**Events in.** When the host initiates (a socket receives, a connection drops,\na timer fires) the adapter appends one plain-data event and returns. It invokes\nnothing. An interpreter reads that stream and decides what the event means.\nConstructively: **a callback is replaced by a function that transforms an event\nkernel/value/image work; W2 shares K2 payload/restore work plus the required\n`yin-engine` park subset; W3–W4 require the Level-2 library services\ndescribed in Rust §6.3, not a second engine. W5 is wasm release\nqualification, not certification of the full K5 standalone node. W6 can\ncontribute wasm measurements to K3's same corpus/harness; K4 still requires\nthe owner's review of K3\n(`docs/design/yin.vm.rust-kernel.md:1167–1182, 1215–1228`). No dependency\nauthorizes unrelated implementation. Register-specific W0–W6 implementation\nslices, per-slice differential acceptance gates, and retained Rust K1–K5 /\nLLVM gates live in\n[`yin.vm.debruijn.register.md`](./yin.vm.debruijn.register.md) `§10.5`.\n\n#### E.5.4 Guest-agnostic non-goals and risks\n\nNon-goals: no second Rust kernel/engine/value/codec port; no K4 LLVM\nimplementation, full K5 standalone node, new Rust network transport,\ncross-node artifact service or Rust `Rc`-layout exposure; no universal\ncross-model continuation conversion, new portable continuation schema, direct\narbitrary host FFI imports, WASI imports in the VM core, threads/shared\nmemory, WasmGC dependency, numeric NaN boxing, a mandatory standalone\nhost-free runtime for W5, or immediate JVM/Dart application bindings; no\npromise to execute malicious wasm from the network; no optimization before W5\ncorrectness.\n\nGuest-agnostic host risks (register-specific risks live in\n[`yin.vm.debruijn.register.md`](./yin.vm.debruijn.register.md) §10.6):\n- **Host boundary overhead.** A `ValueRef` implementation deliberately leaves semantic services in conforming embedders, with mandatory serialized messages even for CLJS. Measure in W6 whether compiled dispatch helps before moving collection/value/store services into wasm; the native host implementation needed for portability is already required by W1–W4.\n- **Persistent public state over mutable memory.** Snapshot aliasing and old-state retention are correctness concerns, not just GC details. Decide in W0 whether each public state owns a copy-on-write arena root or a copied active shadow; require old-state replay tests before optimizing.\n- **Host exceptions and partial effects.** Catch host failures at the adapter boundary for shaped diagnostics, then reproduce the existing public exception behavior. Do not roll back an already appended stream event or retry a completed effect. The mailbox needs a single completion/commit rule.\n- **Predicate drift.** `machine-data?`, value wrappers, integer carriers and effect authorization have evolved beyond old doc prose. Reuse the source helpers, and make ABI tests fail when their contracts change. External plain-data serialization and internal trusted-wrapper values are separate gates.\n- **Single-port dependency.** Native coverage depends on the shared Rust crates, including the substantial Level-2 engine subset needed before W3/W4. A faster parallel port is not a workaround. Keep one transition implementation per host language, one exported K0/W0 kit and explicit shared-reference versioning; duplicate dispatchers must not duplicate semantic code. The `Value` facade and snapshot barriers must preserve check-then-commit and `Rc` ownership while keeping handles task-local.\n- **Host coverage.** Node/CLJS and native Wasmtime/Wasmer are shipping targets under one pinned baseline ABI/profile. Browser and later JVM/Dart application integrations may have different resource limits and must rerun the same parity suite. Native semantic-service drift and ABI codec fidelity are the principal portability risks.\n\n#### E.5.5 Reference conventions and open items\n\nReference convention: `register.cljc` means\n`src/cljc/yin/vm/debruijn/register.cljc`; `engine`, `ffi`, `module`,\n`values`, and `debruijn_register_effects` filenames are under\n`src/cljc/yin/vm/`; `vm` means `src/cljc/yin/vm.cljc`; `apply2` is the alias\nfor `dao.stream.apply` at `src/cljc/dao/stream/apply.cljc`. `module/IModuleKernel`\nand its `gc-roots` method are declared in `module.cljc`. `IVM` `step`/`run`\nare methods of `yin.vm/IVM`. `halted-with-empty-queue?`, `scheduler-round`,\n`check-wait-set`, `handle-close` and `collect` are `engine.cljc` functions.\nThe quoted restore/call helper names are existing source semantics, whereas\n`exchange`/`fetch-response`, ABI statuses and all native implementation entry\nnames are proposed protocol vocabulary. Rust-design line references retain\nr5's coordinates (`1–1334`).\n\n**Open items** (retained from r5 without resolution across this specification,\n[`yang.wasm.md`](./yang.wasm.md), and\n[`yin.vm.debruijn.register.md`](./yin.vm.debruijn.register.md) `§10.6`):\n- §E.3.4 (r5 §4.1.1) says guest closure calls stay compiled, while the single-port rule in §E.4.2 (r5 §4.1.1) requires service sites to reuse the owning host's call transition. The division between generated dispatch and shared transition work needs to be made concrete without creating a second semantic implementation (see also [`yang.wasm.md`](./yang.wasm.md) `## Open items`).\n- W0 retains the transport-test notation `COMPLETE→NEED_SPACE→fetch-response` (§E.5.3). §E.3.2 (r5 §4.1.1) defines `COMPLETE` and `NEED_SPACE` as alternative `exchange` results; the notation's test sequencing needs clarification, not an ABI change.\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
