tion: Import ABI `yin.vm.engine.host.v1`\ndocs/design/yang.wasm.md:408:`yin.vm.engine.host.v1` — renamed from `yin.register.host.v1` in r5 §4.1.1 — and its canonical\ndocs/design/yang.wasm.md:413:**`yin.vm.engine.host.v1`** defined in [`yin.vm.engine.md`](./yin.vm.engine.md) §E.3 (r5 §4.1.1).\ndocs/design/yang.wasm.md:417:(`datom.world.md`, Streams → Host Boundaries → The system ABI). Owner ruling,\ndocs/design/yang.wasm.md:423:1. **Module import bindings ([`yin.vm.engine.md`](./yin.vm.engine.md) §E.3):** The shared runtime module imports `memory` (one wasm32, non-shared memory), `functions` (one original function table), and **`exchange(control_ptr: i32) -> i32`** from module name `yin.vm.engine.host.v1`. Generated component modules import the same `memory`/`functions` and the service entry as needed, plus declared immutable `i32` base-offset words. No import or export passes a JS value, `externref`, Rust pointer, string object, multi-value result, or `i64` through a host-call signature.\ndocs/design/yang.wasm.md:425:3. **Transport statuses, execution statuses, and `fetch-response` ([`yin.vm.engine.md`](./yin.vm.engine.md) §E.3.2):** Emitted service thunks handle the transport-status set and the response-retry rule exactly as §E.3.2 defines: on `NEED_SPACE` the guest thunk resizes the response buffer to the required byte length and issues a `fetch-response` for the same sequence number without re-executing or re-sending the semantic transition. Decoded responses carry the private scheduling status set of §E.3.2.\ndocs/design/yang.wasm.md:429:The host-side implementation of `yin.vm.engine.host.v1`, the **single-engine-port rule**, and the\ndocs/design/yang.wasm.md:435:## 5. Per-image-row compilation, relocation, and artifact-cache law\ndocs/design/yang.wasm.md:438:the same `yin.vm.engine.host.v1` ABI memory and services. Instantiate it for the receiving task\ndocs/design/yang.wasm.md:496:## 6. Compiler toolchain, optimization axis (W6), non-goals, and risks\ndocs/design/yang.wasm.md:498:### 6.1 Toolchain and W0–W6 lowering gates\ndocs/design/yang.wasm.md:507:- **W1–W5**: Compile each admitted image once and execute those identical wasm bytes under Node, Wasmtime (from W1), and Wasmer (complete W1–W4 suite in W4, release gate in W5), verifying byte-identical artifact emission and `yin.vm.engine.host.v1` import allowlist compliance.\ndocs/design/yang.wasm.md:508:- **W6 (measured optimization, after correctness)**: Keep Node + Wasmtime + Wasmer full-trace parity mandatory for every optimization. Measure compilation latency, binary size, native stack depth, arena/heap retention, dispatch cost, serialized message bytes/copy/codec time, call and suspension cost, and end-to-end time on each embedder; compare Node against interpreted register and report native results separately without attributing service-language speed to wasm codegen. Only then coalesce blocks, reduce host crossings, add dirty spilling, or use optional Binaryen passes. Re-run all parity and binary validation gates for each transformation. If crossing cost dominates, prototype the self-contained interpreter-loop variant with native-to-wasm migration of value/scheduler services and only stream/FFI ABI crossings. Compare it against compiled bodies with batched definitions/block dispatch on the same workloads; select by measurements while keeping explicit resumable state. Any service migration reuses/compiles the same Rust crate semantics where the toolchain/profile permits it, rather than authoring a third engine; the i32-handle `yin.vm.engine.host.v1` ABI and full kit remain the contract. K3 may measure wasm on this same harness alongside its JVM/Rust/AOT comparisons; these measurements do not commission K4.\ndocs/design/yang.wasm.md:510:### 6.2 Lowering non-goals and risks\ndocs/design/yang.wasm.md:529:## Reference conventions\ndocs/design/yang.wasm.md:554:## Open items\ndocs/design/yin.vm.debruijn.register.md:5:> **Amendment 2026-10-10: A is the only code identity.