{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_14","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/zsh -lc 'tail -15 collab/1792392118180-architect-wasm-boundary-clarification-restructure.gpt-6-astra.stdout.log'","aggregated_output":"{\"type\":\"item.completed\",\"item\":{\"id\":\"item_5\",\"type\":\"command_execution\",\"command\":\"/bin/zsh -lc \\\"sed -n '1,310p' docs/design/yang.wasm.md\\\"\",\"aggregated_output\":\"# yang.wasm: lowering register images to WebAssembly modules\\n\\nStatus: design, owner-authorized planning; not implemented.\\n\\nConsensus provenance (2026-10-18): gpt-6-astra, author; gemini-4-argon,\\nreviewer; five review rounds, APPROVE on r5. Author thread:\\n`01a14f30-ca56-7530-bb93-3a870eca7a7b`; reviewer thread:\\n`f365ef1d-caa2-4379-8e96-51e64ca36af8`. This specification distills the\\ncompiler and target-ABI half of the frozen r5 consensus: semantic\\nfidelity, portability, determinism and Rust-kernel reconciliation. It\\ndoes not report an implementation or runtime acceptance result.\\n\\n2026-10-19 decomposition, owner-ruling, and fold notes:\\n1. **Decomposition and owner-directed fold (2026-10-19)**: Register-image-to-WebAssembly lowering and all 23\\n   opcode lowerings live in this specification (`yang.wasm.md`). By explicit\\n   owner ruling, `docs/design/yin.vm.register-wasm.md` and\\n   `docs/design/yin.vm.conformance-kit.md` are retired and folded into the\\n   existing architecture documents: the guest-agnostic execution host, the\\n   canonical `yin.vm.engine.host.v1` service protocol, and the conformance\\n   **method** live in [`yin.vm.engine.md`](./yin.vm.engine.md) (`§E.1–§E.5`);\\n   the register-machine wasm execution instance, register fixture schema, and\\n   register W0–W6 / Rust K1–K5 lane gates live in\\n   [`yin.vm.debruijn.register.md`](./yin.vm.debruijn.register.md) (`§10`).\\n2. **Owner ruling (2026-10-19, ABI naming)**: Because the host-independent execution\\n   contract belongs to `yin.vm.engine`, the service import ABI is renamed\\n   `yin.register.host.v1` → `yin.vm.engine.host.v1`, and its canonical\\n   protocol tables live in [`yin.vm.engine.md`](./yin.vm.engine.md)\\n   `§E.3`. Section 4 below specifies the compiler's target-interface\\n   binding to `yin.vm.engine.host.v1` and references those tables. Normative\\n   content is otherwise unchanged.\\n\\n## Authorities\\n\\n- [datom.world.md](./datom.world.md): foundational axioms and invariants.\\n- [yang.antlr.md](./yang.antlr.md): frontend compilation into the Universal AST.\\n- [yin.vm.code-as-tuples.md](./yin.vm.code-as-tuples.md): Universal AST content and projection discipline.\\n- [yin.vm.semantic.md](./yin.vm.semantic.md): A identity, semantic execution and A→R derivation.\\n- [yin.vm.debruijn.register.md](./yin.vm.debruijn.register.md): register contract, live sets, sparse continuations, and (`§10`) the register-machine WebAssembly execution instance (the eight materialized register fields, sparse continuations on wasm, `register-restore`, register fixture schema, and register-specific W0–W6 / Rust K1–K5 gates).\\n- [yin.vm.linker.md](./yin.vm.linker.md): admission, offset tables and module transport.\\n- [yin.vm.engine.md](./yin.vm.engine.md): scheduler, effects, custody, and (`§E.1–§E.5`) the guest-agnostic execution host, `yin.vm.engine.host.v1` service protocol, and K0/W0 conformance method.\\n- [yin.vm.rust-kernel.md](./yin.vm.rust-kernel.md): shared Rust crates, Value ownership, transition reuse and LLVM relationship.\\n\\nSource precedence and the explicit Rust-design reconciliations below are\\nretained from r5.\\n\\n---\\n\\n## 1. Pipeline position, decision and source precedence\\n\\nPipeline position:\\n\\n    yang source --(yang.antlr)--> Universal AST --(project)--> semantic vector A\\n        --(yin.vm.semantic/derive register-image)--> register image R\\n        --(yang.wasm, this spec)--> WebAssembly module (disposable local artifact)\\n\\n**A is the only code identity.** R is the register image checksum. The\\nemitted WebAssembly module is a derived, disposable local execution\\nartifact with no identity in `dao.jing`, no content address, and no\\npresence in continuation payloads or module manifests.\\n\\nImplement a **compiled register backend with resumable body functions and an explicit\\ntrampoline**. Each declared body becomes a wasm function with one local per physical register.\\nIntra-body control initially uses generated `br_table` dispatch; guest calls and returns update\\nexplicit machine state and return to the trampoline. No guest continuation ever depends on a\\nwasm activation. Target host services through the versioned plain-data linear-memory\\n**guest-host service ABI** `yin.vm.engine.host.v1` (§4 below; canonical protocol\\ntables in\\n[`yin.vm.engine.md`](./yin.vm.engine.md) §E.3, r5 §4.1.1). The existing CLJS engine is one\\nconforming embedder; a native service implementation shared by Wasmtime and Wasmer adapters is\\nanother ([`yin.vm.engine.md`](./yin.vm.engine.md) §E.4,\\n[`yin.vm.debruijn.register.md`](./yin.vm.debruijn.register.md) §10). Neither JS nor a browser is required to\\nexecute the same emitted binary.\\n\\nThe toolchain is a JVM Clojure emitter producing deterministic WAT, assembled with WABT and\\ninstantiated unchanged by Node/browser, Wasmtime or Wasmer. This is emission from register\\nimages, not compilation of ClojureScript to wasm. W0 names exact toolchain pins\\n([`yin.vm.engine.md`](./yin.vm.engine.md) §E.5.3);\\nthese are implementation acceptance targets, not tools claimed to be installed or tested here. A\\nRust native embedder implements the guest-host service ABI and semantic services without invoking Node, embedding a\\nJS engine, or delegating transitions to a CLJS subprocess. Wasmtime is required beside Node from\\nW1; Wasmer joins the release gate by W5. Browser embedding uses the same ABI. JVM and Dart\\nremain semantic reference lanes; binding wasm into their application APIs is separate from the\\nrequired standalone native embedders.\\n\\nThe mandatory feature profile is **core wasm 1.0 with mutable globals and the original function\\ntable / `call_indirect` facility**: i32/i64/f32/f64, structured control, one non-shared wasm32\\nmemory and one function table per module, single-result functions, active data/element segments.\\nNo externref or first-class reference values, typed function references, `call_ref`,\\n`return_call`, WasmGC, bulk-memory/table instructions, multi-value, multi-memory, memory64,\\nSIMD, exceptions, threads, component model or JS string builtins are assumed. The baseline table\\nelement kind used by `call_indirect` is not permission to emit the later reference-types\\ninstruction set. Copy/clear uses scalar loops; no implicit `memory.copy`, `table.set` or\\n`table.grow` instruction may slip in through optimization. Mutable globals need not cross the\\nimport boundary; instance base coordinates can use immutable scalar imports.\\n\\nEvery extension requires a separate named profile, W0 validation/probe on all target engines and\\nmatching cache key; the baseline artifact must continue to work without it. WABT/Binaryen\\ndefaults are not the feature contract. Uneven native-runtime support for post-MVP features is\\nthe reason for this deliberate cut, not an unresolved portability risk. WasmGC remains a\\nnon-goal despite its inclusion in the September 2025 Wasm 3.0 standard; standardization does not\\nrequire this backend to adopt it. [Wasm 3.0\\nannouncement](https://webassembly.org/news/2025-09-17-wasm-3.0/).\\n\\nWASI stays outside the VM core and its imports. A WASI-based embedder may implement stream\\ntransports/FFI below the same ABI; the generated program imports neither WASI nor\\nruntime-specific system calls. Binaryen is optional after parity; no wasm-ld pipeline is\\nrequired for generated guest code. Native embedder builds have their own pinned native\\ntoolchain.\\n\\nRead authorities (source symbols are semantic specifications for every embedder; calls to\\nexisting CLJC helpers below mean direct reuse in CLJS and equivalently tested native\\ntransitions, not a requirement for native hosts to load Clojure):\\n`src/cljc/yin/vm/debruijn/register.cljc`, `debruijn_register_code.cljc`,\\n`debruijn_register_effects.cljc`, `semantic/derive.cljc`, `semantic.cljc`, `engine.cljc`,\\n`values.cljc`, `yin/vm.cljc`, `linker.cljc`; the register, semantic, linker and engine design\\ndocuments; and the semantic and register test corpora. In particular:\\n\\n- The register contract is version 4 / `\\\"r2\\\"`, with exactly 23 opcodes. Preserve the image, physical L/T allocation, instruction pcs, BLAKE3 checksum, and six in-band live operands. Do not allocate another guest register namespace.\\n- The October amendments to the register and semantic designs make **A the code identity**. R is the register image checksum. Existing `:hash`, `:image`, and offset rows still use R for native compatibility. Do not publish wasm binaries as a new code identity, fetch code by a wasm checksum, or bypass A-to-R admission. Retain access to the Universal AST through existing provenance/addressing.\\n- Older paragraphs in register §5.2 still put code-space fields on return frames and restore code from payloads. Linker §7.3 r6/r7 and the implementation supersede them: return frames contain no code space; restore never rewinds it. Engine documentation (`yin.vm.engine.md` §1.1, §2.1, §3, corrected 2026-10-19) also contained historical `:store-updates` wording; current ready entries update private `:resources` through `:resource-updates`.\\n- Current `vm/machine-data?`, used by register restore and payload validation, recursively accepts kernel-minted Closure/Continuation wrappers by their payloads, as well as exact-integer carriers. It is not identical to `plain-data?`. Arbitrary host functions/errors remain invalid. Preserve the predicate, not an approximate JSON test.\\n- The current module-slice encoder in `engine/encoder` explicitly refuses reified and parked continuations with `:yin.k/status :yin.k/non-portable`, kind `:non-canonicalizable`. Thus universal linker transport of every register continuation is not already implemented. This lane must support the existing native payload where admissible, and preserve qualified refusals elsewhere; it must not silently promise a new universal migration mechanism.\\n\\n### 1.1 Rust-kernel authority, scope and explicit reconciliations\\n\\n[`docs/design/yin.vm.rust-kernel.md`](./yin.vm.rust-kernel.md) revision 2 is an authority for\\nthe native implementation: status and subordination at lines 3–35, crates at 1134–1158,\\ndecisions at 1234–1265. It remains **design-only, not authorized for implementation**. This\\nspecification, [`yin.vm.engine.md`](./yin.vm.engine.md) (§E.1–§E.5), and\\n[`yin.vm.debruijn.register.md`](./yin.vm.debruijn.register.md) (§10) record the owner-authorized wasm plan;\\nthey neither commission K4 LLVM nor the unrelated standalone Rust node/transports. Where native\\nownership, Value, transition reuse, fault isolation and crate boundaries govern, this plan is\\nsubordinate to that design. Crate/type names below are proposed by that document, not claims of\\nimplemented Rust symbols.\\n\\nThis plan explicitly **supersedes only the DEFERRED item “WASM as an artifact target”**\\n(`docs/design/yin.vm.rust-kernel.md:1294–1300`). Its other deferred items and K3-owner-review\\nprerequisite for K4 remain. The following narrower reconciliations prevent silent\\ncontradictions:\\n\\n- Current A identity and A→R admission above govern over the older Rust topology and R-as-identity wording (§3, lines 282–318; §7.5, lines 997–1011). R remains the exact executable checksum/offset-row token. Neither backend creates an identity or bypasses canonical admission.\\n- Rust K-D2b uses u32 indices and qualified host limits (lines 434–448, 1245–1246); wasm keeps 64-bit **wire pc fields**, not a promise of unrestricted native execution. The native facade checks every pc/operand/attachment against the Rust domain and declared limits before conversion or mutation and returns the same qualified `:host-limit`. W0 records the common accepted profile; tests outside it require declared refusals, never truncation or a second widened Rust Machine. Wide register tests above 65,535 still fit u32.\\n- Rust phases exclude existing CLJC edits (lines 93–95, 1164–1165). The deterministic service traversal policy in [`yin.vm.engine.md`](./yin.vm.engine.md) §E.4.3 (r5 §4.1.2) is a separately identified **wasm-driven shared-reference stabilization prerequisite**, outside that K-phase restriction; it remains an explicit scoped amendment for implementation review, not permission for a second native ordering policy. Export a versioned extension of the common kit after that shared change, preserving original goldens/history. This specification changes no source.\\n- Rust public `step` always interprets (lines 677–678). Keep that rule for the Rust interpreter lane. The wasm backend's explicit one-instruction test/inspection mode is a different dispatcher used to verify its generated execution; it does not silently replace Rust's public `step`. Optimized wasm run chunks obey the same boundary equivalence law.\\n- The native wasm embedder needs Level-2 engine services in W2–W4 even though the Rust standalone-node roadmap leaves K5 last (§6.3, lines 748–781; §10, lines 1170–1182). Develop/reuse those **same crates** to the slice's required coverage ([`yin.vm.engine.md`](./yin.vm.engine.md) §E.4.2); a Level-1 offload service cannot satisfy native standalone wasm parity. This schedules the necessary library subset for wasm, not the full K5 node or a new Rust network client.\\n\\nOwner question 1 (lines 1282–1284) and C1/A3 (lines 243–258, 1275–1277) align with the owner's\\ninstruction to lower held register images: wasm is a kernel execution strategy over an admitted\\nimage, not a replacement source emitter. Do not reopen that choice or infer authorization for\\nLLVM. The node-local cache choice aligns with question 2; compile farms remain out of scope.\\nWorker envelope choice and K3 commissioning threshold remain with the LLVM work; W0's declared\\nscalar profile fixes only this lane's supported domain, not all future Rust profiles (questions\\nat 1285–1292).\\n\\n---\\n\\n## 2. Emitted module contract and linear-memory layout\\n\\n### 2.1 Values in emitted code: i32 ValueRef locals\\n\\nChoose **i32 ValueRef locals**, not NaN boxing and not externref registers. Reserve 0 = nil, 1 =\\nfalse, 2 = true; all other values use checked arena indices. Every integer, float, string,\\nsymbol, keyword, collection, primitive, closure, continuation, and sealed stream/cursor\\nreference is an arena entry. Numeric computation initially remains in embedder primitive\\nservices: the CLJS adapter uses existing implementations, and the native service implements the\\nsame declared primitive profiles, integer carriers, float behavior, equality and metadata\\nsemantics. W0 codec/profile fixtures prohibit substituting Rust or JS default numeric/coercion\\nrules. Zero, empty strings, and empty collections remain truthy: only references 0 and 1 are\\nfalse.\\n\\nEach embedder owns an explicit per-task arena, not a process global. Linear memory contains a\\nparallel descriptor table: kind, generation, opaque host-slot and optional machine-record\\noffset. The slot is a task-local numeric identity, never a JS object address or native pointer.\\nAll service exchanges serialize plain-data messages in linear memory under\\n`yin.vm.engine.host.v1` (§4 below; [`yin.vm.engine.md`](./yin.vm.engine.md) §E.3, r5 §4.1.1),\\nincluding in Node; there is no privileged direct-object fast path. Known immutable arena values\\nmay be referenced by checked task-local ids in those messages, with definitions transmitted on\\nfirst use.\\n\\nUsing i32 handles avoids passing i64 values as JS numbers or requiring externref persistence. It\\ncosts host crossings and arena indirection. This is acceptable for the first semantic\\nimplementation: register instructions themselves contain no numeric arithmetic opcode. A later\\nnative scalar fast path needs explicit numeric laws and benchmarks, not a change to the public\\nimage contract.\\n\\nHost-side value reconstruction, the native `handle↔Value` facade over `yin-value`'s 16-byte\\n`#[repr(C, u8)]` `Value` enum (`K-D2`), and the three mandatory encoding/hashing domains\\n(`yin-scalar`, `yin-image`, `yin-wire`) are specified in\\n[`yin.vm.engine.md`](./yin.vm.engine.md) §E.2.1 and §E.2.2.\\n\\n### 2.2 Private linear-memory layout\\n\\nUse imported, non-shared wasm32 memory, little-endian and bounds checked. One task owns one\\nmemory arena; all its compiled image instances import that arena. Separate tasks receive\\nseparate arenas even when compiled Module objects are cached. A supplied coremem can implement\\nthe same interface only with task isolation and allocation ownership; shared mutable guest state\\nis not introduced.\\n\\n| Region / record | Contents and rules |\\n|---|---|\\n| Task header | ABI version, run status, absolute pc as unsigned 64-bit field, body id, active register count/pointer, lexical-chain pointer, return-stack pointer, store-of ValueRef, value ValueRef, host-state token, code-layout generation, scratch/request pointers. Pointer fields are u32. |\\n| Body directory | Absolute start/end pcs, register/local counts, image row, compiled function slot, body-relative entry mapping. Append-only while attached. |\\n| Active register shadow | Dense array of u32 ValueRefs of exactly the current body's `:registers` size. L slots are initialized from positional arguments; T slots start nil. Wasm locals cache this array while a body executes. |\\n| Lexical frames | Immutable vector records: count followed by ValueRefs. Chain is an immutable outermost-first vector of frame pointers. Depth d indexes `count - 1 - d`, then position. |\\n| Return stack | Immutable/persistent vector of return-record pointers, innermost last. Records contain site-pc, return-pc, frames pointer, sparse pairs pointer, live vector pointer, dest, optional store-of. No segment/hash/layout snapshot. |\\n| Sparse snapshots | Count followed by sorted `(u32 register-index, u32 ValueRef)` pairs. Records do not alias mutable active-register storage. Only the verified live set is captured. |\\n| Continuation record | Immutable snapshot metadata: format/hash/image ValueRefs, capture-time segment and optional images ValueRefs, site-pc, pc, frames, sparse regs/live, return stack, destination presence/index, resume mode, optional store-of. Engine/FFI transport fields remain losslessly represented in the host payload. |\\n| Value descriptors | Kind/generation/host slot plus optional closure or continuation record pointer. Host slot contains the actual immutable value or trusted wrapper. |\\n| Engine state roots | ValueRefs/tokens for store, module-stores, free-env, primitives, modules, wait-set, ready-queue, parked, resources, heap/gc, owner, origin/ancestry, id-counter, `:yin.k/gate`, `:yin.k/issued`, and `:yin.k/closes`. Preserve all other extension keys as well. The actual issue-counter key is `:yin.k/issued`, distinct from id-counter; do not invent `:yin.k/next-issue`. These are state/arena retention roots, not an expansion of the engine’s logical heap-GC root set. The full existing state map is authoritative for engine extension keys. |\\n| Exit mailbox | Pending instruction site/op and argument refs, pending result/error ref, completion kind. Private implementation state, never a portable continuation. |\\n\\nPcs use 64-bit fields so wasm32 pointers do not silently narrow the existing layout domain (up\\nto `2^52-1` in `compose`). Functions use small body-relative i32 dispatch indices after checked\\nresolution. Reject resource/address exhaustion with a qualified backend diagnostic before\\nallocation/attach; never wrap a pc, handle, or byte offset. The counter remains the existing\\nhost integer value and advances only through engine operations.\\n\\nThe program store is logically an immutable map, usually name-keyed but **not restricted to\\nnames**: `:store-get`/`:store-put` accept `:data` keys, including keywords and numbers in the\\ncorpus. Its linear-memory representation is a root ValueRef to the embedder’s persistent map,\\nnot a second wasm hash map; the map and updates are addressable through ABI value\\ndefinitions/state messages on every runtime. All writes go through `engine/put-active` →\\n`engine/store-put`. Non-nil store-of selects only that module's store, with no ambient fallback.\\nPrivate resources and heap are distinct from program store.\\n\\nThe guest-agnostic machine-state-as-data synchronization barrier is specified in\\n[`yin.vm.engine.md`](./yin.vm.engine.md) §E.1 and §E.2.3; the register machine's eight-field\\ninstance and dense-register shadow spilling rules are specified in\\n[`yin.vm.debruijn.register.md`](./yin.vm.debruijn.register.md) §10.1.1 and §10.1.2.\\n\\n### 2.3 Module imports, exports and deterministic emission\\n\\nThe instance exports conceptual `run(state, budget)`, `step(state)`, `read-state(state)` and\\nvalidated state-install operations. The shared runtime module imports `memory` (one wasm32,\\nnon-shared memory), `functions` (one original function table), and\\n**`exchange(control_ptr: i32) -> i32`** from module name `yin.vm.engine.host.v1` (renamed from\\n`yin.register.host.v1` by owner ruling on 2026-10-19; see §4 and\\n[`yin.vm.engine.md`](./yin.vm.engine.md) §E.3). Generated component modules import the same\\nmemory/table and the service entry as needed, plus declared immutable i32 base-offset words. No\\nimport/export passes a JS value, externref, Rust pointer, string object, multi-value result or\\ni64 through a host-call signature. i64 pcs/numbers are bytes in memory; core i64 computation\\ninside wasm is allowed.\\n\\nUse finite status values: TRANSFER (continue trampoline), HOST_SERVICE (complete current\\ninstruction), YIELDED (budget), BLOCKED, PARKED, HALTED, ERROR. These statuses are private\\nscheduling results; exported VM maps retain exact `:blocked?`, `:halted?`, `:value`, wait-set,\\nready-queue, and parked behavior. **Blocking is a normal return, never a trap.** Unexpected wasm\\nvalidation/memory traps are backend faults and never substitutes for `:yin/blocked` or an FFI\\nerror envelope.\\n\\nEmission is byte-deterministic: equal admitted register image, emitter/ABI version, and feature\\nprofile yield byte-identical WAT and assembled `.wasm` binaries across runs and platforms.\\n\\n---\\n\\n## 3. Code generation strategy and all 23 opcode lowerings\\n\\n### 3.1 Choice among the alternatives\\n\\n| Strategy | Benefits | Costs / semantic obligations |\\n|---|---|---|\\n| Fully structured body functions with direct guest `call` | Small fast code for ordinary functions; conventional optimizer input | Captured continuations require every caller activation to be materialized and abandoned; arbitrary resume PCs need secondary entries; suspension cannot retain native stack frames. A shadow stack alone does not unwind native callers. |\\n| One interpreter loop over instruction records | Small wasm binary, trivial append and pc resume | Runtime operand decoding and memory-indexed registers on every step; does not exploit known register operands as locals. Uses the same K0/W0 oracle; retained as a W6 optimization axis with services moved into wasm if serialized crossing cost dominates. |\\n| **Compiled body dispatch plus trampoline** | Static register locals and operands, arbitrary entry PCs, appendable bodies, constant native stack depth, explicit continuations | More generated code than interpreter; indirect dispatch and spills at transfers; per-body branch tables may be large. |\\n\\nChoose the third. A body function has conceptual signature `(state-ptr i32, entry-index i32,\\nbudget i32) -> status i32`; updated budget/state lives in the header. All bodies share the same\\nsignature for a function table. The trampoline resolves absolute pc, calls one body function,\\nthen loops on transfer. A body **never invokes a guest callee directly**. Guest call saves a\\nreturn frame (unless tail), installs callee state, and returns TRANSFER. Return installs caller\\nstate and returns TRANSFER; final return returns HALTED. The trampoline's wasm `call_indirect`\\nis an implementation dispatch, not the guest call stack. On HOST_SERVICE the body has returned;\\na shared runtime service thunk serializes the mailbox and invokes the `yin.vm.engine.host.v1`\\nimport ABI (§4; [`yin.vm.engine.md`](./yin.vm.engine.md) §E.3). It decodes the completed response\\nor returns the blocked/error status. No guest activation remains live across an asynchronous\\nwait. The same thunk and body binaries run on each embedder.\\n\\nConsequently flattening is exact and incremental: every pending guest call is already a sparse\\nmemory record before its callee runs. Capturing K copies/shares those immutable records;\\nabortive application replaces the current chain and returns from the one active body. No stack\\nwalk, Asyncify, native-stack copying, wasm exception unwinding, or retained JS Promise\\ncontinuation is needed.\\n\\n`return_call` is **not required or emitted**. Tail guest calls retain K unchanged and replace\\nthe active lexical chain/register file. This gives bounded native stack depth on engines without\\nthe tail-call extension. A later direct-call optimization may be allowed only for a proven\\nnoncapturing, nonsuspending region with a deoptimization protocol; it is not part of this plan.\\n\\n### 3.1.1 LLVM sibling and shared equivalence law\\n\\nThe wasm path and Rust K4 LLVM path are sibling execution strategies under\\n`docs/design/yin.vm.rust-kernel.md:805–831` (§7.2): from equal reachable boundary states they\\nreach equal later states, ordered effects and refusal diagnostics. Both use body functions,\\nentry-pc dispatch, live sets as spill maps and data guest calls; their native control\\ninstructions differ, their guest semantics do not (§7.3, lines 833–918). W0’s shared kit tests\\nall lanes; neither translator forks the validator, liveness rules, payload schema or semantic\\nservice implementation. An artifact may be discarded at a materialized boundary and execution\\ncontinued by the owning interpreter from the same state. Add wasm→Rust interpreter and wasm→CLJS\\ninterpreter fallback fixtures; no artifact field may be needed to restore.\\n\\n### 3.2 Structuring algorithm\\n\\n1. Admit the image with the exact contract check and `register-image-defect`. Preserve body ranges and the original source instruction vector as metadata. Reuse `body-liveness`/`live-slot-index`; do not derive live sets from the optimized wasm graph.\\n2. Build each body's CFG using the existing successor rules: conditional two-way edge; jump target; no successor for return/halt/resume or tail call; ordinary call and other operations resume at pc+1. Jumps must remain inside their body. Do not assume a hand-authored valid image is the structured syntax emitted by `derive`.\\n3. For W1, make every instruction an entry case. Load locals from shadow, enter a wasm `loop` containing nested void `block`s and a `br_table` selecting the pc case. Each case consumes its operands into locals, leaves an empty operand stack, updates the next pc/case, then branches to dispatch or returns one status. All branch targets have the same empty result type. Functions return their status only at the common exit. Invalid entry takes a qualified fault path, not execution of arbitrary memory.\\n4. For W6, merge straight-line instruction cases into basic blocks, retaining leaders at targets, body entries, all resume pcs, and every externally observable step/exit site. Preserve an entry path for each supported pc; debug one-step mode can retain instruction cases. General irreducible CFGs keep dispatch. A subsequent measured optimization can compute dominators/SCCs and structure single-entry regions as `block`/`loop`/`if`, retaining a local dispatcher for multi-entry SCCs. There is no need to ship a full relooper to get a correct backend.\\n5. Check budget at instruction boundaries for bounded/debug execution. A budget yield materializes the **full** active state and returns YIELDED; it is not a new guest suspension site, has no invented sparse live operand, and is not serializable as an r2 continuation. `step` counts one guest instruction, even when several host service transitions implement it.\\n\\n### 3.3 All 23 opcode lowerings\\n\\n\",\"exit_code\":0,\"status\":\"completed\"}}\n{\"type\":\"item.started\",\"item\":{\"id\":\"item_6\",\"type\":\"command_execution\",\"command\":\"/bin/zsh -lc \\\"sed -n '310,565p' docs/design/yang.wasm.md; sed -n '1211,1500p' docs/design/yin.vm.debruijn.register.md\\\"\",\"aggregated_output\":\"\",\"exit_code\":null,\"status\":\"in_progress\"}}\n{\"type\":\"item.completed\",\"item\":{\"id\":\"item_6\",\"type\":\"command_execution\",\"command\":\"/bin/zsh -lc \\\"sed -n '310,565p' docs/design/yang.wasm.md; sed -n '1211,1500p' docs/design/yin.vm.debruijn.register.md\\\"\",\"aggregated_output\":\"\\nHere `r` means ValueRef local, `next` means pc+1, and “host service” means a synchronous\\n`yin.vm.engine.host.v1` ABI message exchange ([`yin.vm.engine.md`](./yin.vm.engine.md) §E.3)\\nimplementing the specified engine transition after returning from the body, not an application\\ncallback or new external FFI path. The mailbox records the already-read operands so re-entry\\nnever repeats an effect.\\n\\n| Opcode | Lowering and observable behavior |\\n|---|---|\\n| `:const rd value` | Load interned literal reference into rd; next. Preserve actual scalar/collection value and metadata. |\\n| `:load-bound rd depth position` | Read frame chain at `count-1-depth`, then position. Preserve `:load-bound-depth` and `:load-bound-position` failures. Never substitute reserved L locals for the runtime chain. |\\n| `:load-free rd name` | Host `engine/resolve-var` with free-env, active store, primitives, modules. Preserve lookup order, present-nil bindings, Rule R, and module resolution. Write rd; next. |\\n| `:closure rd arity body-pc` | Mint owned Closure with type, arity, absolute body-pc, immutable captured frames, and optional store-of. Write rd; next. Captured frames outlive active body. |\\n| `:move rd rs` | Read rs before writing rd; next. |\\n| `:call rd fn-reg arg-regs tail? live` | Read operator and ordered arguments first. Use `engine/operator-kind`. Closure: nil-fill/drop extras to arity, append bound frame to captured chain, initialize L args and nil T slots; push exact sparse caller frame only for non-tail; set closure store-of and body pc; TRANSFER. Host fn: apply f to args. A typed effect first passes `engine/check-callee-effect!`, then follows the complete call-effect contract below (including blocked results and site builders). An ordinary result writes rd/next or executes return transition for tail. Continuation: `engine/operator-kind` checks ownership first, then `engine/continuation-argument` checks exactly one arg, then check format/image, abort current K and write back captured state; no external data gate on this internal path. |\\n| `:branch-false cond target` | Branch only when nil/false; all other values next. Empty operand stack on either edge. |\\n| `:jump target` | Set pc/case to the verified same-body target. |\\n| `:return rs` | Read value. If K nonempty, pop frame, nil-initialize caller register vector, restore saved pairs and dest, frames and store-of, set value and return-pc; TRANSFER. Else set only value, halted true, store-of nil: keep pc at the executing return instruction and preserve active registers, frames, empty K and all other fields. The return-result restore case first changes pc as specified in [`yin.vm.debruijn.register.md`](./yin.vm.debruijn.register.md) §10.2.2 (r5 §3.2). |\\n| `:halt rs` | Set value, halted true, store-of nil. Does not pop K as return would; preserve pc at the halt instruction (no increment), active registers, frames, K, blocked flag and all other fields. |\\n| `:store-get rd key` | Read literal key from active store, absent → nil; rd/next. |\\n| `:store-put rd key value` | Write the **literal value operand**, not a source register, via put-active; return that value in rd; next. |\\n| `:define rd name rs` | Read rs, put-active under literal name, write rd; next. Never resolve the definition operator. |\\n| `:gensym rd prefix` | Engine gensym using the same task counter as stream/park ids; rd/next. |\\n| `:stream-make rd capacity` | Mint authorized `:stream/make` effect; engine calls supplied make-stream. Preserve default-capacity handling; rd/next. Nonblocking under current contract. |\\n| `:stream-put rd sr vr live` | Build site payload; typed put effect from read sr/vr. On success rd/result and next. On full enqueue exact writer wait entry, advance pc, BLOCKED. Other outcomes follow engine. |\\n| `:stream-cursor rd sr` | Typed cursor effect, engine resource/cell handling, rd/next; nonblocking. |\\n| `:stream-next rd cr live` | Build site payload; typed next effect. Immediate result → rd/next; blocked → reader wait entry, advance pc, BLOCKED; all other outcomes follow engine. |\\n| `:stream-close rd sr` | Typed close effect and engine result, rd/next; nonblocking. |\\n| `:current-continuation rd live` | Snapshot before writing rd at next pc, mint owned Continuation with `:type :reified-continuation`, write rd; next. Destination is excluded from saved live set. |\\n| `:park rd live` | Snapshot and advance pc, call engine park-continuation. Adds id/type to parked record, increments counter, sets value to record and halted true. PARKED status; do not invent a stream wait. |\\n| `:resume parked-id rs` | Literal keyword id and value from rs. Engine resume-continuation performs custody gate/missing-id checks and removes parked entry, then register restore. Abort current path; no next-pc fallthrough. |\\n| `:ffi-call rd op arg-regs live` | Require call pair first; snapshot and read ordered args; pin outgoing refs; park under correlated call-id and advance pc; append request; enter reader wait or full writer wait. Always yields to protocol; no direct call of bridge handler from wasm. |\\n\\n### 3.4 Exact host-function effect completion\\n\\nFor `:call` whose `apply f args` result satisfies `module/effect?`, first use the state returned\\nby `engine/check-callee-effect!`. Build `payload-of` at the original call site from the\\nmaterialized state and relocated combined instruction. Tail calls use `:resume-mode\\n:return-result`, `:dest nil`, and their verified empty live set; non-tail calls use\\n`:write-result` and rd. Pass `{:park-entry-fns (park-entry-fns payload)}` to\\n`engine/handle-effect`, with builders for `:stream/put`, `:stream/next`, and `:module/require`.\\nThe module handler uses the require builder as fallback for `:module/try-require`; gated stream\\npoll uses the next builder as fallback. Do not wire only the dedicated stream opcodes.\\n\\nGiven `{:state state :value value :blocked? blocked?}` from handle-effect:\\n\\n- Blocked: return `(assoc state :pc (inc site-pc))`, with no destination write and no return transition, even for a tail call. The payload retains the pending destination/return mode for later delivery.\\n- Nonblocked tail: run `return-transition` on the **post-effect state** and value. Do not pre-increment pc: if K is empty, this immediate path halts at the call site. This differs observably from a blocked tail effect later restored at the payload’s next pc.\\n- Nonblocked non-tail: set pc to next and write value into rd of `(:registers state)`, preserving the rest of the post-effect state, including `:value` set by handle-effect.\\n\\nNever reconstruct the result from the pre-effect wasm header: module attachment,\\nstore/module-store writes, resources, heap/GC, counters and custody bookkeeping may have\\nchanged. A non-effect host result follows the separate plain-result arm of `register.cljc`,\\nincluding preservation of old `:value` on a non-tail call.\\n\\n### 3.5 Boundary continuation payload and internal continuation call\\n\\nAt a six-op boundary at p, export exactly the payload built by `effects/continuation-payload`:\\n\\n```clojure\\n{:segment capture-time-segment\\n :site-pc p :pc (inc p)\\n :frames outermost-first-frame-vectors\\n :regs [[live-index value] ...] :live [live-index ...]\\n :continuation [return-frame ...]\\n :dest rd-or-nil :resume-mode :write-result-or-return-result\\n :format :yin.debruijn.register\\n :hash capture-time-register-hash\\n :image identity-of-row-containing-resume-pc\\n ;; :images capture-time-offset-table when count > 1\\n ;; :store-of module-address when present\\n}\\n```\\n\\nReturn-frame shape is `{:site-pc :return-pc :frames :regs :live :dest}` plus optional store-of.\\nFrame order, sparse pair order, absent-vs-present optional fields, and keyword values are\\npreserved. The `image-row` convention includes the one-past-last-row endpoint for address\\nhelpers; that does not make it a valid write-result continuation pc: continuation-defect still\\nrequires the site's and resume pc's body to agree.\\n\\nA `:call` of a trusted reified continuation uses the distinct internal path:\\n`engine/operator-kind` ownership classification first, `engine/continuation-argument` arity\\ncheck second, format/image membership third, then write-back; it deliberately skips\\ncontinuation-defect/machine-data gates. For an unowned continuation with wrong arity, ownership\\nfails first with `{:reason :foreign-value :kind :continuation}`, not `:continuation-arity` (and\\nnot `:foreign-continuation`). A live primitive in an internal capture is valid and must not be\\nrejected merely because it could not cross a wire. This is abortive, and applying a retained\\ncapture more than once is allowed; do not mutate its saved records. Explicit parked-id\\nconsumption is separate and removes that parked map entry.\\n\\nHost-side payload materialization, the 5-step `register-restore` algorithm, and cross-layout\\ntransport refusals are specified in [`yin.vm.debruijn.register.md`](./yin.vm.debruijn.register.md) §10.2.\\n\\n---\\n\\n## 4. Target interface specification: Import ABI `yin.vm.engine.host.v1`\\n\\n*(Owner amendment note, 2026-10-19: By explicit owner ruling, the service import ABI is named\\n`yin.vm.engine.host.v1` — renamed from `yin.register.host.v1` in r5 §4.1.1 — and its canonical\\nprotocol tables live in [`yin.vm.engine.md`](./yin.vm.engine.md) §E.3. This section specifies the\\ncompiler's target-interface binding and references those tables rather than duplicating them.)*\\n\\nEmitted WebAssembly modules target the guest-agnostic engine service protocol\\n**`yin.vm.engine.host.v1`** defined in [`yin.vm.engine.md`](./yin.vm.engine.md) §E.3 (r5 §4.1.1).\\nThe emitted module never observes `dao.stream` directly — a wasm module cannot\\nexpress a stream operation — so this protocol is the process-local shim between\\nthe guest and the embedding composition, which alone speaks the system ABI\\n(`datom.world.md`, Streams → Host Boundaries → The system ABI). Owner ruling,\\n2026-10-19: this guest-host ABI exists **only as an engineering-cost measure**\\n(per-instruction crossing cost), never as doctrine; the engine-in-guest lane,\\nwhose sole interface is `dao.stream`, is the strategic endpoint at which it\\ndissolves, and W-gates shrink it in the meantime:\\n\\n1. **Module import bindings ([`yin.vm.engine.md`](./yin.vm.engine.md) §E.3):** The shared runtime module imports `memory` (one wasm32, non-shared memory), `functions` (one original function table), and **`exchange(control_ptr: i32) -> i32`** from module name `yin.vm.engine.host.v1`. Generated component modules import the same `memory`/`functions` and the service entry as needed, plus declared immutable `i32` base-offset words. No import or export passes a JS value, `externref`, Rust pointer, string object, multi-value result, or `i64` through a host-call signature.\\n2. **Control-block layout and buffer ownership ([`yin.vm.engine.md`](./yin.vm.engine.md) §E.3.1):** Emitted service thunks address ABI control exactly as §E.3.1 defines — the control-block field/offset map, magic, version fields, header length, request/response buffer boundaries and task-allocation start are owned by that section, and the compiler must emit offsets consistent with it. Guest allocation/growth occurs between `exchange` calls, never inside `exchange`.\\n3. **Transport statuses, execution statuses, and `fetch-response` ([`yin.vm.engine.md`](./yin.vm.engine.md) §E.3.2):** Emitted service thunks handle the transport-status set and the response-retry rule exactly as §E.3.2 defines: on `NEED_SPACE` the guest thunk resizes the response buffer to the required byte length and issues a `fetch-response` for the same sequence number without re-executing or re-sending the semantic transition. Decoded responses carry the private scheduling status set of §E.3.2.\\n4. **TLV framing, expanded-canonical-bytes ordering, and Float64/NaN encoding ([`yin.vm.engine.md`](./yin.vm.engine.md) §E.3.3):** All request/response payloads and constant/value definitions emitted or decoded by the runtime module obey §E.3.3's single outer framing rule, its expanded-canonical-bytes ordering rule (map keys and set elements sorted before tag-`13` handle compression), and its Float64/NaN canonical encoding.\\n5. **Message envelope, register snapshot fields, and compiled vs. service division ([`yin.vm.engine.md`](./yin.vm.engine.md) §E.3.4, [`yin.vm.debruijn.register.md`](./yin.vm.debruijn.register.md) §10.1):** Each service request carries the materialized register execution snapshot (`pc`/`registers`/`frames`/`K`/`store-of`/`value`/`halted`/`blocked` plus layout generation) before any service that can collect or park. External continuation export resolves tag-`13` arena references to the payload values in §3.5 or refuses. Pure body control, register moves, and guest closure calls stay compiled in the emitted wasm module; the host service is never allowed to “run the remaining program” to manufacture the expected trace.\\n\\nThe host-side implementation of `yin.vm.engine.host.v1`, the **single-engine-port rule**, and the\\n“one transition implementation, two dispatchers” rule are specified in\\n[`yin.vm.engine.md`](./yin.vm.engine.md) §E.4.1 and §E.4.2.\\n\\n---\\n\\n## 5. Per-image-row compilation, relocation, and artifact-cache law\\n\\nCompile each independently admitted image into a wasm Module whose body entry functions import\\nthe same `yin.vm.engine.host.v1` ABI memory and services. Instantiate it for the receiving task\\nand install its functions into the task's imported function table using the runtime embedding\\nAPI. This table maps implementation body ids to uniform functions; it is not the guest offset\\ntable.\\n\\nAttach is a staged, append-only transaction:\\n\\n1. Apply the same contract/validator gates as `attach-image`. Empty images are no-ops; duplicate component R is a no-op. Production admission retains the existing A-to-R derivation check before executable attachment.\\n2. Compute offset = held instruction count. Relocate every operand whose opcode-table kind is `:pc`; shift body start/end. Compute the exact combined segment and `register-hash`, and append `[component-R offset length]`.\\n3. Compile/instantiate the new component, reserve directory/table capacity, install its body entries, then publish the new code-layout generation atomically. Prefer component-relative constants plus an instance base offset so the same compiled Module can serve different task layouts. Every pc generated from a component-relative coordinate adds the instance base exactly once before entering public state: header/exit pc, payload site/next pc, return-frame site/return pc, and minted closure body-pc. Incoming absolute restore/return pcs are already absolute and must not receive another base. Resolve absolute pcs through the combined body directory and subtract the selected body start for local dispatch.\\n4. Preserve pc, registers, frames, K, all pending payloads, stores, and queues. No existing function slot or instruction pc is renumbered; old compiled instances remain reachable. Failure before publish leaves the old task executable. Attach only while wasm is at a materialized boundary.\\n\\nFor every boundary payload, fetch the instruction as `(nth (:instructions (:segment vm)) (:pc\\nvm))` from the current relocated **combined** segment at the materialized absolute site pc. Pass\\nthat instruction and current segment/hash/images to `effects/continuation-payload`; never pair\\ncomponent-relative site metadata or a component-only segment with absolute payload pcs. The\\ncached component image is compilation input, not the current capture-time code space. Even where\\nthe live operand is numerically unchanged by relocation, all payload coordinates and validation\\ncontext must agree.\\n\\nAn empty-image creation seeds `:images [[empty-R 0 0]]`, `:hash nil`, `:pc 0`, `:registers []`,\\nand `:halted? true`. Attaching a nonempty image of length n at offset 0 keeps the empty row and\\nproduces `[[empty-R 0 0] [R 0 n]]`; it does not unhalt the task, resize registers, or initialize\\na body. Reset or a valid execution-entry transition performs that separate work. A later capture\\nincludes `:images` because the row count is two, even though only one row contains instructions.\\nKeep the zero-length row in payload validation and layout operations; it is distinct from the\\nrule that attaching an empty image is a no-op.\\n\\nThere is no machine-code patching and no need to grow an existing wasm Module's function\\nsection. The baseline preallocates table capacity (W0 test profile: 65,536 slots) and uses host\\ntable installation plus directory append for new functions; exhaustion is a qualified resource\\nrefusal. No guest `table.grow`/`table.set` instruction is required. Optional host-API growth\\nmust pass the W0 probe on each runtime and must not change the baseline module feature profile.\\nDynamic instantiation/attachment occurs outside an active exchange after returning a staged\\nhost-work result; the next synchronized state publishes the new layout. The same component bytes\\nand import names are used on all runtimes. Old call frames use absolute return-pc and resolve in\\nthe grown directory. Captures retain their old segment/hash for validation, but resume sees the\\ncurrent code space. Reset preserves offset rows as in the kernel; load-image replaces the code\\nspace and resets execution, so old continuation membership checks can fail.\\n\\nHost-side `spawn-module`, `module/IModuleKernel` closure/resource lifting and lowering, and\\nmodule store snapshots are specified in [`yin.vm.engine.md`](./yin.vm.engine.md) §E.4.4 and\\n[`yin.vm.debruijn.register.md`](./yin.vm.debruijn.register.md) §10.3. Cache compiled code by A, r2\\nderivation/checksum, wasm ABI/emitter version and feature profile; R alone is not executable\\nadmission authority.\\n\\nArtifact policy is also shared with LLVM: `docs/design/yin.vm.rust-kernel.md:997–1011,\\n1113–1122, 1261–1263`. Wasm binaries are disposable **node-local caches**, never dao.jing\\ncontent identities, refs, continuation fields or portable module payloads. Include\\ntarget/profile, translator and ABI versions in the cache key alongside the admitted A/R\\nprovenance above. No cross-node artifact fetching/sharing is introduced. A missing/dropped\\nartifact falls back to the same host’s register interpreter; no compiler or LLVM worker is\\nrequired to resume. Install derived artifacts only at materialized boundaries, with no pending\\nmutation or active guest stack. Body-relative code and task base offsets follow the same\\nrow-local cache rule in both backends.\\n\\n---\\n\\n## 6. Compiler toolchain, optimization axis (W6), non-goals, and risks\\n\\n### 6.1 Toolchain and W0–W6 lowering gates\\n\\nThe full W0–W6 slice schedule, runtime acceptance gates, and exact toolchain pins (`Node 25.6.1`,\\n`Clojure CLI 1.12.4.1602 / shadow-cljs 3.3.5`, `WABT 1.0.37`, `Wasmtime =38.0.0`, `Wasmer 6.0.1`,\\n`Rust / Cargo 1.90.0`) are specified in [`yin.vm.engine.md`](./yin.vm.engine.md) §E.5.3 and\\n[`yin.vm.debruijn.register.md`](./yin.vm.debruijn.register.md) §10.5.\\n\\nFor the lowering pipeline specifically:\\n- **W0**: Pin the JVM Clojure WAT emitter and WABT `1.0.37` assembler/validator with an explicit baseline feature allowlist; reject every opcode/type outside the baseline allowlist even when an engine enables extensions by default. Binaryen is absent from the baseline toolchain.\\n- **W1–W5**: Compile each admitted image once and execute those identical wasm bytes under Node, Wasmtime (from W1), and Wasmer (complete W1–W4 suite in W4, release gate in W5), verifying byte-identical artifact emission and `yin.vm.engine.host.v1` import allowlist compliance.\\n- **W6 (measured optimization, after correctness)**: Keep Node + Wasmtime + Wasmer full-trace parity mandatory for every optimization. Measure compilation latency, binary size, native stack depth, arena/heap retention, dispatch cost, serialized message bytes/copy/codec time, call and suspension cost, and end-to-end time on each embedder; compare Node against interpreted register and report native results separately without attributing service-language speed to wasm codegen. Only then coalesce blocks, reduce host crossings, add dirty spilling, or use optional Binaryen passes. Re-run all parity and binary validation gates for each transformation. If crossing cost dominates, prototype the self-contained interpreter-loop variant with native-to-wasm migration of value/scheduler services and only stream/FFI ABI crossings. Compare it against compiled bodies with batched definitions/block dispatch on the same workloads; select by measurements while keeping explicit resumable state. Any service migration reuses/compiles the same Rust crate semantics where the toolchain/profile permits it, rather than authoring a third engine; the i32-handle `yin.vm.engine.host.v1` ABI and full kit remain the contract. K3 may measure wasm on this same harness alongside its JVM/Rust/AOT comparisons; these measurements do not commission K4.\\n\\n### 6.2 Lowering non-goals and risks\\n\\nNon-goals for the lowering: no new opcode/contract version, alternate A→R allocator, native\\nstack continuations, async-stack capture, universal cross-model continuation conversion, new\\nportable continuation schema, direct arbitrary host FFI imports, WASI imports in the VM core,\\nthreads/shared memory, WasmGC dependency, numeric NaN boxing, cross-node artifact service, or\\noptimization before W5 correctness. Guest-agnostic and register-runtime non-goals are listed in\\n[`yin.vm.engine.md`](./yin.vm.engine.md) §E.5.4 and\\n[`yin.vm.debruijn.register.md`](./yin.vm.debruijn.register.md) §10.6.\\n\\nLowering-specific risks (retained from r5 §7; host/runtime risks in\\n[`yin.vm.engine.md`](./yin.vm.engine.md) §E.5.4 and\\n[`yin.vm.debruijn.register.md`](./yin.vm.debruijn.register.md) §10.6):\\n- **Large bodies and dispatch size.** One instruction per case can create deep block nesting and large binaries. W0 should pin practical limits; W6 can shard dispatch into bounded pages/helpers while keeping entry-pc precision. Such helpers must never hold suspended guest state in their native stack.\\n- **Module growth and code retention.** Wasm module code cannot be appended in place. Many image instances may increase memory and startup cost; function-table limits and compile failures require explicit resource refusals. Code unloading while live absolute pcs exist is out of scope.\\n- **Canonical admission and provenance.** Bare hand images remain useful under the existing raw kernel API; production module execution must retain A derivation verification. Compiled wasm caches are disposable artifacts. They cannot become another authority for code or continuation identity.\\n\\n---\\n\\n## Reference conventions\\n\\nReference convention: `register.cljc` means `src/cljc/yin/vm/debruijn/register.cljc`; `engine`,\\n`ffi`, `module`, `values`, and `debruijn_register_effects` filenames are under\\n`src/cljc/yin/vm/`; `vm` means `src/cljc/yin/vm.cljc`; `apply2` is the alias for\\n`dao.stream.apply` at `src/cljc/dao/stream/apply.cljc`. Unqualified register helpers such as\\nreturn-transition, write-back, payload-of, park-entry-fns, attach-image and spawn-module are in\\nregister.cljc; spawn-module/gc-roots are protocol implementations, not invented defns.\\n`module/IModuleKernel` and its gc-roots method are declared in module.cljc. IVM step/run are\\nmethods of `yin.vm/IVM`, not a namespace named IVM. `effects/*` names denote\\ndebruijn_register_effects.cljc. `register-image-defect`, `body-liveness`, `live-slot-index`,\\n`register-hash` and `opcode-table` belong to `src/cljc/yin/vm/debruijn_register_code.cljc`\\n(alias rcode); the `register-image` derivation belongs to\\n`src/cljc/yin/vm/semantic/derive.cljc`. `halted-with-empty-queue?`, `scheduler-round`,\\n`check-wait-set`, `handle-close` and `collect` are engine.cljc functions. The quoted\\nrestore/call helper names are existing source semantics, whereas exchange/fetch-response, ABI\\nstatuses and all native implementation entry names are proposed protocol vocabulary.\\n\\nThe `.cljc` line references in this specification, [`yin.vm.engine.md`](./yin.vm.engine.md)\\n(§E.1–§E.5), and [`yin.vm.debruijn.register.md`](./yin.vm.debruijn.register.md) (§10) are the r5-verified\\nreferences, checked against the current tree for this distillation. Rust-design line references\\nretain r5's coordinates; the related-spec additions to that document do not shift its existing\\nlines. New ABI names and native entry names describe planned interfaces, not existing repository\\nfunctions.\\n\\n## Open items\\n\\nThe frozen r5 text is retained where it leaves this codegen/host-boundary detail in tension;\\nthis decomposition does not resolve it (see [`yin.vm.engine.md`](./yin.vm.engine.md) §E.5.5 and\\n[`yin.vm.debruijn.register.md`](./yin.vm.debruijn.register.md) §10.6 for the runtime\\ntransport-test notation item):\\n\\n- §4 / [`yin.vm.engine.md`](./yin.vm.engine.md) §E.3.4 (r5 §4.1.1) says guest closure calls stay\\n  compiled, while the single-port rule in [`yin.vm.engine.md`](./yin.vm.engine.md) §E.4.2 (r5\\n  §4.1.1) requires service sites to reuse the owning host's call transition. The division\\n  between generated dispatch and shared transition work needs to be made concrete without\\n  creating a second semantic implementation.\\n## 10. Wasm execution instance (2026-10-19, design only)\\n\\nStatus: design, owner-authorized planning; not implemented. Sections 1–9 above\\n(as amended on 2026-10-10 and 2026-10-15) remain authoritative for the register\\nimage contract and the landed R4 register kernel.\\n\\n> **Amendment 2026-10-19 (design only): WebAssembly execution instance and\\n> K0/W0 register conformance fixtures.**\\n>\\n> Consensus provenance (2026-10-18): gpt-6-astra, author; gemini-4-argon,\\n> reviewer; five review rounds, APPROVE on r5. Author thread:\\n> `01a14f30-ca56-7530-bb93-3a870eca7a7b`; reviewer thread:\\n> `f365ef1d-caa2-4379-8e96-51e64ca36af8`. It does not report an implementation\\n> or runtime acceptance result.\\n>\\n> Owner rulings and decomposition lineage (2026-10-19):\\n> 1. Register-image-to-WebAssembly lowering, the 10-region linear-memory layout,\\n>    and all 23 opcode lowerings live in [`yang.wasm.md`](./yang.wasm.md).\\n> 2. Because the host-independent execution contract belongs to `yin.vm.engine`,\\n>    the service import ABI is named `yin.vm.engine.host.v1` (renamed from\\n>    `yin.register.host.v1` in r5 §4.1.1 by explicit owner ruling on 2026-10-19).\\n>    The guest-agnostic execution-host contract, the canonical\\n>    `yin.vm.engine.host.v1` protocol tables, and the K0/W0 differential\\n>    conformance method live in [`yin.vm.engine.md`](./yin.vm.engine.md)\\n>    (`§E.1–§E.5`).\\n> 3. By explicit owner ruling (2026-10-19), neither `yin.vm.register-wasm.md`\\n>    nor `yin.vm.conformance-kit.md` exists as a separate `yin.vm.*` document:\\n>    the register-machine instance of the WebAssembly execution host and the\\n>    register-specific K0/W0 conformance fixtures and W0–W6 slice gates are\\n>    folded into this section (§10). Normative content is otherwise frozen.\\n> 4. **Precedence over older §5.2 prose in this document:** §5.2.2 above\\n>    (lines 866–878) still shows `:segment` and `:hash` on each return frame;\\n>    `yin.vm.linker.md` §7.3 r6/r7 and current\\n>    `src/cljc/yin/vm/debruijn_register_effects.cljc` supersede that prose:\\n>    return frames contain `{:site-pc :return-pc :frames :regs :live :dest}`\\n>    plus optional `:store-of`, with no code space, and restore never rewinds\\n>    code space. Likewise §5.2.3 above (lines 911, 953) uses historical\\n>    `:store-updates` wording; current `src/cljc/yin/vm/engine.cljc` merges\\n>    ready-entry `:resource-updates` into private `:resources` (refusing\\n>    non-keyword keys with `{:rule :resource-update-key}`).\\n\\nAuthorities and companion specifications for §10:\\n- [datom.world.md](./datom.world.md): foundational axioms and invariants.\\n- [yin.vm.semantic.md](./yin.vm.semantic.md): A identity, semantic execution, and A→R derivation.\\n- [yin.vm.linker.md](./yin.vm.linker.md): admission, offset tables, and module transport.\\n- [yin.vm.engine.md](./yin.vm.engine.md): scheduler, effects, custody, and (`§E.1–§E.5`) the guest-agnostic execution host, `yin.vm.engine.host.v1` service protocol, and K0/W0 differential conformance method.\\n- [yin.vm.rust-kernel.md](./yin.vm.rust-kernel.md): shared Rust crates, `Value` ownership, transition reuse, and LLVM sibling relationship.\\n- [yang.wasm.md](./yang.wasm.md): lowering register images to WebAssembly modules, the 10-region linear-memory layout, and all 23 opcode lowerings targeting `yin.vm.engine.host.v1`.\\n\\n### 10.1 Register machine state and the register instance of the synchronization barrier\\n\\nThis section defines the **register-machine instance** (`version 4 / \\\"r2\\\"`, 23\\nopcodes) of the engine execution host specified in\\n[`yin.vm.engine.md`](./yin.vm.engine.md) (`§E.1–§E.5`). It executes WebAssembly\\nmodules lowered from admitted register images under [`yang.wasm.md`](./yang.wasm.md)\\nover the `yin.vm.engine.host.v1` service protocol\\n([`yin.vm.engine.md`](./yin.vm.engine.md) §E.3, r5 §4.1.1). Host embedder targets\\n(Node/CLJS, Wasmtime, Wasmer), the task-owned `i32` `ValueRef` arena, the native\\n`handle↔Value` facade over `yin-value`, the three encoding/hashing domains\\n(`yin-scalar`, `yin-image`, `yin-wire`), the single-engine-port rule, and\\nRust-kernel reconciliations are specified in [`yin.vm.engine.md`](./yin.vm.engine.md)\\n§E.2 and §E.4.\\n\\n#### 10.1.1 The eight materialized register fields and the synchronization barrier instance\\n\\nEach register-wasm task owns one imported, non-shared wasm32 memory arena with\\nthe ten-region layout (`Task header`, `Body directory`, `Active register shadow`,\\n`Lexical frames`, `Return stack`, `Sparse snapshots`, `Continuation record`,\\n`Value descriptors`, `Engine state roots`, `Exit mailbox`), 64-bit wire `pc`\\nfields, and program-store `ValueRef` rules specified in\\n[`yang.wasm.md`](./yang.wasm.md) §2.2.\\n\\nAs the **register instance** of the engine extension's machine-state-as-data\\nboundary pattern ([`yin.vm.engine.md`](./yin.vm.engine.md) §E.1), the register\\nmachine's declared execution state consists of **eight materialized fields** on\\nthe VM state map:\\n\\n    :pc  :registers  :frames  :continuation  :store-of  :value  :halted?  :blocked?\\n\\nLinear memory is a private execution cache over these eight fields plus the\\ncurrent register code layout (`:segment`, `:hash`, `:images`) and engine state.\\nThe register instance of the four-step synchronization protocol\\n([`yin.vm.engine.md`](./yin.vm.engine.md) §E.1) is:\\n\\n1. **Public entry / host-to-wasm:** `step`, `run`, and `register-restore` accept the supplied map as authoritative. Import `:pc`, `:registers`, `:frames`, `:continuation`, `:store-of`, `:value`, `:halted?`, and `:blocked?`, plus the current code layout and engine state. W1 imports unconditionally. A later cache may skip only after proving all relevant keys agree with its last materialized snapshot; a retained private token alone is not proof. In particular `(assoc parked :pc 2 :halted? false)` must execute the requested state. Import a copied or privately owned active register shadow so running an old or externally modified map never mutates another returned map.\\n2. **Wasm-to-host:** before every state-consuming `engine/*` call, including `handle-effect`, pinning, collection, scheduler, park/resume and module work, materialize all eight register execution fields (`:pc`, `:registers`, `:frames`, `:continuation`, `:store-of`, `:value`, `:halted?`, `:blocked?`) onto the VM record. Materialize them also at every public `step`, `run`, restore or inspection return. Keep the record's protocol implementations so `module/gc-roots` can inspect the current map. Dense memory synchronization without map synchronization is insufficient.\\n3. **Host completion:** the returned host state is the new authority, including its eight execution fields and every extension key. Apply only the current opcode's specified post-service changes ([`yang.wasm.md`](./yang.wasm.md) §3.3–§3.4) to that returned state, then reimport it into memory. Never overlay a pre-service header onto it. In particular `handle-effect` sets `:value` even for nonblocking effects; preserve that value, resource/heap/GC updates, stores, counters, queues and custody fields. A plain non-tail primitive call, by contrast, only updates `:pc`/`rd` in the kernel and does not automatically set `:value`.\\n4. **Seam `pc`:** before a host service, the map contains the instruction's absolute site `pc`, unless the source transition explicitly advances it first (such as parking in the FFI path). Capture the payload at the site; apply the opcode's `pc` update exactly once after service completion. Mailbox state does not count as a completed guest instruction.\\n\\nBecause `:cell/new` may collect *inside* `handle-effect` before the adapter\\nreceives its result ([`yin.vm.engine.md`](./yin.vm.engine.md) §E.1), map-based\\n`:registers`, `:frames`, `:continuation`, and `:value` must already be current\\nbefore `handle-effect` is entered.\\n\\n#### 10.1.2 Dense register shadow, sparse saved frames, and register GC roots\\n\\nStart with a dense active register shadow (`Active register shadow` in\\n[`yang.wasm.md`](./yang.wasm.md) §2.2) synchronized on every register write and\\nbefore every return to the host. The wasm local is the execution operand; the\\nshadow makes complete current state and GC roots available. **Sparse saved\\nframes and payloads are nevertheless mandatory:** never retain the full dense\\nshadow through a guest call or park. On a successful call/restore, replace the\\nactive shadow; clear abandoned mailbox/argument roots when no longer needed. A\\nparked VM's observable active `:registers` vector may still contain dead slots,\\nas the reference register VM does; do not confuse those current-state roots with\\nthe sparse continuation payload.\\n\\nUnder the arena and engine heap collection rules of\\n[`yin.vm.engine.md`](./yin.vm.engine.md) §E.2.3, mark active `:registers`,\\n`:frames`, `:value`, `:continuation`, `parked`/`wait-set`/`ready-queue` entries,\\n`store`, `module-stores`, engine heap and pinned/outgoing values, constants,\\nand pending mailbox arguments. Traverse trusted wrappers via `values/payload`\\nand preserve the register kernel's `module/gc-children` implementation and its\\ntreatment of code. Persistent snapshots share immutable arena objects, never a\\nmutable register buffer; the first implementation may copy the active dense\\n`:registers` vector at every public `IVM` transition and optimize only after\\nsnapshot-alias tests pass.\\n\\n### 10.2 Sparse continuation materialization, `register-restore`, and layout transport\\n\\n#### 10.2.1 Materialization and capture-time metadata\\n\\nAt any of the six in-band live-set opcodes (`:call`, `:stream-put`,\\n`:stream-next`, `:current-continuation`, `:park`, `:ffi-call`) at site `p`, the\\nexported boundary payload and return-frame shape are those built by\\n`effects/continuation-payload` as specified in [`yang.wasm.md`](./yang.wasm.md)\\n§3.5 (`:segment`, `:site-pc p`, `:pc (inc p)`, `:frames`, sparse `:regs` and\\n`:live`, `:continuation`, `:dest`, `:resume-mode`, `:format\\n:yin.debruijn.register`, `:hash`, `:image`, and optional `:images` /\\n`:store-of`). Return frames contain `{:site-pc :return-pc :frames :regs :live\\n:dest}` plus optional `:store-of`, with no code space.\\n\\nMaterialization recursively resolves `ValueRef`s and never puts numeric handle\\nIDs or tag-`13` arena references into payload values. Import performs the\\ninverse into immutable records. The capture-time `:segment`/`:hash`/`:images`\\nare validation data and must survive later `attach-image` unchanged. They can\\nshare immutable objects in memory, but a plain-data export must contain the\\nactual required image data. A wasm function index, memory pointer, instance id,\\nor private ABI version is not a guest format identity.\\n\\n#### 10.2.2 `register-restore` algorithm and failure ordering\\n\\nExpose one adapter restore with the same `(base entry val) -> state` contract\\n([`yin.vm.engine.md`](./yin.vm.engine.md) §2.1) and use it from both engine\\nresume paths:\\n\\n1. Check `:format` equals `:yin.debruijn.register` and entry `:image` is in base `:images`; preserve `:rule :continuation-format`, `:reason :foreign-format`, and diagnostic context. Do **not** compare entry `:hash` to current base `:hash`.\\n2. Run the existing `effects/continuation-defect` against the entry, preserving deterministic first-defect results, including capture-time hash recomputation (`yin-image` R in [`yin.vm.engine.md`](./yin.vm.engine.md) §E.2.2) and per-component image/table validation.\\n3. Check the actual `vm/machine-data?` predicate on `val`; reject unsupported host functions/errors with `:rule :resume-value`. Do not turn an error-shaped ordinary map into an exception.\\n4. If `request-sent`, enqueue `ffi/response-wait-entry` using `call-id` and remain blocked with `:yin/blocked`, `halted` false ([`yin.vm.engine.md`](./yin.vm.engine.md) §4 and §E.4.4). Do not deliver writer acknowledgement into `rd`.\\n5. Otherwise remove `call-id` from `parked` when present and apply `ffi/call-result` to its response. For `:write-result` allocate the receiving body's register count, nil-fill, copy sparse pairs, then write `dest`; restore `pc`, `frames`, `K`, `store-of`, `value` and `halted` false. For `:return-result` first `assoc` `:pc (:pc entry)`, `:frames (:frames entry)`, `:continuation (:continuation entry)`, `:store-of (:store-of entry)`, and `:halted? false` onto `base`, leaving `:registers` equal to `(:registers base)`; then run the ordinary `return-transition` ([`yang.wasm.md`](./yang.wasm.md) §3.3). With empty captured `K` this halts at the entry's next `pc`, retains `base` registers and captured frames, and clears `store-of`. With nonempty `K`, the popped caller frame replaces `pc`/`registers`/`frames`/`store-of` as usual. Preserve `base`'s `store`, `resources`, queues, counters, `modules`, and code space.\\n\\nDo not clear `blocked` universally in the low-level restore: the engine clears\\nit in ready-queue resumption; direct paths preserve the source's exact field\\nbehavior. Public APIs should route through engine scheduling instead of\\nmanufacturing a partially cleared state. Failed restoration must not commit a\\nmutated arena/state snapshot: stage validation and allocations before publishing\\nthe successful transition.\\n\\nThe distinct internal `:call` path for a trusted reified continuation\\n(`engine/operator-kind` ownership first → `engine/continuation-argument` arity\\nsecond → format/image membership third → `write-back`, skipping external\\n`continuation-defect`/`machine-data?` gates) is specified in\\n[`yang.wasm.md`](./yang.wasm.md) §3.3 and §3.5.\\n\\n#### 10.2.3 Cross-runtime continuation transport and layout compatibility\\n\\nSame-layout native payloads with transportable values can round-trip between\\ninterpreted register and wasm execution. Inside the same task, trusted wrappers\\ntravel across `yin.vm.engine.host.v1` as checked task-local capability\\nreferences or trusted-constructor results ([`yin.vm.engine.md`](./yin.vm.engine.md)\\n§E.3.4), without granting external stream portability. For the present\\nmodule/linker path, keep the existing `:yin.k/non-portable` /\\n`:non-canonicalizable` continuation refusal in `engine/encoder`.\\n\\nIf a future authorized transport carries register continuation coordinates\\nimage-relatively, it must translate **site-pc, pc, every return frame's\\nsite/return pc, and recursively all closure body pcs**, attach admitted origins,\\nreconstruct self-consistent segment/hash/live metadata, and apply normal native\\n`register-restore`. Raw payloads with absolute pcs must not be fed to a\\ndifferently ordered receiving layout merely because one R is present. W0 must\\ngate the native transport API to a verified matching layout/prefix or return\\nqualified `:continuation-format`; this transport restriction does not change\\nsame-task `register-restore` semantics. General rebasing and canonical semantic\\nUCF migration remain outside this backend's completion claim.\\n\\n#### 10.2.4 Register wait-entry validation scope\\n\\nGuest-agnostic stream, park, custody-gate, and FFI two-step rules are specified\\nin [`yin.vm.engine.md`](./yin.vm.engine.md) §E.4.4. For register wait entries\\nspecifically, `effects/wait-entry-defect` is an acceptance oracle **only for its\\nfour newly built stream/FFI forms**: `put`/`next` writers/readers and\\n`request-sent`/`call-id` writer/reader entries. Do not apply it universally to\\nthe wait set: `link-request`, `link-response`, `install`, and gated `observe`\\nentries are outside its shape vocabulary. For these, check\\n`effects/continuation-defect` on the register payload plus the owning\\nmodule/engine reason-specific fields and transitions. Do not apply a newly\\nparked-entry oracle to stale-key-bearing ready entries either.\\n\\n### 10.3 Register module attachment, `spawn-module`, and artifact cache\\n\\nPer-image-row compilation, PC relocation, the four-step staged `attach-image`\\ntransaction, combined-segment boundary payload reads, empty-base\\n`[[empty-R 0 0]]` offset-table rules, and the 65,536-slot preallocated function\\ntable are specified in [`yang.wasm.md`](./yang.wasm.md) §5. The host\\ninstantiates each admitted component for the receiving task and installs its\\nbody entry functions into the task's imported function table using the runtime\\nembedding API, outside an active `yin.vm.engine.host.v1` `exchange` after\\nreturning a staged host-work result; the next synchronized state publishes the\\nnew layout. `reset` preserves offset rows as in the register kernel;\\n`load-image` replaces the code space and resets execution, so old continuation\\nmembership checks can fail.\\n\\nUnder the `spawn-module` isolation rules of\\n[`yin.vm.engine.md`](./yin.vm.engine.md) §E.4.4, register `spawn-module` creates\\na fresh task/memory/arena/owner/counter/resources context with the composition\\noptions copied by the register kernel; it does not borrow the parent's mutable\\nactive register shadow or per-module store. Reuse the module scheduler\\nchild-task lifecycle, encoder/decoder, and `module/IModuleKernel` seams.\\nRegister closure lifting uses image-relative body references and `store-of`;\\nlowering validates binding/format, attached origin, and arity against actual\\nclosure instructions before minting the receiver's trusted Closure value.\\nCache compiled wasm modules by `A`, `r2` derivation/checksum `R`,\\n`yin.vm.engine.host.v1` ABI/emitter version, and feature profile; `R` alone is\\nnot executable admission authority. A missing or dropped artifact falls back to\\nthe same host's register interpreter at any materialized boundary.\\n\\n### 10.4 Register conformance fixture schema (K0/W0)\\n\\n**W0 and Rust K0 are the same conformance-kit deliverable**, exported from R4,\\nnot independent harness authorities\\n(`docs/design/yin.vm.rust-kernel.md:1185–1199`). The guest-agnostic differential\\nconformance method (oracle export discipline, the CLJS/Rust-interpreter/wasm/LLVM\\nlanes, trace-not-just-values comparison, B0-normalized vs. raw boundary\\ncomparison, and determinism/snapshot gates) is specified in\\n[`yin.vm.engine.md`](./yin.vm.engine.md) §E.5.1–§E.5.3.\\n\\nFor the register format and kernel, the K0/W0 kit exports, per corpus program:\\n- the register image as canonical data,\\n- `R` (`register-hash`),\\n- the B0-normalized result or error, and\\n- the format-level trace R4 produces (sparse continuation payload at every boundary, `K` depth, saved register pairs).\\n\\nPlus the required register fixture groups:\\n- refusal fixtures for all fourteen validator rules with rule keywords,\\n- the descriptor hash (`descriptor-hash`),\\n- executable register scalar-encoding vectors (`yin-scalar` / `debruijn_code.cljc`),\\n- diagnostic message fixtures,\\n- refused-step atomicity fixtures (a refused step leaves the state equal),\\n- tail-site fixtures for every row of the Rust design's §7.3 call-outcome table (including a tail primitive value and a tail primitive effect returning into a caller in another body, and halting with `K` empty; `docs/design/yin.vm.rust-kernel.md:833–918`),\\n- a wide-body fixture whose register count exceeds `65,535`,\\n- `:host-limit` refusal fixtures, and\\n- the million-iteration tail fixtures.\\n\\nRequired repository corpora for register-wasm and Rust K0–K5 (each processed by the\\ngeneric projection/admission/execution/comparison procedure of\\n[`yin.vm.engine.md`](./yin.vm.engine.md) §E.5.1; only the register-specific\\nfixtures, bindings, and compared fields are listed here):\\n- `test/yin/vm/semantic/corpus.cljc` **programs and literal goldens**: these goldens specify syntax/address, not a runnable environment or expected final value for every entry. Supply deterministic `f`/`g`/`h`/`k`/`x`/`y`/`c`/`loop` bindings, stream fixtures, and genuine parked targets for resume cases; otherwise compare the same qualified refusal.\\n- All fixtures and assertions of `test/yin/vm/debruijn/register_test.cljc` (plus `test/yin/vm/debruijn_code_test.cljc` and `test/yin/vm/debruijn_register_code_test.cljc`), including hand images, B0/B2 parity, stream scripts, continuation corruption, and `IVM` behavior. The register raw observable fields compared beyond the method's B0-normalized values: register payload shapes, `store`/`module-stores`, `pc`, frame order, `K`, counter, flags, `wait-set`/`ready-queue`/`parked` entries, and emitted request traces.\\n\\n### 10.5 Register-specific W0–W6 implementation slices and acceptance gates\\n\\nGuest-agnostic W0 toolchain pins (`Node 25.6.1`, `Clojure CLI 1.12.4.1602 /\\nshadow-cljs 3.3.5`, `WABT 1.0.37`, `Wasmtime =38.0.0`, `Wasmer 6.0.1`, `Rust /\\nCargo 1.90.0`), core-wasm feature probes, three-codec byte/hash gates,\\n`yin.vm.engine.host.v1` transport gates (`COMPLETE→NEED_SPACE→fetch-response`,\\nrepeated fetch, `BAD_MESSAGE`, `ABI_MISMATCH`, `RESOURCE_LIMIT`), and the W/K\\ndependency map are specified in [`yin.vm.engine.md`](./yin.vm.engine.md)\\n§E.5.2–§E.5.3. The register-specific slice obligations and acceptance gates are:\\n\\n#### 10.5.1 W0 — Register admission, codec, and eight-field synchronization gates\\n\\nAcceptance: all semantic goldens retain their literal vector/address; all\\nderived register images retain `R` and exact live operands. Existing register\\ntests pass unchanged on the reference lane. Invalid contract, Rule R, unknown\\nopcode, cross-body target, bad live set and empty-image cases have identical\\nadmission outcomes. Validate a minimal wasm instance and explicit resource-limit\\ndiagnostics. Establish matching-layout register payload transport and qualified\\n`:continuation-format` refusal for incompatible layouts (§10.2.3). Freeze the\\neight-field register map/memory authority protocol (§10.1.1) and its\\nbyte-message equivalents, including external `assoc`/snapshot replacement,\\nold-state replay, and preservation of unknown engine extension keys; do not\\ndefer that contract to performance tuning. Add cross-embedder vectors for\\n`descriptor-hash`/`R` (including empty and concatenated images), exact encoded\\nbytes, owner/seal/profile inputs, and manifest addresses.\\n\\n#### 10.5.2 W1 — Pure register execution and body dispatch\\n\\nRequired runtime gate: Node + Wasmtime, identical image binaries and full\\n\",\"exit_code\":0,\"status\":\"completed\"}}\n{\"type\":\"item.started\",\"item\":{\"id\":\"item_7\",\"type\":\"command_execution\",\"command\":\"/bin/zsh -lc \\\"sed -n '405,621p' docs/design/yin.vm.engine.md; sed -n '952,1302p' docs/design/yin.vm.engine.md\\\"\",\"aggregated_output\":\"\",\"exit_code\":null,\"status\":\"in_progress\"}}\n{\"type\":\"item.completed\",\"item\":{\"id\":\"item_7\",\"type\":\"command_execution\",\"command\":\"/bin/zsh -lc \\\"sed -n '405,621p' docs/design/yin.vm.engine.md; sed -n '952,1302p' docs/design/yin.vm.engine.md\\\"\",\"aggregated_output\":\"### E.1 The machine-state-as-data boundary and synchronization protocol\\n\\nA guest machine's execution state must materialize as plain host-readable\\ndata at every service boundary; the host never reaches into guest-private\\nstorage. Each task owns one imported, non-shared wasm32 linear-memory arena\\nas a private execution cache; separate tasks receive separate arenas even\\nwhen compiled Module objects are cached. A supplied `coremem` can implement\\nthe same interface only with task isolation and allocation ownership; shared\\nmutable guest state is not introduced.\\n\\nThe public CLJS VM remains a persistent record with ordinary map keys, not a\\ntoken that requires callers to use `read-state`. A native embedder exposes\\nthe equivalent immutable named-field state and explicit snapshot replacement;\\nit need not implement Clojure records, but must preserve their transition\\nsemantics. The `yin.vm.engine.host.v1` ABI (§E.3) serializes these snapshots\\nand checked value references. Treat linear memory as a private execution\\ncache. The guest-agnostic synchronization protocol across every host/guest\\nseam is:\\n\\n1. **Public entry / host-to-guest:** `step`, `run`, and restore accept the supplied state map as authoritative. Import the guest's declared machine-state fields (for the register machine's eight-field instance — `:pc`, `:registers`, `:frames`, `:continuation`, `:store-of`, `:value`, `:halted?`, and `:blocked?` — see [`yin.vm.debruijn.register.md`](./yin.vm.debruijn.register.md) §10.1), plus the current code layout and engine state. W1 imports unconditionally. A later cache may skip only after proving all relevant keys agree with its last materialized snapshot; a retained private token alone is not proof. In particular, externally `assoc`-modified state (such as `(assoc parked :pc 2 :halted? false)`) must execute the requested state. Import a copied or privately owned active state shadow so running an old or externally modified map never mutates another returned map.\\n2. **Guest-to-host:** before every state-consuming `engine/*` call, including `handle-effect`, pinning, collection, scheduler, park/resume and module work, materialize all of the guest's declared execution fields onto the VM record. Materialize them also at every public `step`, `run`, restore or inspection return. Keep the record's protocol implementations so `module/gc-roots` can inspect the current map. Dense memory synchronization without map synchronization is insufficient.\\n3. **Host completion:** the returned host state is the new authority, including its execution fields and every extension key. Apply only the current operation's specified post-service changes to that returned state, then reimport it into memory. Never overlay a pre-service header onto it. In particular, `handle-effect` sets `:value` even for nonblocking effects; preserve that value, resource/heap/GC updates, stores, counters, queues and custody fields. A plain non-tail primitive call, by contrast, only updates the guest's destination/program counter in the kernel and does not automatically set `:value`.\\n4. **Seam site coordinate:** before a host service, the map contains the instruction's absolute site coordinate (`:pc`), unless the source transition explicitly advances it first (such as parking in the FFI path). Capture the boundary payload at the site; apply the operation's coordinate update exactly once after service completion. Mailbox state does not count as a completed guest instruction.\\n\\nThis synchronization barrier is also a GC safety requirement: `:cell/new` may\\ncollect *inside* `handle-effect`, before the adapter receives its result. At\\nthat moment map-based execution fields (`:value` and the guest's active\\nregisters/frames/continuation) must already be current. Reuse the engine's\\nexact logical heap tracing rules; arena retention of a host value cannot\\nsubstitute for marking the heap cells that value references.\\n\\nThe program store is logically an immutable map, usually name-keyed but **not\\nrestricted to names**: `:store-get`/`:store-put` accept `:data` keys,\\nincluding keywords and numbers in the corpus. Its linear-memory\\nrepresentation is a root `ValueRef` to the embedder's persistent map, not a\\nsecond wasm hash map; the map and updates are addressable through ABI value\\ndefinitions/state messages on every runtime. All writes go through\\n`engine/put-active` → `engine/store-put`. Non-nil `store-of` selects only\\nthat module's store, with no ambient fallback. Private `:resources` and heap\\nare distinct from the program store.\\n\\nThe engine state roots retained across linear memory and host synchronization\\ninclude `ValueRef`s/tokens for `store`, `module-stores`, `free-env`,\\n`primitives`, `modules`, `wait-set`, `ready-queue`, `parked`, `resources`,\\n`heap`/`gc`, `owner`, `origin`/`ancestry`, `id-counter`, `:yin.k/gate`,\\n`:yin.k/issued`, and `:yin.k/closes`, preserving all other extension keys as\\nwell. The actual issue-counter key is `:yin.k/issued`, distinct from\\n`id-counter`; do not invent `:yin.k/next-issue`. These are state/arena\\nretention roots, not an expansion of the engine's logical heap-GC root set.\\nThe full existing state map is authoritative for engine extension keys.\\n\\n### E.2 The value arena, handle discipline, and three encoding domains\\n\\n#### E.2.1 Task-owned value arena, `i32` handles, and the native `handle↔Value` facade\\n\\nGuest execution operates on **`i32` `ValueRef` handles**, not NaN boxing and\\nnot `externref` registers. Reserve `0 = nil`, `1 = false`, `2 = true`; all\\nother values use checked arena indices. Every integer, float, string, symbol,\\nkeyword, collection, primitive, closure, continuation, and sealed\\nstream/cursor reference is an arena entry. Numeric computation initially\\nremains in embedder primitive services: the CLJS adapter uses existing\\nimplementations, and the native service implements the same declared\\nprimitive profiles, integer carriers, float behavior, equality and metadata\\nsemantics. W0 codec/profile fixtures prohibit substituting Rust or JS default\\nnumeric/coercion rules. Zero, empty strings, and empty collections remain\\ntruthy: only references `0` and `1` are false.\\n\\nEach embedder owns an explicit per-task arena, not a process global. Linear\\nmemory contains a parallel descriptor table: kind, generation, opaque\\nhost-slot and optional machine-record offset. The slot is a task-local\\nnumeric identity, never a JS object address or native pointer. All service\\nexchanges serialize plain-data messages in linear memory under the\\n`yin.vm.engine.host.v1` ABI (§E.3), including in Node; there is no privileged\\ndirect-object fast path. Known immutable arena values may be referenced by\\nchecked task-local ids in those messages, with definitions transmitted on\\nfirst use.\\n\\nCLJS reconstructs its actual values and uses `values/closure`,\\n`values/continuation` and typed effects. The native service uses the Rust\\ndesign's `yin-value` `Value` consumed by `yin-kernel`: the fixed 16-byte\\n`#[repr(C, u8)]` enum with safe-Rust `Rc` ownership, explicitly not NaN-boxed\\n(K-D2; `docs/design/yin.vm.rust-kernel.md:324–392, 1238–1244`). Its equality,\\nhashing and opaque printing follow that shared implementation. Trusted kernel\\nconstructors mint closure/continuation identities; literal maps remain data\\nregardless of keys. Native primitive/effect capabilities are supplied\\nexplicitly by the composition, never encoded as function pointers.\\nPointer/handle equality is not guest equality. Round-trip tests include\\nmetadata, keyword versus symbol, exact integers, float bit representations,\\nnested collections and trusted wrappers.\\n\\nStream and cursor *references* are sealed plain data, not host types in\\n`yin.vm.values`. Their underlying stream handles and mutable cursor resources\\nstay in `:resources`. `:make-stream`, primitive functions, and bridge\\nhandlers are composition services, never serialized guest values. Any\\nreturned effect must pass `module/effect?` and `engine/check-callee-effect!`;\\na map shaped like an effect is not dispatched.\\n\\nThe native **`handle↔Value` facade** is an explicit adapter layer, not a\\nreplacement `Value` representation. Each task owns an arena of those\\n`yin-value` `Value`s; `i32` handles index its entries, with `0`/`1`/`2`\\nresolving to `Nil`/`false`/`true`. Lookup checks task ownership, bounds, kind\\nand generation before calling safe kernel/engine APIs. Allocation/retention\\nclones `Value`s through their safe `Clone`/`Drop` implementation; arena\\nrelease drops them there. Neither Rust pointers, enum bytes, `Rc`\\npayloads/counts nor a borrowed `Machine` address appear in wasm memory or\\nmessages. Generation/no-reuse rules are exactly §E.2.3. Machine-local\\n`Value`s/arenas are not `Send`/`Sync`; immutable admitted `Image`s retain\\nplain owned literals and `Arc` sharing, materializing constants per task\\n(`docs/design/yin.vm.rust-kernel.md:394–432, 553–563`). Stream/cursor/cell\\nreferences remain ordinary sealed `Map` `Value`s, with actual handles in\\ncomposition resources.\\n\\nThe facade inherits the determinism rules of §E.3.3 (r5 §4.1.1) **unchanged**:\\norder by recursively expanded canonical encoded value bytes before handle\\ncompression, never arena indices; canonical float64 NaN `0x7ff8000000000000`\\nwhile preserving integral-float carriers and `-0.0`; and one outer TLV\\npayload-length framing with no alternative collection counts. It does not\\ncopy Rust discriminants onto the wire. Rust's `Double` implements the wire's\\nFloat64 semantic class; “Float64 variant” denotes that class, not a newly\\ninvented native enum variant. The three encoding domains stay separate.\\n\\n#### E.2.2 Three encoding domains and mandatory native hashing\\n\\nThe shared Rust crates must implement **three distinct codecs**, not\\nsubstitute the convenient ABI transport encoding for repository identity\\nbytes:\\n\\n| Domain | Existing authority / exact obligation | Observable products |\\n|---|---|---|\\n| Private import messages | §E.3.3 (r5 §4.1.1) TLV codec, reference expansion, framing and NaN rules | ABI request/response bytes only; not code identities or seals. |\\n| Executable register scalar/image encoding | `src/cljc/yin/vm/debruijn_code.cljc` `scalar-class`, `encode-scalar`, and `framed`; `src/cljc/yin/vm/debruijn_register_code.cljc` `encode-register-image`, `descriptor-hash`, `register-hash` | R for empty/base/attached/composed images, layout slice checks and continuation self-consistency. |\\n| Jing canonical content encoding | `src/cljc/dao/jing/cbor.cljc` `encode`/`decode` and its existing corpus; `src/cljc/dao/jing.cljc` `canonical-bytes`, `content-hash`, `segment-key` | Owner tags, reference seals, primitive-profile addresses, module/semantic addresses and existing content admission. |\\n\\nPort executable framing exactly: class tags and eight-hex-digit **UTF-8 byte\\nlength** prefix content; some content is hex-encoded numeric bits, while\\nstring content is the actual string. `jing/blake3` hashes the UTF-8 bytes of\\nthe resulting **string**, not hex-decoded bytes. R is BLAKE3 of the string\\nconcatenation of descriptor-hash text and encoded-register-image text\\n(`debruijn_register_code.cljc:258–271`). Do not replace it with a CBOR hash\\nor hash the ABI node bytes. Preserve descriptor material, body order, tuple\\noperand order, exact scalar classification, no NFC, unsupported-value rules,\\nnumeric endian rules and canonical-key collision refusals\\n(`debruijn_code.cljc:198–203, 224–242, 350–378, 410–465`). The native backend\\nmatches the admitted CLJS scalar profile where that host rejects JVM-only\\nscalar classes; broader Jing integer support does not silently widen\\nregister-image admission.\\n\\nPort Jing's actual canonical CBOR profile, not a generic library's defaults:\\nkeyword/symbol/list tags, exact integer/bignum, float64 carriers,\\ndecimal/rational forms, collection ordering, metadata stripping/validation,\\nequality-collapse and duplicate checks, depth limits, surrogate refusals and\\ndecode/re-encode identity. The private ABI may carry a lone UTF-16 surrogate\\nfor in-machine fidelity; that does not make it hashable by either repository\\ncodec. Keep that domain distinction and the existing qualified refusal.\\n`jing/content-hash` digests canonical CBOR bytes directly and returns\\nlowercase hex; `jing/segment-key` applies the exact\\nnamespace/algorithm/digest spelling. The default here is BLAKE3; any\\nadvertised alternative algorithm must reproduce the repository registry's\\nbehavior rather than aliasing BLAKE3.\\n\\nRequired formulas are the actual inputs in source: owner = content-hash of\\n`[:yin.k/owner secret]` (nil secret → nil owner); seal = content-hash of\\n`[:yin.k/seal secret kind id]` with `issue-ref`'s missing-secret refusal;\\nprimitive profile = content-hash of the exact description in\\n`vm/primitive-profile`, followed by the `:yin.k.pp/blake3-…` keyword\\nconstruction; module address = segment-key of the manifest. Preserve A's\\nexisting canonical construction as well. Equal test secrets, identities and\\ninput data must yield **bit-identical** R, owner, seal, profile and address\\nacross CLJS and Rust. Do not normalize these fields away or let the native\\nservice trust supplied digest strings instead of recomputing them.\\n\\nAssign executable scalar encoding to `yin-scalar`, image/descriptor/R and\\nadmission to `yin-image`, Clojure values/equality to `yin-value`, primitive\\nprofiles to `yin-prims` with engine composition, and Jing payload/content\\nserialization to `yin-wire` using the canonical Jing implementation. The\\nfacade alone owns private ABI framing/reference expansion; it calls these\\ncrates for identity bytes and hashes. These are the crate responsibilities in\\n`docs/design/yin.vm.rust-kernel.md:1136–1145`, extended for the current\\ncontracts; never duplicate codec ports inside each runtime wrapper. W0\\nports/verifies these codecs and hashing primitives before native execution\\ngates. Reuse `test/resources/dao/jing/cbor-v1.json` and its README/errata plus\\n`test/dao/jing/cbor_conformance_test.cljc`,\\n`test/dao/jing/hash_registry_contract_test.cljc`,\\n`test/yin/vm/debruijn_code_test.cljc` and\\n`test/yin/vm/debruijn_register_code_test.cljc`; do not regenerate existing\\ngoldens. Add cross-embedder vectors for descriptor-hash/R (including empty and\\nconcatenated images), exact encoded bytes, owner/seal/profile inputs and\\nmanifest addresses. W1 verifies load/primitive profiles, W2 recomputes\\ncontinuation hashes, W3 checks every minted reference seal, and W4 checks\\ncompose/attach/layout/module addresses and received slices. These are native\\nservice obligations independent of the ABI codec implementation.\\n\\n#### E.2.3 Arena collection, GC root discipline, and snapshot isolation\\n\\nNo collection runs while a guest holds unreported local values. Initially run\\narena collection only after full materialization at an exit; engine heap\\ncollection may run inside a host service after the mandatory map barrier in\\n§E.1; later dirty-slot spilling can replace write-through with a separate\\nGC/inspection barrier. Mark the guest's materialized execution fields (for\\nregister machines, see [`yin.vm.debruijn.register.md`](./yin.vm.debruijn.register.md)\\n§10.1.2), `value`, `parked`/`wait-set`/`ready-queue` entries, `store`,\\n`module-stores`, engine heap and pinned/outgoing values, constants, and\\npending mailbox arguments. Traverse trusted wrappers via `values/payload` and\\npreserve the kernel's `module/gc-children` implementation and its treatment\\nof code. Integrate `engine/pin-refs` for arguments leaving through FFI. Keep\\nembedder arena ownership and the existing logical heap GC distinct, with\\nexplicit roots connecting them. Reclaim only unreachable implementation\\nstorage, not stream history or persistent datoms. Avoid handle reuse\\ninitially; later reuse needs generations checked at every host import.\\n\\nExternal state inspection produces immutable host snapshots. Resuming one\\nsnapshot cannot mutate another snapshot's frames, values, or state fields.\\nPersistent snapshots share immutable arena objects, never a mutable guest\\nexecution buffer. The first implementation may copy the active state shadow\\nat every public IVM transition; optimize only after snapshot-alias tests pass.\\n\\n### E.3 Service protocol: `yin.vm.engine.host.v1`\\n#### E.4.3 Deterministic service traversal prerequisite\\n\\nSource verification found real unsorted map/set walks that can mint ids or\\nchoose diagnostics. Rust must not reproduce them with its default map order,\\nand canonicalizing only the final ABI bytes is too late. Introduce an\\nexplicit **shared deterministic traversal policy in the CLJC helpers and the\\nnative service**, then use that revised reference for wasm parity. This is a\\nplanned semantic stabilization of previously host-order-dependent behavior,\\nnot an already satisfied property of current source and not a wasm-only\\npatch. The traversal stabilization is planned work, not an implemented\\nproperty.\\n\\nFor currently unordered traversal sites use a pure order key: for ordinary\\nmachine values, the fully expanded canonical ABI value bytes defined in\\n§E.3.3 (r5 §4.1.1); for trusted values, their non-minting structural\\ndescriptors. Sort maps by key and sets by element, using unsigned\\nlexicographic comparison, never arena numbering, hash-table iteration or the\\noutput of a resource-lifting encoder. Build keys for a collection before\\ninvoking any id-minting traversal of that collection. Retain vector/list\\norder, ordered exports, wait/ready queue order and first-occurrence\\ndeduplication. Retain **existing explicit source orderings** such as\\n`sort-by str` on cursor ids/module-store addresses and `sort-by key` on\\nattached image identities, with cross-host fixtures for those comparators; do\\nnot replace them with ABI order under the guise of fixing unordered maps.\\n\\n| Site (existing symbols in `engine.cljc` unless qualified) | Required shared traversal change / preserved order |\\n|---|---|\\n| `encoder`, `encode-store` | Sort raw literal-map/store keys before visiting key then value; sort sets before recursively encoding. Never sort the already encoded output: `cell-for!` has already minted `:yin.k/c-N` by then. Keep vector/list and explicit export sequence order. |\\n| `markers` | Maintain pre-order tree walk; for maps visit values in sorted-key order (preserve the source's values-only walk), for sets sort elements, for vectors/lists preserve sequence order. No incidental host map order may determine first stream discovery. |\\n| `lower-resources` | Preserve sorted used-cursor ids and the direct-stream-marker pass followed by cursor-derived streams; consume canonically traversed marker sequences, deduplicate by stream identity at first encounter, then mint gensym ids. Do not reorder queues or change aliasing of repeated cursor cells. |\\n| `receive-module` | Keep its explicit sorted image attach order. Enumerate slice values and fresh-store values in sorted-key order before `lower-resources`; use the same order for bindings/store decoding and deterministic error selection. Preserve slice-before-fresh-store phase order and first-link-wins stores. |\\n| `module/module-entries`, `module/list-modules` (`module.cljc`) | Walk registry child maps in sorted-key order and return top-level module names in that order. Missing-module `:available` vectors and registry consumers no longer inherit host map iteration. |\\n| `resume-from-run-queue` | Keep FIFO entry selection and terminal-check ordering; sort resource-update keys before selecting the first non-keyword diagnostic. Preserve updates and merge semantics. |\\n| `lift-slice`, `decode-store` and adjacent consumers | Preserve ordered export input; if a collection is a set, sort before visitation. Audit downstream unordered traversal for id minting, first-error choice or observable vectors, and route those paths through this policy. |\\n\\nThe helpers named above exist; the ordering policy/helper itself is proposed\\nimplementation work. It must land with shared CLJC/native regression tests\\nbefore the corresponding W3/W4 acceptance gate. This deliberately can change\\nold host-specific `c-N` assignments, stream ids/seals, slice-entry vectors or\\ndiagnostic order; record those deltas as the named prerequisite, never\\nnormalize them out. A fixed CLJS-only historical map order is not a\\ncross-runtime contract. Existing canonical R/Jing hash algorithms and frozen\\nbyte corpora remain unchanged.\\n\\nW0 inventories unordered observation sites and freezes order-key fixtures. W3\\nimplements resource-update diagnostic order and validates counters/seals; W4\\ncompletes module lift/lower ordering and tests equal maps built with different\\ninsertion histories, small and large maps spanning CLJS map representations,\\nnested maps/sets, repeated stream/cursor refs, multi-store modules and\\nmultiple invalid update keys. Compare exact minted ids, seals,\\n`:yin.k/entries`, `:cells`, `:available`, counters and resource maps. A parity\\nfailure here blocks the slice; it cannot be hidden as implementation-private\\narena variation. The old Rust K-phase restriction on CLJC edits remains\\ndistinct from this explicit wasm-driven shared-reference prerequisite: version\\nthe kit after that stabilization and retain the original goldens/history, as\\nspecified in §E.4.2 and [`yang.wasm.md`](./yang.wasm.md) §1.1's Rust-authority\\nreconciliation; do not silently choose Rust map order or normalize away\\nchanged guest-visible ids.\\n\\n#### E.4.4 Stream, park, custody-gate, FFI two-step, and `spawn-module` host rules\\n\\n**Stream and park transitions:** Stream `make`/`cursor`/`close` finish\\nwithout suspension under the current contract. `put`-full and `next`-blocked\\ncreate per-site wait entries using the guest's site builders. Advance the\\nguest site coordinate once, set `blocked` true / `halted` false / `value`\\n`:yin/blocked`, and return to the scheduler. Explicit `park` instead produces\\nthe parked record as `value` and sets `halted` true; it is not automatically\\nblocked and has no `wait-set` entry. The scheduler may still have other ready\\nwork. Resume from a ready entry uses `engine/resume-from-run-queue`: pop FIFO\\nentry, validate `resource-updates` keys, merge updates into `:resources`,\\nclear flags, and handle terminal wake diagnostics before adapter restore. The\\nsame errors must occur whether a stream failed immediately or after blocking.\\nExported entries contain no callbacks, raw stream handles, timers, or stale\\nservice-mailbox data.\\n\\n**Custody gates:** Retain `:yin.k/gate`, `:yin.k/issued`, `:yin.k/closes`,\\nentry issue numbers, and every extension key across synchronization and\\npublic map edits. Use `vm/gate-mode` and existing engine/FFI/module handlers;\\ndo not replace them with one generic “gate means error” switch:\\n- Any gate suppresses `ffi/put-request` append and returns `full`, so FFI retains its request as a `request-sent` writer. Link misses likewise park as `link-request` without minting a response cursor or appending; already-resolved module and install-join branches still follow the handler's ordering.\\n- Any gate suppresses autonomous link retry/response scans, FFI response polling, and `put`/`next` retries in `check-wait-set`. Gated install children are stamped/stepped only in `running` mode; ungated scheduling remains unchanged.\\n- `running` stream cursor creation creates the sealed reference and unminted resource cell without a stream cursor call. `exporting`/`ended` immediate observation paths refuse through the existing gate checks.\\n- `running` `put` uses the engine's deferred observation path and stamps a blocked writer with `:yin.k/issue`, advancing `:yin.k/issued` independently of `id-counter`. `running` blocked poll becomes `:reason :observe, :op :poll`, using the call-site `next` builder fallback; its application remains the driver's operation.\\n- **Current source nuance:** `running` stream close queues a record in `:yin.k/closes`, increments the issue counter, returns `nil` and does not call the stream handle. `exporting`/`ended` close refuses.\\n\\n**FFI two-step, without duplicate sends:**\\n1. `ffi/require-call-pair!` runs before allocating an id or parking. Read args in vector order, pin references, build the boundary payload, derive `ffi/call-id` from task identity and `park-id`, and park under that correlated id. Increment the shared `id-counter` once through park.\\n2. Build `apply2/request` and invoke `ffi/put-request`. On `ok`, enqueue `response-wait-entry`. On `full`, enqueue payload plus `request-sent` true, `call-id`, `op`, `reason` `:put`, call-in stream id and the exact request datom. Other outcomes preserve the existing qualified error. Under any custody gate `put-request` returns `full` without appending; retain the request for the driver rather than retrying it autonomously.\\n3. The ungated engine retries a full writer. Its wake means the request append completed: restore converts it to a response reader, strips stale wake/writer fields through `ffi/response-wait-entry`, and stays blocked. It must neither append the request again nor advance the guest beyond the pending result.\\n4. On correlated response, terminal response-loss handling runs first, then `call-result` unwraps success/error, parked `call-id` is removed, and the guest destination or tail return receives the result exactly once. Use constants from `yin.vm` for reserved resources. Keep the bridge in the existing host interpreter; do not implement wasm imports named after arbitrary FFI operations.\\n\\n**`spawn-module` isolation and node-local artifact-cache fallback:**\\n`spawn-module` creates a fresh task/memory/arena/owner/counter/resources\\ncontext, with the composition options copied by the kernel; it never borrows\\nthe parent's mutable execution shadow or per-module store. Under the shared\\nartifact-cache policy (`docs/design/yin.vm.rust-kernel.md:997–1011, 1113–1122,\\n1261–1263`; [`yang.wasm.md`](./yang.wasm.md) §5;\\n[`yin.vm.debruijn.register.md`](./yin.vm.debruijn.register.md) §10.3), compiled\\nwasm binaries are disposable **node-local caches**, never `dao.jing` content\\nidentities, refs, continuation fields or portable module payloads. A missing\\nor dropped artifact falls back to the same host's interpreter at any\\nmaterialized boundary, with no pending mutation or active guest stack.\\n\\n### E.5 Conformance method, guest-agnostic acceptance gates, toolchain pins, non-goals, risks, and open items\\n\\n*(Owner amendment note, 2026-10-19: By explicit owner ruling, the\\nconformance **method** previously housed in `docs/design/yin.vm.conformance-kit.md`\\n(`§1–§5`, `§8`) is folded into this section as the acceptance specification of\\nthe engine execution host; the register-specific fixture schema and register\\nW0–W6 / Rust K1–K5 lane gates live in\\n[`yin.vm.debruijn.register.md`](./yin.vm.debruijn.register.md) `§10.4–§10.5`.)*\\n\\n#### E.5.1 Oracle export, differential execution lanes, and trace comparison method\\n\\nThe K0/W0 conformance kit is the single **shared deliverable** of\\n[`yin.vm.rust-kernel.md`](./yin.vm.rust-kernel.md), this specification\\n(`§E.1–§E.5`), [`yang.wasm.md`](./yang.wasm.md), and\\n[`yin.vm.debruijn.register.md`](./yin.vm.debruijn.register.md) (`§10`). It is\\nexported from R4 and extended for wasm lowering, `yin.vm.engine.host.v1`\\ntransport, and runtime conformance, not a second reference interpreter or a\\nsecond kit (`docs/design/yin.vm.rust-kernel.md:1185–1199`, equivalence law at\\n`:805–831`). The execution specifications retain their respective\\nauthorization boundaries; the planned LLVM lane remains behind K3 owner review\\nbefore K4. W0–W6 are the wasm slices; K0–K5 are the Rust design's phases. No\\nnew fixture semantics are introduced. For the register machine's exported\\nfixture schema (canonical images, R, B0-normalized results/errors, format-level\\nboundary traces/K depth/saved pairs, 14 validator refusal rules, descriptor/scalar\\nvectors, diagnostic messages, refused-step atomicity, 7.3 tail-site table,\\n`>65,535`-register, host-limit, and million-iteration tail fixtures), see\\n[`yin.vm.debruijn.register.md`](./yin.vm.debruijn.register.md) `§10.4`. If K0\\nexports are not yet available, create that shared deliverable once; do not\\nfabricate a wasm-only reference interpreter. Record the actual reference\\nrevision and fixture version, including the explicit shared traversal\\nstabilization delta (§E.4.3).\\n\\nOne manifest drives **three execution lanes: R4 CLJS reference, Rust\\nkernel/interpreter, wasm**. Wasm runs the same bytes under Node and native\\nruntimes as gated below; all use the same scripted inputs, scalar/host-limit\\ndeclarations and expected refusals. ABI-specific probes extend K0 as a\\ntransport test group without replacing format-level fixtures. Rust interpreter\\ncoverage advances with each W slice; it cannot be omitted because native wasm\\nhappens to pass. B0 results and complete boundary traces remain shared; wasm\\nsingle-step traces add finer evidence, not a second oracle.\\n\\nBuild an explicit differential adapter rather than replacing the existing\\ntests' private `register-run` vars by global mutation. Parameterize/extract\\nshared fixtures when implementation is authorized. Each case receives\\nindependent scripted streams with identical inputs, capability secret,\\nprimitive registry, origin and counter setup. From W1 onward, compile each\\nimage once and run those identical wasm bytes under **Node and Wasmtime**,\\neach against interpreted register after every guest instruction and scheduler\\nevent; Wasmer must also pass the complete suite by W5. Compare serialized ABI\\ntraffic as well as full semantic traces; also compare semantic execution where\\nthe existing A→R fragment permits it.\\n\\nThe generic per-fixture procedure, applied to every lane against the oracle\\nexports: project the guest's admitted source to its canonical form and assert the\\nliteral goldens; derive and validate the lowerings; compile and instantiate per\\nlane; execute with the deterministic declared bindings (the guest specification\\nnames them) and the genuine parked targets its resume cases require; execute every\\nconditional arm where applicable; and compare the same qualified refusal where a\\ncase is unsupported. Never silently skip resume, terminal, or closure cases, and\\nnever relabel unsupported cases as successes. B0 normalization alone is\\ninsufficient for backend acceptance: the guest specification's raw observable\\nfields are compared in addition, as enumerated by the guest fixture specification\\n(for the register machine, [`yin.vm.debruijn.register.md`](./yin.vm.debruijn.register.md)\\n§10.4). Compare resources by\\nauthenticated logical identity/cursor progress and stream emissions, not by\\nhost object address. With identical secrets and inputs,\\nhash/owner/seal/profile/address strings, guest-visible ids and slice ordering\\nmust be bit-identical. Compare canonical expanded observable-state encodings,\\nnot pointer layouts; private arena numbers may be renamed only where they\\ncannot affect key ordering, id minting or guest state. Across independent\\ntasks use the same explicit owner inputs; never erase owner checks. Test\\nprimitive implementations and scripted transports exist in both native and\\nCLJS compositions with the same declared profiles; a JS function fixture must\\nget an equivalent native fixture, not disappear from the native run. Normalize\\nonly private memory addresses, arena numbering and runtime object identities;\\npreserve all guest values, errors, `pc`s, frames, snapshots, queues, counters,\\nstore/heap/custody changes, resource progress and emitted request order.\\nCompare failures by qualified rule/reason/outcome and relevant message\\nassertions.\\n\\nThe planned LLVM lane uses this same kit under Rust §7.2: interpreted and\\nnative runs compare boundary states, ordered emissions and refusal\\ndiagnostics. Artifact-drop fallback continues interpreted from the same\\nboundary. Wasm adds wasm→Rust-interpreter and wasm→CLJS-interpreter fallback\\nfixtures. No backend forks the validator, live-set rules or payload\\nvocabulary. K3 may measure wasm on the same harness; a wasm result does not\\ncommission K4.\\n\\n#### E.5.2 Cross-embedder encoding, hash, and traversal determinism gates\\n\\nAcross every embedder (Node/CLJS, Wasmtime, Wasmer) and the shared Rust\\ncrates, the conformance vectors enforce the three encoding domains (§E.2.2),\\nthe private `yin.vm.engine.host.v1` ABI (§E.3), and the shared deterministic\\ntraversal prerequisite (§E.4.3):\\n- **Three-domain hash and byte parity (§E.2.2):** Equal test secrets,\\n  identities, and inputs yield **bit-identical** R, owner, seal,\\n  primitive-profile, and manifest addresses across CLJS and Rust. Reuse\\n  `test/resources/dao/jing/cbor-v1.json` and its README/errata plus\\n  `test/dao/jing/cbor_conformance_test.cljc`,\\n  `test/dao/jing/hash_registry_contract_test.cljc`,\\n  `test/yin/vm/debruijn_code_test.cljc`, and\\n  `test/yin/vm/debruijn_register_code_test.cljc` without regenerating existing\\n  goldens; add cross-embedder vectors for `descriptor-hash`/R (including empty\\n  and concatenated images), exact encoded bytes, owner/seal/profile inputs,\\n  and manifest addresses. W1 verifies load/primitive profiles, W2 recomputes\\n  continuation hashes, W3 checks every minted reference seal, and W4 checks\\n  compose/attach/layout/module addresses and received slices.\\n- **`yin.vm.engine.host.v1` TLV and ordering conformance (§E.3):** Map keys\\n  and set elements sort by fully expanded canonical value bytes before handle\\n  compression, never by arena-reference bytes; float64 NaN is\\n  `0x7ff8000000000000`, with integral floats and `-0.0` preserved by their\\n  carriers; each node has exactly one outer\\n  `u8 tag | u32 payload-byte-length (LE) | payload` frame, with no inner\\n  counts. Duplicate/colliding entries and malformed nodes refuse. Task-local\\n  references and trusted descriptors do not confer portable value authority.\\n  Native handles resolve through the safe shared `Value` facade, not a\\n  separate codec/engine port.\\n- **Deterministic service traversal gates (§E.4.3):** Identical minted\\n  `:yin.k/c-N` cell ids, stream/cursor ids and seals, `:yin.k/entries`,\\n  `:cells`, `:available` vectors, counters, resource maps, and\\n  resource-update diagnostic order across equal maps built with different\\n  insertion histories, small and large maps spanning CLJS map representations,\\n  nested maps/sets, repeated stream/cursor refs, multi-store modules, and\\n  multiple invalid update keys.\\n\\n#### E.5.3 W0 baseline toolchain pins, feature probes, and transport/snapshot gates\\n\\nDeliver/extend K0's single exported fixture kit and its CLJS,\\nRust-interpreter, and wasm adapters, the core-profile feature probe, pinned\\nassembler/validator, ABI codec/schema, and operation-by-operation conformance\\nfixtures. The following are **exact baseline pins** for W0, not “latest”\\nconstraints or a claim of an already working build:\\n\\n| Tool | Pin / scope | Evidence |\\n|---|---|---|\\n| Node | `25.6.1` | Existing `mise.toml` node pin; keep the repository selection. |\\n| Clojure CLI / shadow-cljs | `1.12.4.1602` / `3.3.5` | Existing `mise.toml` and `deps.edn` selections. JVM emitter uses the repository Java 21 lane; record resolved vendor/build in the W0 manifest. |\\n| WABT | `1.0.37` | [Release](https://github.com/WebAssembly/wabt/releases/tag/1.0.37); assemble and validate with an explicit baseline feature allowlist. |\\n| Wasmtime native crate/runtime | `=38.0.0` | [Versioned embedding API](https://docs.rs/wasmtime/38.0.0/wasmtime/); required native runtime from W1. |\\n| Wasmer native runtime | `6.0.1` | [Release](https://github.com/wasmerio/wasmer/releases/tag/v6.0.1); second wrapper and completion gate by W5. |\\n| Rust / Cargo | `1.90.0` toolchain | [Release](https://blog.rust-lang.org/2025/09/18/Rust-1.90.0/); host service/wrapper builds, not guest wasm generation. |\\n\\nW0 must build these pins together, freeze transitive native dependencies in a\\nlockfile, record checksums and target triples, and run the ABI smoke tests.\\nThey are reproducibility starting points, not assertions that all engines'\\nlater features work. If a compatibility or security issue requires a pin\\nchange, document the exact replacement and rerun all baseline probes; no\\nsilent floating version. Binaryen is absent from the baseline toolchain, and\\nany later introduction needs its own pin/profile gate. Browser engine/version\\nis recorded when its adapter is qualified; browser API convenience never\\nexpands the baseline.\\n\\n**Feature probes:** Probe imported memory, byte-order/bounds behavior,\\nsingle-result `i32` imports, scalar `i64` memory fields, mutable globals,\\nactive segments, table installation/`call_indirect`, memory growth/view\\nrefresh, and multiple image instances sharing one task memory. Explicitly\\nreject every opcode/type outside the baseline allowlist, even when an engine\\nenables extensions by default. Test same-binary import/export signatures on\\nNode, Wasmtime and Wasmer with no WASI imports. Native custom imports require\\nthe embedding harness; invoking a bare runtime CLI with no imports is not this\\ntest. Freeze deterministic fixture manifests. No new guest semantics. Gate\\nevery nonempty image before codegen. Separate native payload transport from\\nlinker export capability.\\n\\n**W0 baseline, snapshot, codec, and transport acceptance:**\\n- **Baseline and snapshot isolation:** All semantic goldens retain their\\n  literal vector/address; all derived images retain R and exact live operands.\\n  Existing register tests pass unchanged on the reference lane. Invalid\\n  contract, Rule R, unknown opcode, cross-body target, bad live set and\\n  empty-image cases have identical admission outcomes. Validate a minimal wasm\\n  instance and explicit resource-limit diagnostics. Establish matching-layout\\n  payload transport and qualified refusal for incompatible layouts. Freeze the\\n  map/memory authority protocol (§E.1) and its byte-message equivalents,\\n  including external `assoc`/snapshot replacement, old-state replay (resuming\\n  one snapshot never mutates another snapshot's frames, values, or state\\n  fields) and preservation of unknown engine extension keys; do not defer that\\n  contract to performance tuning.\\n- **Additional W0 codec acceptance:** Run all three codec domains against\\n  frozen bytes and hashes before executing native register images. Include\\n  map/set canonical collisions, metadata, numeric carriers, surrogate\\n  rejection in repository codecs versus private ABI preservation, deep\\n  compound referenced keys with permuted arena ids, canonical NaN and\\n  float/integer distinction, and exact outer-only framing (odd text lengths,\\n  odd map node count, truncated/extra children). Keep private message-codec\\n  and repository hash failures separately diagnosable.\\n- **W0 transport acceptance:** Force small/zero response capacity:\\n  `COMPLETE→NEED_SPACE→fetch-response`, repeated fetch,\\n  acknowledgement/release, wrong sequence/task, repeated sequence with changed\\n  content, `BAD_MESSAGE`, `ABI_MISMATCH` and `RESOURCE_LIMIT`. Validate\\n  bounds/overlap/overflow and confirm malformed/unsupported requests do not\\n  execute a semantic transition. A counter-only test service proves that\\n  retained responses do not re-execute the action. These tests run on Node and\\n  native harnesses during W0, not first in W5.\\n\\n**W/K dependency map:** W0 consumes/extends K0; W1 shares K1 pure\\nkernel/value/image work; W2 shares K2 payload/restore work plus the required\\n`yin-engine` park subset; W3–W4 require the Level-2 library services\\ndescribed in Rust §6.3, not a second engine. W5 is wasm release\\nqualification, not certification of the full K5 standalone node. W6 can\\ncontribute wasm measurements to K3's same corpus/harness; K4 still requires\\nthe owner's review of K3\\n(`docs/design/yin.vm.rust-kernel.md:1167–1182, 1215–1228`). No dependency\\nauthorizes unrelated implementation. Register-specific W0–W6 implementation\\nslices, per-slice differential acceptance gates, and retained Rust K1–K5 /\\nLLVM gates live in\\n[`yin.vm.debruijn.register.md`](./yin.vm.debruijn.register.md) `§10.5`.\\n\\n#### E.5.4 Guest-agnostic non-goals and risks\\n\\nNon-goals: no second Rust kernel/engine/value/codec port; no K4 LLVM\\nimplementation, full K5 standalone node, new Rust network transport,\\ncross-node artifact service or Rust `Rc`-layout exposure; no universal\\ncross-model continuation conversion, new portable continuation schema, direct\\narbitrary host FFI imports, WASI imports in the VM core, threads/shared\\nmemory, WasmGC dependency, numeric NaN boxing, a mandatory standalone\\nhost-free runtime for W5, or immediate JVM/Dart application bindings; no\\npromise to execute malicious wasm from the network; no optimization before W5\\ncorrectness.\\n\\nGuest-agnostic host risks (register-specific risks live in\\n[`yin.vm.debruijn.register.md`](./yin.vm.debruijn.register.md) §10.6):\\n- **Host boundary overhead.** A `ValueRef` implementation deliberately leaves semantic services in conforming embedders, with mandatory serialized messages even for CLJS. Measure in W6 whether compiled dispatch helps before moving collection/value/store services into wasm; the native host implementation needed for portability is already required by W1–W4.\\n- **Persistent public state over mutable memory.** Snapshot aliasing and old-state retention are correctness concerns, not just GC details. Decide in W0 whether each public state owns a copy-on-write arena root or a copied active shadow; require old-state replay tests before optimizing.\\n- **Host exceptions and partial effects.** Catch host failures at the adapter boundary for shaped diagnostics, then reproduce the existing public exception behavior. Do not roll back an already appended stream event or retry a completed effect. The mailbox needs a single completion/commit rule.\\n- **Predicate drift.** `machine-data?`, value wrappers, integer carriers and effect authorization have evolved beyond old doc prose. Reuse the source helpers, and make ABI tests fail when their contracts change. External plain-data serialization and internal trusted-wrapper values are separate gates.\\n- **Single-port dependency.** Native coverage depends on the shared Rust crates, including the substantial Level-2 engine subset needed before W3/W4. A faster parallel port is not a workaround. Keep one transition implementation per host language, one exported K0/W0 kit and explicit shared-reference versioning; duplicate dispatchers must not duplicate semantic code. The `Value` facade and snapshot barriers must preserve check-then-commit and `Rc` ownership while keeping handles task-local.\\n- **Host coverage.** Node/CLJS and native Wasmtime/Wasmer are shipping targets under one pinned baseline ABI/profile. Browser and later JVM/Dart application integrations may have different resource limits and must rerun the same parity suite. Native semantic-service drift and ABI codec fidelity are the principal portability risks.\\n\\n#### E.5.5 Reference conventions and open items\\n\\nReference convention: `register.cljc` means\\n`src/cljc/yin/vm/debruijn/register.cljc`; `engine`, `ffi`, `module`,\\n`values`, and `debruijn_register_effects` filenames are under\\n`src/cljc/yin/vm/`; `vm` means `src/cljc/yin/vm.cljc`; `apply2` is the alias\\nfor `dao.stream.apply` at `src/cljc/dao/stream/apply.cljc`. `module/IModuleKernel`\\nand its `gc-roots` method are declared in `module.cljc`. `IVM` `step`/`run`\\nare methods of `yin.vm/IVM`. `halted-with-empty-queue?`, `scheduler-round`,\\n`check-wait-set`, `handle-close` and `collect` are `engine.cljc` functions.\\nThe quoted restore/call helper names are existing source semantics, whereas\\n`exchange`/`fetch-response`, ABI statuses and all native implementation entry\\nnames are proposed protocol vocabulary. Rust-design line references retain\\nr5's coordinates (`1–1334`).\\n\\n**Open items** (retained from r5 without resolution across this specification,\\n[`yang.wasm.md`](./yang.wasm.md), and\\n[`yin.vm.debruijn.register.md`](./yin.vm.debruijn.register.md) `§10.6`):\\n- §E.3.4 (r5 §4.1.1) says guest closure calls stay compiled, while the single-port rule in §E.4.2 (r5 §4.1.1) requires service sites to reuse the owning host's call transition. The division between generated dispatch and shared transition work needs to be made concrete without creating a second semantic implementation (see also [`yang.wasm.md`](./yang.wasm.md) `## Open items`).\\n- W0 retains the transport-test notation `COMPLETE→NEED_SPACE→fetch-response` (§E.5.3). §E.3.2 (r5 §4.1.1) defines `COMPLETE` and `NEED_SPACE` as alternative `exchange` results; the notation's test sequencing needs clarification, not an ABI change.\\n\",\"exit_code\":0,\"status\":\"completed\"}}\n{\"type\":\"item.completed\",\"item\":{\"id\":\"item_8\",\"type\":\"agent_message\",\"text\":\"The stream contract makes one distinction important for the rewrite: `full` is an append outcome, not a generic response-buffer status. I’ll reserve response space before an adapter operation and retain each operation’s existing outcome maps. Parking and scheduler work will stay inside the instance.\\n\"}}\n{\"type\":\"item.started\",\"item\":{\"id\":\"item_9\",\"type\":\"command_execution\",\"command\":\"/bin/zsh -lc \\\"sed -n '1498,1738p' docs/design/yin.vm.debruijn.register.md; sed -n '1000,1055p' docs/design/yin.vm.engine.md; rg -n 'The system ABI|Owner ruling|Effects out' docs/design/datom.world.md; rg -n 'handle.*outcome|:dao.stream/(ok|full)|close\\\"'!'\\\"' docs/design/dao.stream.md | head -35; tail -8 docs/design/yin.vm.rust-kernel.md\\\"\",\"aggregated_output\":\"\",\"exit_code\":null,\"status\":\"in_progress\"}}\n{\"type\":\"item.completed\",\"item\":{\"id\":\"item_9\",\"type\":\"command_execution\",\"command\":\"/bin/zsh -lc \\\"sed -n '1498,1738p' docs/design/yin.vm.debruijn.register.md; sed -n '1000,1055p' docs/design/yin.vm.engine.md; rg -n 'The system ABI|Owner ruling|Effects out' docs/design/datom.world.md; rg -n 'handle.*outcome|:dao.stream/(ok|full)|close\\\"'!'\\\"' docs/design/dao.stream.md | head -35; tail -8 docs/design/yin.vm.rust-kernel.md\\\"\",\"aggregated_output\":\"#### 10.5.2 W1 — Pure register execution and body dispatch\\n\\nRequired runtime gate: Node + Wasmtime, identical image binaries and full\\nstep/ABI traces; provide those services through the shared Rust crates and\\nrequire the Rust interpreter to pass the same pure fixtures. Also test\\nrefused-step atomicity and per-machine fault isolation from K0/K1, including\\nthe wide-register (`>65,535`) and million-tail cases.\\n\\nImplement arena, dense register shadow, body dispatcher,\\nconstants/moves/loads/branches, closures, explicit calls/returns/halt, primitive\\nresults, `store`/`define`/`gensym`, and one-step materialization.\\nEffects/continuation execution remains explicitly unavailable until its slice,\\nrather than silently emulated as a completed wasm feature.\\n\\nAcceptance: register\\nliteral/load-bound/free/store/nested-closure/multi-arg/compose/branch/B0/B2/gensym\\ntests; pure semantic corpus entries including `nested-if-same-rd`,\\n`body-queue-order`, `define-then-call`, and unreachable structural slots. Add\\nnil-fill/extra-drop, zero truthiness, frame shadowing, backward/irreducible\\nvalid CFGs, source/destination aliasing, bounded yields, deep non-tail\\nrecursion and long tail recursion. Assert guest `K` behavior and bounded native\\ncall depth. Preserve snapshot immutability between steps. Assert bit-identical\\nload `R`, owner tags and primitive-profile addresses, plus direct map\\n`pc`/register/frame reads, externally `assoc`-modified entry state, and\\n`:halt`/final-`:return` preservation of the executing `pc`, `:registers`, and\\n`:frames` (including nonempty `K` on `:halt`).\\n\\n#### 10.5.3 W2 — Continuations and `register-restore`\\n\\nRequired runtime gate: Node + Wasmtime full continuation/restore traces, plus\\nnative↔CLJS admissible payload round-trips and identical qualified refusals.\\nImplement native payload/owner/type validation before this gate.\\n\\nImplement sparse payload materialization (§10.2.1), reification, internal\\ncontinuation application, explicit `park`/`resume`, and external\\n`register-restore` (§10.2.2). Use existing validators and engine gates.\\n\\nAcceptance: `current-continuation`, `park-and-resume`, `bytecode-resume`, every\\ntampered/refusal test in `register_test`; semantic corpus `park`/`resume`\\nproductions with valid fixture targets or matching errors. Add nested non-tail\\ncapture then abortive invocation, multi-shot capture, tail `:return-result`,\\nlive Closure versus live primitive distinctions under `machine-data?`,\\nhost-function/error resume refusal, error-shaped data success, dead-slot `nil`\\nrestoration, snapshot alias isolation, and both native\\ninterpreted→wasm/wasm→interpreted payload round-trips. Assert expected linker\\n`:non-canonicalizable` refusals rather than claiming transport support from\\nsame-process tests. Assert native `continuation-defect` recomputes the same\\nrepository `R` on original and tampered images, without trusting the supplied\\nhash or hashing ABI bytes. Add `:return-result` fixtures with empty and\\nnonempty captured `K`, deliberately different `base` `pc`/registers, and exact\\npost-restore `pc`/register/frame assertions. Add an unowned wrong-arity\\ncontinuation case proving `:foreign-value` precedes `:continuation-arity`, and\\nan owned wrong-arity/foreign-format case proving arity precedes format.\\n\\n#### 10.5.4 W3 — Streams, shared scheduler, and FFI\\n\\nRequired runtime gate: Node + Wasmtime full scheduler/event/ABI traces using\\nindependent scripted transports; native scheduler, custody, collection and\\ntwo-step FFI services are required now.\\n\\nImplement typed effect mailbox completion, register site wait builders,\\nscheduler seam, call-effect handling including tail effects\\n([`yang.wasm.md`](./yang.wasm.md) §3.4), bridge wrapper, FFI request/response\\nlogic and pinning.\\n\\nAcceptance: every immediate/blocking stream and bridge success/error test in\\n`register_test`; semantic corpus streams/ffi/park with scheduled events. Add\\nfull-writer two-step and stale-key checks, pending-ready-after-halt,\\nlost/closed stream outcomes, missing call-pair before id allocation,\\ncall-result errors, repeated polling without duplicate append, effect\\npermission checks and resource-update validation. Compare value/store/parking\\nand external emission traces after each wake, not only final values. Exercise\\nthe [`yang.wasm.md`](./yang.wasm.md) §3.4 (r5 §2.4) host-call matrix:\\nimmediate/blocked × tail/non-tail, including `put`/`next`, module\\n`require`/`try-require` builder fallback (full module installation in W4), and\\nrunning `poll`'s `observe` fallback. Assert blocked `pc` advances without `rd`\\nwrite or tail return, and post-effect state mutations survive each completion.\\nCompare immediate empty-`K` tail completion at site `pc` with restored blocked\\ncompletion at entry `pc`.\\n\\nAdd a low-threshold `:cell/new` host-effect test whose only live cell roots\\noccur in newly materialized registers/frames/K, forcing collection inside\\n`handle-effect`; it must retain those cells. Check `:value` after each\\nnonblocking effect and verify a plain non-tail primitive leaves the previous\\nvalue unchanged. Test all supported gate modes\\n(`ungated`/`running`/`exporting`/`ended`), no handle calls for deferred\\nFFI/cursor/put/poll/close/link work, separate issue/id counters, queued running\\ncloses versus exporting/ended refusals, suppressed wait polling, and driver\\napply synchronization. Scope `wait-entry-defect` to its four shapes (§10.2.4);\\nuse payload plus owning-shape checks for `observe`/`link`/`install`. These are\\nW3 correctness gates, not deferred GC optimizations. Assert exact seals on\\nstream/cursor/cell refs with the same secrets and ids, and deterministic\\n`resource-updates` diagnostic order under [`yin.vm.engine.md`](./yin.vm.engine.md)\\n§E.4.3 (r5 §4.1.2).\\n\\nW3 also forces `NEED_SPACE` after real effectful transitions: stream append,\\nFFI request send/retained-writer conversion, gensym/id allocation and\\nparked-state update. Repeated `fetch-response` must return the same response\\nbytes/state revision without a second append, new id, repeated GC transition or\\nrepeated resource update. Verify `BAD_MESSAGE`/`ABI_MISMATCH`/`RESOURCE_LIMIT`\\npaths do not accidentally execute an effect, and semantic error responses obey\\nthe same retained-response protocol. Repeat on Node and Wasmtime; Wasmer\\nrepeats this suite in W4.\\n\\n#### 10.5.5 W4 — Attach, modules, and lifetime correctness\\n\\nRequired runtime gate: Node + Wasmtime full module/state/ABI traces; implement\\nnative admission, module stores and resource/closure transport. Bring up Wasmer\\nwith the same native service library and run W1–W4 suites before W5.\\n\\nImplement independent image instances/function-table append, `IModuleKernel`\\nadapter, closure/resource transport, `store-of` restoration, `reset`/`load-image`\\ndistinctions and arena/heap root integration (§10.3).\\n\\nAcceptance: rerun both corpora completely; add A-calls-B-while-C-attaches\\nfollowed by ordinary return, park-before-attach then resume, stale hash but\\npresent image success, absent-image refusal, duplicate/empty attach no-op,\\nempty-base row, layout-image rehashing, independent `spawn-module`\\ncounters/stores and module `require` in a tail call. Cross-layout closure lower\\nvalidates arity/format and private store behavior. GC tests retain captured\\nlive values and FFI-pinned refs, release unreachable scratch records, and never\\nfree values held by old immutable state snapshots. No return or resume shrinks\\nthe code space. Reuse one compiled component in two different attachment orders\\nand assert absolute exit/capture/return/closure pcs and combined-segment\\npayload checks. Assert empty-base attachment retains both offset rows, `halted`\\ntrue and `registers []`, then `reset` and capture with the two-row table.\\nComplete gated install-child scheduling and link-response/install oracle tests.\\n**W4 does not pass until Wasmer passes the entire W1–W4 full-trace suite** on\\nthe identical binaries and its native service, alongside Node and Wasmtime.\\n\\nRequire native↔CLJS module-slice parity in both directions: lift with\\n`engine/lift-slice`, transfer real canonical content, then receive with\\n`engine/receive-module` semantics into matched receiver\\nlayouts/secrets/resources. Compare exact slice-entry vectors, cells/aliasing,\\nstore snapshots, component image hashes/addresses, minted stream/cursor ids and\\nseals, owner/profile fields, counters, diagnostics and subsequent module\\nexecution. Vary insertion history and map size, nested sets/maps, multiple\\nmodule stores and repeated references; verify the shared traversal\\nprerequisite, not just final return values. Recompute repository hashes on\\nreceiving native hosts. Also test distinct receiver secrets: compare each\\nreceiver against its own CLJS oracle, since valid resealing intentionally\\ndiffers across different secrets.\\n\\n#### 10.5.6 W5 — Lane completion and release gate\\n\\nRegister wasm as an embedder-agnostic backend with Node/CLJS, Wasmtime and\\nWasmer adapters, with no changes to the `r2` opcode table. Freeze\\n`yin.vm.engine.host.v1` ABI versioning and deterministic binary generation. Run\\nall named register tests and every semantic golden through the harness; report\\nunsupported canonical-semantic fragment cases as named refusals with reasons.\\nThe wasm backend must not silently narrow behavior relative to an admitted\\nregister image except documented resource limits or preexisting transport\\nrefusal boundaries.\\n\\nAcceptance: required corpora pass in full, every opcode exercised, public\\n`IVM`/`IVMState` behavior matches (including eval/environment refusals),\\ndifferential traces agree, no host handle leaks into wire payloads, and all\\nbinary artifacts validate. Run focused JVM reference tests with the existing\\n`clojure -M:test -n yin.vm.debruijn.register-test` invocation, the existing\\nNode tests through the documented\\n`clj -M:cljs -m shadow.cljs.devtools.cli compile test` lane, and the new wasm\\nharness in that lane. Add dedicated native Wasmtime and Wasmer harness tasks\\nand a shared cross-runtime trace runner; run all W1–W5 cases on Node and both\\nnative runtimes against the reference. No native lane may be reduced to\\nfinal-value checks. Verify byte-identical artifacts, `yin.vm.engine.host.v1`\\nimport allowlist, malformed ABI/overflow/`NEED_SPACE`/repeated-fetch handling,\\nno duplicate effects and no JS/WASI dependencies in native execution. Register\\nthese build/tasks during implementation rather than pretending they exist\\ntoday. Follow repository quick/slow phase gates and existing Dart regression\\nlane for shared CLJC edits. These acceptance tests are planned, not reported\\nexecution results.\\n\\n#### 10.5.7 W6 — Measured optimization and register non-goals\\n\\nKeep Node + Wasmtime + Wasmer full-trace parity mandatory for every\\noptimization. Measure compilation latency, binary size, native stack depth,\\narena/heap retention, dispatch cost, serialized `yin.vm.engine.host.v1` message\\nbytes/copy/codec time, call and suspension cost, and end-to-end time on each\\nembedder; compare Node against interpreted register and report native results\\nseparately without attributing service-language speed to wasm codegen. Only\\nthen coalesce blocks, reduce host crossings, add dirty spilling, or use\\noptional Binaryen passes ([`yang.wasm.md`](./yang.wasm.md) §6.1). Re-run all\\nparity and binary validation gates for each transformation. If crossing cost\\ndominates, prototype the self-contained interpreter-loop variant with\\nnative-to-wasm migration of value/scheduler services and only stream/FFI ABI\\ncrossings. Compare it against compiled bodies with batched definitions/block\\ndispatch on the same workloads; select by measurements while keeping explicit\\nresumable state. Any service migration reuses/compiles the same Rust crate\\nsemantics where the toolchain/profile permits it, rather than authoring a third\\nengine; the `i32`-handle `yin.vm.engine.host.v1` ABI and full kit remain the\\ncontract. K3 may measure wasm on this same harness alongside its JVM/Rust/AOT\\ncomparisons; these measurements do not commission K4. This optional service\\nmigration is outside W5's implementation, not excluded from W6 investigation. A\\nperformance regression is possible; making wasm work is not evidence it is\\nfaster.\\n\\nRegister-specific non-goals (in addition to the guest-agnostic non-goals in\\n[`yin.vm.engine.md`](./yin.vm.engine.md) §E.5.4): no new register\\nopcode/contract version, no alternate A→R register allocator, no native stack\\ncontinuations or async-stack capture, and no full semantic VM feature parity\\nbeyond the established native register fragment.\\n\\n#### 10.5.8 Rust K1–K5 and LLVM acceptance retained\\n\\nThe same K0/W0 conformance kit retains the Rust phase gates\\n(`docs/design/yin.vm.rust-kernel.md:1201–1228`):\\n- **K1:** pure fixtures and R/refusal parity; constant stack and one file per live activation on tail fixtures; refused-step atomicity; no panic. Fault-injected decoded images refuse with `:kernel-invariant` or poison at most the one machine, while another task completes.\\n- **K2:** payload/effect descriptor canonical bytes equal R4, including every return frame's `:live`; bidirectional Rust↔R4 restore and equal `continuation-defect` refusal rules.\\n- **K3:** benchmark evidence for owner review before K4 is commissioned.\\n- **K4:** deterministic translator bytes across runs/platforms, full-kit interpreted/native boundary and refusal equality, artifact-drop fallback, and interpretation with no toolchain plus `:unsupported`.\\n- **K5:** Level-2 engine parity and blocked-machine wire round-trips with JVM R4 in both directions.\\n\\nWasm completion does not certify the full K5 standalone node. Rust interpreter\\ncoverage advances alongside the W slices and cannot be omitted because native\\nwasm passes. Native runtime execution is not CLI validation, replay, a JS\\nsubprocess or a success-only primitive stub.\\n\\n### 10.6 Register-specific risks, reference conventions, and open items\\n\\nGuest-agnostic host, arena, single-port, and embedder risks are specified in\\n[`yin.vm.engine.md`](./yin.vm.engine.md) §E.5.4; lowering-specific risks (body\\ndispatch size, wasm module code growth, canonical admission) are in\\n[`yang.wasm.md`](./yang.wasm.md) §6.2. Register-host-specific risks retained\\nfrom r5 §7:\\n\\n1. **Native continuation transport has a limited scope.** The sources support same-model register payloads and qualified transport refusal, while the module encoder currently refuses continuations. Keep this distinction visible in API docs/tests. A full portable continuation design would require separate authorization and compatibility work, not an ad hoc wasm-only decoder.\\n2. **Absolute `pc` layout compatibility.** The register kernel's format check tests image membership, not complete receiving-layout equivalence. That suffices for grow-only same-task use; it is not proof that arbitrary imported absolute pcs mean the same thing. The transport adapter must enforce matching layout/prefix or use a separately specified rebaser (§10.2.3). Preserve legacy restore diagnostics without presenting the legacy check as stronger than it is.\\n\\nThe wasm register implementation is complete when the same emitted baseline\\nbinary for an admitted `r2` image has the same values, store transitions,\\ncontinuation records, refusals, parking/wake behavior and module growth under\\nNode, Wasmtime and Wasmer as under the existing register kernel, with\\nfull-trace parity and no suspended computation held on the wasm stack.\\n\\n**Reference conventions and open items:** Reference conventions follow\\n[`yin.vm.engine.md`](./yin.vm.engine.md) §E.5.5 and\\n[`yang.wasm.md`](./yang.wasm.md) `## Reference conventions`. The frozen r5 open\\nitems are recorded in [`yin.vm.engine.md`](./yin.vm.engine.md) §E.5.5 and\\n[`yang.wasm.md`](./yang.wasm.md) `## Open items` (the compiled-closure-call vs.\\nsingle-port transition boundary under `yin.vm.engine.host.v1`, and the W0\\n`COMPLETE→NEED_SPACE→fetch-response` transport-test notation).\\nnested maps/sets, repeated stream/cursor refs, multi-store modules and\\nmultiple invalid update keys. Compare exact minted ids, seals,\\n`:yin.k/entries`, `:cells`, `:available`, counters and resource maps. A parity\\nfailure here blocks the slice; it cannot be hidden as implementation-private\\narena variation. The old Rust K-phase restriction on CLJC edits remains\\ndistinct from this explicit wasm-driven shared-reference prerequisite: version\\nthe kit after that stabilization and retain the original goldens/history, as\\nspecified in §E.4.2 and [`yang.wasm.md`](./yang.wasm.md) §1.1's Rust-authority\\nreconciliation; do not silently choose Rust map order or normalize away\\nchanged guest-visible ids.\\n\\n#### E.4.4 Stream, park, custody-gate, FFI two-step, and `spawn-module` host rules\\n\\n**Stream and park transitions:** Stream `make`/`cursor`/`close` finish\\nwithout suspension under the current contract. `put`-full and `next`-blocked\\ncreate per-site wait entries using the guest's site builders. Advance the\\nguest site coordinate once, set `blocked` true / `halted` false / `value`\\n`:yin/blocked`, and return to the scheduler. Explicit `park` instead produces\\nthe parked record as `value` and sets `halted` true; it is not automatically\\nblocked and has no `wait-set` entry. The scheduler may still have other ready\\nwork. Resume from a ready entry uses `engine/resume-from-run-queue`: pop FIFO\\nentry, validate `resource-updates` keys, merge updates into `:resources`,\\nclear flags, and handle terminal wake diagnostics before adapter restore. The\\nsame errors must occur whether a stream failed immediately or after blocking.\\nExported entries contain no callbacks, raw stream handles, timers, or stale\\nservice-mailbox data.\\n\\n**Custody gates:** Retain `:yin.k/gate`, `:yin.k/issued`, `:yin.k/closes`,\\nentry issue numbers, and every extension key across synchronization and\\npublic map edits. Use `vm/gate-mode` and existing engine/FFI/module handlers;\\ndo not replace them with one generic “gate means error” switch:\\n- Any gate suppresses `ffi/put-request` append and returns `full`, so FFI retains its request as a `request-sent` writer. Link misses likewise park as `link-request` without minting a response cursor or appending; already-resolved module and install-join branches still follow the handler's ordering.\\n- Any gate suppresses autonomous link retry/response scans, FFI response polling, and `put`/`next` retries in `check-wait-set`. Gated install children are stamped/stepped only in `running` mode; ungated scheduling remains unchanged.\\n- `running` stream cursor creation creates the sealed reference and unminted resource cell without a stream cursor call. `exporting`/`ended` immediate observation paths refuse through the existing gate checks.\\n- `running` `put` uses the engine's deferred observation path and stamps a blocked writer with `:yin.k/issue`, advancing `:yin.k/issued` independently of `id-counter`. `running` blocked poll becomes `:reason :observe, :op :poll`, using the call-site `next` builder fallback; its application remains the driver's operation.\\n- **Current source nuance:** `running` stream close queues a record in `:yin.k/closes`, increments the issue counter, returns `nil` and does not call the stream handle. `exporting`/`ended` close refuses.\\n\\n**FFI two-step, without duplicate sends:**\\n1. `ffi/require-call-pair!` runs before allocating an id or parking. Read args in vector order, pin references, build the boundary payload, derive `ffi/call-id` from task identity and `park-id`, and park under that correlated id. Increment the shared `id-counter` once through park.\\n2. Build `apply2/request` and invoke `ffi/put-request`. On `ok`, enqueue `response-wait-entry`. On `full`, enqueue payload plus `request-sent` true, `call-id`, `op`, `reason` `:put`, call-in stream id and the exact request datom. Other outcomes preserve the existing qualified error. Under any custody gate `put-request` returns `full` without appending; retain the request for the driver rather than retrying it autonomously.\\n3. The ungated engine retries a full writer. Its wake means the request append completed: restore converts it to a response reader, strips stale wake/writer fields through `ffi/response-wait-entry`, and stays blocked. It must neither append the request again nor advance the guest beyond the pending result.\\n4. On correlated response, terminal response-loss handling runs first, then `call-result` unwraps success/error, parked `call-id` is removed, and the guest destination or tail return receives the result exactly once. Use constants from `yin.vm` for reserved resources. Keep the bridge in the existing host interpreter; do not implement wasm imports named after arbitrary FFI operations.\\n\\n**`spawn-module` isolation and node-local artifact-cache fallback:**\\n`spawn-module` creates a fresh task/memory/arena/owner/counter/resources\\ncontext, with the composition options copied by the kernel; it never borrows\\nthe parent's mutable execution shadow or per-module store. Under the shared\\nartifact-cache policy (`docs/design/yin.vm.rust-kernel.md:997–1011, 1113–1122,\\n1261–1263`; [`yang.wasm.md`](./yang.wasm.md) §5;\\n[`yin.vm.debruijn.register.md`](./yin.vm.debruijn.register.md) §10.3), compiled\\nwasm binaries are disposable **node-local caches**, never `dao.jing` content\\nidentities, refs, continuation fields or portable module payloads. A missing\\nor dropped artifact falls back to the same host's interpreter at any\\nmaterialized boundary, with no pending mutation or active guest stack.\\n\\n### E.5 Conformance method, guest-agnostic acceptance gates, toolchain pins, non-goals, risks, and open items\\n74:**The system ABI.** `dao.stream` is the system's binary interface — the only\\n96:**Owner ruling, 2026-10-19: the boundary is drawn, not found; one ABI is the\\n110:**Effects out.** Portable code appends an effect value describing what must\\n61:| `create!`    | creation specification | handle outcome     |\\n62:| `attach!`    | portable descriptor    | handle outcome     |\\n63:| `descriptor` | handle                 | descriptor outcome |\\n64:| `cursor`     | handle, anchor         | cursor outcome     |\\n65:| `next`       | handle, cursor         | read outcome       |\\n66:| `append!`    | handle, value          | write outcome      |\\n67:| `close!`     | handle                 | close outcome      |\\n128:  carries `:dao.stream/retry? true`. `descriptor` and `close!` never produce\\n166:position holds nothing yet. `append!`'s `:dao.stream/full` is the same\\n317:on a live handle returns `:dao.stream/ok` with `:dao.stream/descriptor`,\\n336:| `:dao.stream/ok`              | A new logical stream exists, its identity settled at once. Implies not that every resource backing it has finished being acquired. | `:dao.stream/handle` |\\n345:| `:dao.stream/ok`                 | A handle exists through which this attachment is operated. Implies neither that a far end has confirmed the stream it names nor that establishment has completed. Where either answer is one only the far end can give, it arrives later as data, on the channel the transport names (see Surfaces). | `:dao.stream/handle`, and `:dao.stream/attachment` where the transport distinguishes attachments |\\n413:(`close!`) — and the transport declares which. The Cursors, Reading, and\\n506:  and returns `:dao.stream/ok` with `:dao.stream/cursor`. The contract\\n521:  `cursor`'s outcome set is therefore `:dao.stream/ok` (with\\n571:`next` takes a handle and a cursor and returns an outcome immediately.\\n577:| `:dao.stream/ok`              | A value was observed.                                                         | `:dao.stream/value`, `:dao.stream/cursor` (successor)                                   |\\n594:`append!` takes a handle and a value and returns an outcome:\\n598:| `:dao.stream/ok`              | The value is accepted at the next position in the sequence this handle's writer surface is on. Implies neither local readability nor remote delivery. | —             |\\n599:| `:dao.stream/full`            | The transport does not accept the value; nothing was appended. Whether this is transient or permanent is the transport's declared nature (see Retention and Gaps). | —             |\\n605:Acceptance is handle-relative, as close outcomes are. **Which sequence** a\\n629:`close!` takes a handle and returns a map whose outcome set is `{ok}` — no\\n631:is also `:dao.stream/ok`.\\n640:`close!` closes **what the handle is on**. A handle explicitly designated by\\n650:what *this handle* is on. (Not every outcome is: `gap` is retention-relative\\n688:truth for that handle; each outcome is one perspective on it at one moment. A\\n   (`:segment`). That is the existing contract, not introduced here; a\\n   Rust node shares the `Arc<Image>` in memory and pays the cost only on\\n   the wire.\\n\\nRelated specs: [yin.vm.engine.md](./yin.vm.engine.md) (§E.1–§E.5, guest-agnostic execution host, `yin.vm.engine.host.v1` ABI, and K0/W0 conformance method); [yang.wasm.md](./yang.wasm.md) (register-image-to-wasm lowering); [yin.vm.debruijn.register.md](./yin.vm.debruijn.register.md) (§10, register wasm execution instance, K0/W0 register fixture schema, and register W0–W6 / Rust K1–K5 lane gates).\\n\\n2026-10-18: Added the owner-authorized wasm-plan and shared-kit pointers; revision 2 status and all other normative content are unchanged.\\n2026-10-19: Owner-directed decomposition and fold of the 2026-10-18 wasm consensus into yang.wasm.md (lowering), yin.vm.engine.md §E.1–§E.5 (guest-agnostic execution host, service protocol yin.vm.engine.host.v1, and conformance method), and yin.vm.debruijn.register.md §10 (register wasm execution instance, register fixture schema, and register W0–W6 / Rust K1–K5 lane gates), retiring yin.vm.register-wasm.md and yin.vm.conformance-kit.md; revision 2 status and line coordinates 1–1334 are unchanged.\\n\",\"exit_code\":0,\"status\":\"completed\"}}\n{\"type\":\"item.started\",\"item\":{\"id\":\"item_10\",\"type\":\"command_execution\",\"command\":\"/bin/zsh -lc \\\"sed -n '70,112p' docs/design/datom.world.md; sed -n '900,952p' docs/design/yin.vm.engine.md; sed -n '510,565p' docs/design/yang.wasm.md; sed -n '330,355p' docs/design/dao.stream.md; sed -n '570,606p' docs/design/dao.stream.md\\\"\",\"aggregated_output\":\"\",\"exit_code\":null,\"status\":\"in_progress\"}}\n{\"type\":\"item.completed\",\"item\":{\"id\":\"item_10\",\"type\":\"command_execution\",\"command\":\"/bin/zsh -lc \\\"sed -n '70,112p' docs/design/datom.world.md; sed -n '900,952p' docs/design/yin.vm.engine.md; sed -n '510,565p' docs/design/yang.wasm.md; sed -n '330,355p' docs/design/dao.stream.md; sed -n '570,606p' docs/design/dao.stream.md\\\"\",\"aggregated_output\":\"A host boundary is a stream boundary, never a function call. The one\\nsanctioned function-shaped contact is a `dao.stream` handle operation, whose\\nexhaustive outcome map is stream protocol, not an application callback.\\n\\n**The system ABI.** `dao.stream` is the system's binary interface — the only\\ncontract that crosses a trust domain. Nodes with no shared code (different\\nruntimes, languages, or machines) interoperate purely through canonical stream\\nvalues (`dao.jing` encodings, content-hash identities) and the\\nappend/park/resume discipline. Interfaces below this line are shims, not peers:\\na guest that cannot express a stream operation (a WebAssembly module's only\\noutgoing capability is an imported function over linear memory) is served by a\\nhost-local ABI — for the wasm lane, `yin.vm.engine.host.v1`\\n([`yin.vm.engine.md`](./yin.vm.engine.md) extension §E) — that terminates at the\\nembedding composition, which alone translates service requests into stream\\noperations. Two consequences are doctrine. First, what crosses a trust domain\\nis a strict subset of what exists inside a process: closures and continuations\\ncross only as portable plain data; host types, resources, and code artifacts\\nnever do — the custody gates mark exactly this seam\\n([`yin.vm.linker.md`](./yin.vm.linker.md)). Second, every new guest substrate\\nspecifies its boundary as such a shim to the composition, never as a second\\nsystem ABI, and a process-local ABI's definitions have exactly one home in the\\nspecification tree. Naming doctrine: \\\"the system ABI\\\" is reserved for\\n`dao.stream`; a guest-host interface such as `yin.vm.engine.host.v1` is always\\nqualified (the guest-host service ABI, the wasm service ABI), never bare \\\"the\\nABI\\\" in a definitional position.\\n\\n**Owner ruling, 2026-10-19: the boundary is drawn, not found; one ABI is the\\nprinciple.** Where the line between machine and world sits above the\\n`dao.stream` floor is a design choice, and it has been redrawn before\\n(contract v4 promoted `:define` from host service to opcode). The sole valid\\njustification for a second, process-local ABI is **performance**: the measured\\nengineering cost of per-instruction boundary crossings. It is therefore an\\nengineering-cost measure, not doctrine — provisional by principle, to be\\nshrunk as measurements allow and dissolved where a lane can sit at the floor\\n(a guest whose engine is compiled inside it needs only `dao.stream`). The\\nfloor itself is not movable: operations whose answers are facts about the\\nworld cannot be computed by shipped code. Architecture review should treat\\nevery addition to a process-local ABI as a cost to be justified, and every\\nremoval as progress toward the one-ABI state.\\n\\n**Effects out.** Portable code appends an effect value describing what must\\nhappen. A host interpreter consumes that stream, performs the operation, and\\nappends the outcome. Portable code reads the outcome. Correlation is by\\n#### E.4.2 Single-engine-port rule, fault containment, and Rust-kernel reconciliations\\n\\n**Single-engine-port rule:** the native service library **is** the\\n`yin-value`, `yin-scalar`, `yin-image`, `yin-kernel`, `yin-prims`,\\n`yin-engine` and `yin-wire` workspace designed at\\n`docs/design/yin.vm.rust-kernel.md:1134–1158`, extended with a handle/ABI\\nfacade and thin Wasmtime/Wasmer wrappers. It is never a parallel port of\\nguest execution, scheduler, validator, primitive semantics, custody, module\\nstores, collection or FFI two-step. The facade decodes validated messages,\\nresolves checked handles, invokes the shared transition functions and\\nserializes their committed outcomes. It owns only transport/arena/runtime\\nmechanics. The Node adapter decodes into existing CLJS records and calls\\nexisting CLJC helpers. Native execution cannot be replay, a JS subprocess or\\nsuccess-only stubs; missing required semantics fails the slice.\\n\\nThe “one transition implementation, two dispatchers” rule\\n(`docs/design/yin.vm.rust-kernel.md:991–995, 1308–1311`) applies across these\\nexecution strategies: CLJS kernel and CLJS wasm adapter reach the same CLJC\\ntransition; Rust interpreter, native wasm facade and later LLVM helpers reach\\nthe same Rust transition. Across CLJC and Rust there is necessarily a\\nlanguage port, governed by one specification and one conformance kit—not\\nliterally one callable binary. Within either host, a second service-specific\\ntransition implementation is a defect. Generated service sites never\\nreproduce call/return/restore/effect logic in a wrapper. Extract reusable\\ntransition helpers in the owning crate if necessary, rather than dispatching\\na second copy of the engine.\\n\\nPreserve check-then-commit atomicity and fault containment\\n(`docs/design/yin.vm.rust-kernel.md:639–713`): refused transitions leave\\nguest state unchanged; a panic is a kernel defect, caught before crossing the\\nruntime boundary, poisons only its machine, and cannot become a success\\npayload. ABI malformed-message refusals precede semantic work; retained\\nresponses preserve once-only effect completion. Safe facade code uses checked\\nslices and `Value` operations. Do not expand the `unsafe` surface beyond the\\nRust design's explicit boundary crates (lines `1154–1158`); any unavoidable\\nruntime binding belongs to a reviewed boundary, never the kernel/engine.\\nImported service calls are internal execution plumbing; guest external\\neffects still use stream emissions, not arbitrary synchronous host callbacks.\\n\\n[`docs/design/yin.vm.rust-kernel.md`](./yin.vm.rust-kernel.md) revision 2\\nremains **design-only, not authorized for implementation** (status and\\nsubordination at lines `3–35`, crates at `1134–1158`, decisions at\\n`1234–1265`). The owner-authorized wasm plan explicitly **supersedes only the\\nDEFERRED item “WASM as an artifact target”**\\n(`docs/design/yin.vm.rust-kernel.md:1294–1300`), with the following explicit\\nreconciliations:\\n- Current A identity and A→R admission govern over the older Rust topology and R-as-identity wording (§3, lines `282–318`; §7.5, lines `997–1011`).\\n- Rust K-D2b uses `u32` indices and qualified host limits (lines `434–448, 1245–1246`); wasm keeps 64-bit **wire pc fields** ([`yang.wasm.md`](./yang.wasm.md) §2.2), and the native facade checks every pc/operand/attachment against the Rust domain and declared limits before conversion or mutation, returning the same qualified `:host-limit`.\\n- Rust phases exclude existing CLJC edits (lines `93–95, 1164–1165`). §E.4.3 below (r5 §4.1.2) is a separately identified **wasm-driven shared-reference stabilization prerequisite**, outside that K-phase restriction. Export a versioned extension of the common kit after that shared change, preserving original goldens/history.\\n- Rust public `step` always interprets (lines `677–678`). The wasm backend's explicit one-instruction test/inspection mode is a separate dispatcher; optimized wasm run chunks obey the same boundary equivalence law ([`yang.wasm.md`](./yang.wasm.md) §3.1.1).\\n- The native wasm embedder needs Level-2 engine services in W2–W4 even though the Rust standalone-node roadmap leaves K5 last (§6.3, lines `748–781`; §10, lines `1170–1182`). Develop/reuse those **same crates** to the slice's required coverage; this schedules the necessary library subset for wasm, not the full K5 node or a new Rust network client.\\n\\n#### E.4.3 Deterministic service traversal prerequisite\\n### 6.2 Lowering non-goals and risks\\n\\nNon-goals for the lowering: no new opcode/contract version, alternate A→R allocator, native\\nstack continuations, async-stack capture, universal cross-model continuation conversion, new\\nportable continuation schema, direct arbitrary host FFI imports, WASI imports in the VM core,\\nthreads/shared memory, WasmGC dependency, numeric NaN boxing, cross-node artifact service, or\\noptimization before W5 correctness. Guest-agnostic and register-runtime non-goals are listed in\\n[`yin.vm.engine.md`](./yin.vm.engine.md) §E.5.4 and\\n[`yin.vm.debruijn.register.md`](./yin.vm.debruijn.register.md) §10.6.\\n\\nLowering-specific risks (retained from r5 §7; host/runtime risks in\\n[`yin.vm.engine.md`](./yin.vm.engine.md) §E.5.4 and\\n[`yin.vm.debruijn.register.md`](./yin.vm.debruijn.register.md) §10.6):\\n- **Large bodies and dispatch size.** One instruction per case can create deep block nesting and large binaries. W0 should pin practical limits; W6 can shard dispatch into bounded pages/helpers while keeping entry-pc precision. Such helpers must never hold suspended guest state in their native stack.\\n- **Module growth and code retention.** Wasm module code cannot be appended in place. Many image instances may increase memory and startup cost; function-table limits and compile failures require explicit resource refusals. Code unloading while live absolute pcs exist is out of scope.\\n- **Canonical admission and provenance.** Bare hand images remain useful under the existing raw kernel API; production module execution must retain A derivation verification. Compiled wasm caches are disposable artifacts. They cannot become another authority for code or continuation identity.\\n\\n---\\n\\n## Reference conventions\\n\\nReference convention: `register.cljc` means `src/cljc/yin/vm/debruijn/register.cljc`; `engine`,\\n`ffi`, `module`, `values`, and `debruijn_register_effects` filenames are under\\n`src/cljc/yin/vm/`; `vm` means `src/cljc/yin/vm.cljc`; `apply2` is the alias for\\n`dao.stream.apply` at `src/cljc/dao/stream/apply.cljc`. Unqualified register helpers such as\\nreturn-transition, write-back, payload-of, park-entry-fns, attach-image and spawn-module are in\\nregister.cljc; spawn-module/gc-roots are protocol implementations, not invented defns.\\n`module/IModuleKernel` and its gc-roots method are declared in module.cljc. IVM step/run are\\nmethods of `yin.vm/IVM`, not a namespace named IVM. `effects/*` names denote\\ndebruijn_register_effects.cljc. `register-image-defect`, `body-liveness`, `live-slot-index`,\\n`register-hash` and `opcode-table` belong to `src/cljc/yin/vm/debruijn_register_code.cljc`\\n(alias rcode); the `register-image` derivation belongs to\\n`src/cljc/yin/vm/semantic/derive.cljc`. `halted-with-empty-queue?`, `scheduler-round`,\\n`check-wait-set`, `handle-close` and `collect` are engine.cljc functions. The quoted\\nrestore/call helper names are existing source semantics, whereas exchange/fetch-response, ABI\\nstatuses and all native implementation entry names are proposed protocol vocabulary.\\n\\nThe `.cljc` line references in this specification, [`yin.vm.engine.md`](./yin.vm.engine.md)\\n(§E.1–§E.5), and [`yin.vm.debruijn.register.md`](./yin.vm.debruijn.register.md) (§10) are the r5-verified\\nreferences, checked against the current tree for this distillation. Rust-design line references\\nretain r5's coordinates; the related-spec additions to that document do not shift its existing\\nlines. New ABI names and native entry names describe planned interfaces, not existing repository\\nfunctions.\\n\\n## Open items\\n\\nThe frozen r5 text is retained where it leaves this codegen/host-boundary detail in tension;\\nthis decomposition does not resolve it (see [`yin.vm.engine.md`](./yin.vm.engine.md) §E.5.5 and\\n[`yin.vm.debruijn.register.md`](./yin.vm.debruijn.register.md) §10.6 for the runtime\\ntransport-test notation item):\\n\\n- §4 / [`yin.vm.engine.md`](./yin.vm.engine.md) §E.3.4 (r5 §4.1.1) says guest closure calls stay\\n  compiled, while the single-port rule in [`yin.vm.engine.md`](./yin.vm.engine.md) §E.4.2 (r5\\n  §4.1.1) requires service sites to reuse the owning host's call transition. The division\\n  between generated dispatch and shared transition work needs to be made concrete without\\n  creating a second semantic implementation.\\na consumer ignores qualified keys it does not understand.\\n\\n`create!` outcomes:\\n\\n| Outcome                       | Meaning                                       | Required keys        |\\n|-------------------------------|-----------------------------------------------|----------------------|\\n| `:dao.stream/ok`              | A new logical stream exists, its identity settled at once. Implies not that every resource backing it has finished being acquired. | `:dao.stream/handle` |\\n| `:dao.stream/invalid-spec`    | The creation specification is malformed.      | —                    |\\n| `:dao.stream/not-found`       | No transport here matches `:dao.stream/type`. | —                    |\\n| `:dao.stream/transport-error` | The handler failed.                           | —                    |\\n\\n`attach!` outcomes:\\n\\n| Outcome                          | Meaning                                                                             | Required keys        |\\n|----------------------------------|-------------------------------------------------------------------------------------|----------------------|\\n| `:dao.stream/ok`                 | A handle exists through which this attachment is operated. Implies neither that a far end has confirmed the stream it names nor that establishment has completed. Where either answer is one only the far end can give, it arrives later as data, on the channel the transport names (see Surfaces). | `:dao.stream/handle`, and `:dao.stream/attachment` where the transport distinguishes attachments |\\n| `:dao.stream/invalid-descriptor` | The descriptor is malformed.                                                        | —                    |\\n| `:dao.stream/not-found`          | The descriptor names no stream here, or no transport matches its type.              | —                    |\\n| `:dao.stream/transport-error`    | The handler failed.                                                                 | —                    |\\n\\nWhere a transport acquires its medium through a process rather than at once —\\nbinding a port, opening a handle through an API that only answers later — `ok`\\nstill means the logical stream exists: its identity is minted, its sequence is\\nempty, and the handle is operable. What it does not mean is that the stream can\\nyet accept values. The handle reports that through its own operations, exactly\\nas any other transient condition is reported, and the result of the acquisition\\n\\n`next` takes a handle and a cursor and returns an outcome immediately.\\nIt is total and non-blocking: every state of the stream has a defined data\\nanswer.\\n\\n| Outcome                       | Meaning                                                                       | Required keys                                                                           |\\n|-------------------------------|-------------------------------------------------------------------------------|-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------|\\n| `:dao.stream/ok`              | A value was observed.                                                         | `:dao.stream/value`, `:dao.stream/cursor` (successor)                                   |\\n| `:dao.stream/blocked`         | Nothing at this position yet; retry later.                                    | —                                                                                       |\\n| `:dao.stream/end`             | What this handle is on is closed and this position is past the retained tail. | —                                                                                       |\\n| `:dao.stream/gap`             | This position was evicted.                                                    | `:dao.stream/cursor` (earliest retained position, or the tail when nothing is retained) |\\n| `:dao.stream/cursor-mismatch` | The cursor was minted by a different logical stream.                          | —                                                                                       |\\n| `:dao.stream/invalid-cursor`  | The value is not a cursor.                                                    | —                                                                                       |\\n| `:dao.stream/refused`         | A policy composed on this handle declined the read; nothing was observed.     | —                                                                                       |\\n| `:dao.stream/transport-error` | The transport failed to perform the read; nothing was observed.               | —                                                                                       |\\n\\nReading never mutates the stream. Two readers with cursors at the same\\nposition observe the same retained value; neither can starve or affect the\\nother. There is no destructive read anywhere in the contract — no drain, no\\ntake. An operation that removes an element as it reads would make one\\nreader's progress every other reader's data loss.\\n\\n## Writing\\n\\n`append!` takes a handle and a value and returns an outcome:\\n\\n| Outcome                       | Meaning                                                                                                                                              | Required keys |\\n|-------------------------------|------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------|---------------|\\n| `:dao.stream/ok`              | The value is accepted at the next position in the sequence this handle's writer surface is on. Implies neither local readability nor remote delivery. | —             |\\n| `:dao.stream/full`            | The transport does not accept the value; nothing was appended. Whether this is transient or permanent is the transport's declared nature (see Retention and Gaps). | —             |\\n| `:dao.stream/invalid-value`   | The transport cannot carry this value (for example, it cannot be encoded); nothing was appended.                                                     | —             |\\n| `:dao.stream/closed`          | What this handle is on is closed to new appends; the value was not appended.                                                                         | —             |\\n| `:dao.stream/refused`         | A policy composed on this handle declined the append; nothing was appended.                                                                          | —             |\\n| `:dao.stream/transport-error` | The transport failed to perform the append. Whether the value was appended is unknown unless the transport declares that its failures are clean.     | —             |\\n\\nAcceptance is handle-relative, as close outcomes are. **Which sequence** a\\nhandle's writer surface is on is part of the transport's declared nature: for a\\n\",\"exit_code\":0,\"status\":\"completed\"}}\n{\"type\":\"item.started\",\"item\":{\"id\":\"item_11\",\"type\":\"command_execution\",\"command\":\"/bin/zsh -lc \\\"python3 - <<'PY'\\nfrom pathlib import Path\\nimport hashlib\\nD=Path('docs/design')\\npaths={n:D/(n+'.md') for n in ['datom.world','yang.wasm','yin.vm.engine','yin.vm.debruijn.register','yin.vm.rust-kernel']}\\no={n:p.read_text() for n,p in paths.items()}\\ndef between(t,a,b): return t[t.index(a):t.index(b)]\\ndef replaceblock(t,a,b,new): return t[:t.index(a)]+new+'\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\n'+t[t.index(b):]\\n# master authority\\nm=replaceblock(o['datom.world'],'**The system ABI.**','**Effects out.**','''**The system ABI.** \\\"'`dao.stream` is the one ABI of datom.world and `yin.vm`,\\nthe only contract crossing a trust domain. Nodes interoperate through canonical\\nstream values (Jing CBOR and content identities), exhaustive stream outcome maps,\\nand append/park/resume. A ws or UDP transport, or wasm imported functions over\\nlinear memory, is a **binding of this ABI**, never a second semantic contract.\\nThe wasm binding is specified once in [yin.vm.engine.md](./yin.vm.engine.md) §E.3.\\n\\nWhat crosses a trust domain is a strict subset of in-machine values: closures\\nand continuations cross only as admissible portable plain data; host types,\\nresources and code artifacts never do. The custody gates retain this distinction\\n([yin.vm.linker.md](./yin.vm.linker.md)); a physical binding grants no additional\\nportability or authority.\\n\\n**Owner ruling, strengthened 2026-10-19: the boundary is drawn, not found;\\none ABI is the principle.** The boundary between wasm and datom.world is\\n`dao.stream`; its implementation uses whatever wasm exposes to the outside\\nworld. The exposed functions are **adapters to dao.stream**, which is the ABI\\nto `yin.vm` and the whole ecosystem. Machine-internal work executes inside the\\nwasm instance: the Rust engine crates and compiled register bodies share memory\\nand direct calls. Only effects/facts cross through the stream adapters. World\\nfacts still require interaction with the world; moving engine implementation\\ninside the machine does not manufacture those facts.\\n\\n**Dated supersession (owner boundary clarification, 2026-10-19):** This replaces\\nthe earlier same-day “sole justification … performance” allowance for a second\\nprocess-local ABI and the service-protocol interpretation of\\n`yin.vm.engine.host.v1` (historically renamed from `yin.register.host.v1`). There\\nis no second ABI to justify. Its private operation vocabulary, TLV codec,\\nexpanded-canonical-bytes comparator and service-status protocol are dissolved.\\nPerformance work optimizes internal execution or the stream binding; it does\\nnot reintroduce a semantic service interface. [yang.wasm.md](./yang.wasm.md)\\nis the compiler implementation plan for this in-instance architecture.'\\\"''')\\n# engine preserve original body and semantic chunks\\nold=o['yin.vm.engine']; start=old.index('## Extension (2026-10-19')\\nprefix=old[:start].replace('\\\"'`yin.vm.engine.host.v1`'\\\"','the dao.stream wasm binding')\\ne1=between(old,'### E.1 ','### E.2 ')\\ne1=replaceblock(e1,\\\\\\\"A guest machine's execution state\\\\\\\",'The program store', '''A guest machine's execution state materializes as immutable named-field data\\ninside the instance. The task-owned Value arena and engine state live in wasm\\nlinear memory; compiled bodies are execution caches over that state. Separate\\ntasks have isolated arenas, even when they share cached compiled Modules. A\\nsupplied coremem must preserve allocation ownership and task isolation.\\n\\nThe CLJS oracle remains a persistent record with ordinary map keys. The Rust\\nengine has equivalent immutable named-field snapshots, not Clojure records.\\nThis is an **internal bodies↔engine synchronization discipline**, not a host\\nmessage or a production state-install import. Test/inspection entry points must\\nexercise old-state replay and supplied-state replacement without creating a\\nsecond system interface. The four-step discipline is:\\n\\n1. **Engine-to-body entry:** import all declared execution fields, layout and engine state from the supplied authoritative snapshot. W1 does so unconditionally. A later cache may skip only after proving all relevant keys agree; a private token is not proof. An externally \\\"'`assoc`-modified oracle fixture must have an equivalent replaced Rust snapshot. Copy or privately own the active shadow so old-state replay cannot mutate another snapshot.\\n2. **Body-to-engine boundary:** before any state-consuming engine helper, including effects, pinning, collection, scheduler, park/resume or modules, materialize all declared execution fields onto the engine Machine/state record. Do the same at step/run/restore/inspection returns. Equivalent `gc-roots` dispatch must inspect those current fields; synchronizing only the dense array is insufficient.\\n3. **Engine completion:** its returned state becomes authoritative, including every extension key. Apply only the opcode'\\\"'s specified post-helper changes, then reload locals/shadow. Never overlay a stale header. Preserve post-effect \\\"'`:value`, resources, heap/GC, stores, counters, queues and custody. A plain non-tail primitive updates destination/pc without automatically replacing `:value`.\\n4. **Site coordinate:** expose the absolute instruction site unless the source transition explicitly advances it first (FFI parking). Capture at that site; apply the opcode'\\\"'s pc update once. Internal pending-operation scratch is not a completed instruction.\\n\\nThis is also a GC barrier: \\\"'`:cell/new` may collect *inside* `handle-effect`.\\nExecution fields and roots must be current before entry. Arena retention cannot\\nsubstitute for the engine'\\\"'s exact logical heap tracing rules.''')\\ne1=e1.replace(\\\\\\\"embedder's persistent map\\\\\\\", \\\\\\\"in-instance engine's persistent map\\\\\\\").replace('the map and updates are addressable through ABI value\\\\\\\\ndefinitions/state messages on every runtime.','all access and updates use direct in-instance helpers.').replace('linear memory and host synchronization','compiled-body and engine synchronization')\\ne21='''### E.2 The in-instance value arena and two repository codecs\\n\\n#### E.2.1 Task-owned handles and the internal handle↔Value facade\\n\\nCompiled operands are \\\"'`i32` ValueRefs: 0=nil, 1=false, 2=true; every other\\ninteger, float, string, symbol, keyword, collection, primitive, closure,\\ncontinuation and sealed stream/cursor reference occupies a checked arena slot.\\nOnly nil and false are false. No NaN boxing or externref is used. Numeric\\nsemantics, metadata, equality and exact carriers come from the shared engine\\ncrates and match the CLJS oracle, not Rust or JS default coercions.\\n\\nThe arena is **inside wasm linear memory**, owned by the in-instance engine.\\nThe descriptor table holds kind, generation, task-local slot and optional\\nmachine-record offset. The internal handle↔Value facade indexes the shared\\n`yin-value` Values consumed by `yin-kernel`; it is not a second Value port.\\nK-D2 specifies the 16-byte `#[repr(C, u8)]` enum with safe `Rc` ownership,\\nexplicitly not NaN-boxed (`docs/design/yin.vm.rust-kernel.md:324–392, 1238–1244`).\\nW0/W1 prove the target layout/build rather than assuming native layout works\\nunchanged on wasm32. Safe Clone/Drop owns retention and release; no copied enum\\nbytes or Rust pointers cross adapters. Task ownership, bounds, kind and generation\\nare checked before safe engine APIs. These Values remain machine-local, not\\nSend/Sync; immutable admitted Images retain owned literals and Arc sharing,\\nmaterializing constants per task (`docs/design/yin.vm.rust-kernel.md:394–432, 553–563`).\\n\\nTrusted constructors alone mint Closure/Continuation/effect identities. A\\nliteral map is data regardless of keys. Returned effects pass `module/effect?`\\nand `engine/check-callee-effect!`. Stream/cursor references are sealed plain\\nmaps; actual operational handles stay in private resources. Primitive functions\\nrun in-instance; external bridge handlers observe streams in the composition.\\nNeither becomes a serialized function pointer. Pointer equality is not guest\\nequality. Round trips cover metadata, exact integers, float bits, nested values\\nand trusted wrappers.\\n\\nInternal representation may hold values that neither repository codec admits\\n(e.g. live primitives or lone-surrogate strings); retain or refuse them under\\nexisting predicates. The binding never makes them portable. Canonical Jing\\nencoding decides wire carriers, NaNs, integral floats and negative zero; no\\nprivate TLV framing or private normalization rule survives the owner ruling.\\n\\n'\\\"'''\\ne22=between(old,'#### E.2.2 ','#### E.2.3 ')\\ne22=replaceblock(e22,'#### E.2.2 ','Port executable framing exactly:', '''#### E.2.2 Two repository codecs and mandatory hashing\\n\\nThe same in-instance crates implement both existing repository codecs. Neither\\nmay be replaced by the other or by a binding-specific encoding.\\n\\n| Domain | Authority / obligation | Observable products |\\n|---|---|---|\\n| Register scalar/image encoding | \\\"'`debruijn_code.cljc` `scalar-class`, `encode-scalar`, `framed`; `debruijn_register_code.cljc` `encode-register-image`, `descriptor-hash`, `register-hash` | R, layout checks, continuation self-consistency. |\\n| Jing canonical CBOR | `dao/jing/cbor.cljc` `encode`/`decode`; `dao/jing.cljc` `canonical-bytes`, `content-hash`, `segment-key` | Stream-binding bytes, owner, seals, profile, module/semantic addresses. |'\\\"''')\\ne22=e22.replace('or hash the ABI node bytes','or hash a handle representation').replace('The private ABI may carry a lone UTF-16 surrogate\\\\\\\\nfor in-machine fidelity; that does not make it hashable by either repository\\\\\\\\ncodec.','In-machine values may retain a lone UTF-16 surrogate; it remains unhashable\\\\\\\\nby either repository codec.').replace('native\\\\\\\\nservice trust','in-instance engine trust')\\ne22=e22.replace('The\\\\\\\\nfacade alone owns private ABI framing/reference expansion; it calls these\\\\\\\\ncrates for identity bytes and hashes.','The\\\\\\\\ninternal facade calls these same crates for identity bytes and hashes.').replace('W0\\\\\\\\nports/verifies these codecs and hashing primitives before native execution\\\\\\\\ngates.','W0\\\\\\\\nfreezes/verifies their oracle vectors before execution gates; W1 proves the\\\\\\\\nshared implementations run inside wasm.').replace('native\\\\\\\\nservice obligations independent of the ABI codec implementation.','in-instance obligations independent of physical stream transport.')\\ne23=between(old,'#### E.2.3 ','### E.3 ')\\ne23=e23.replace('host service','in-instance engine helper').replace('mandatory map barrier','mandatory state-record barrier').replace('embedder arena ownership','in-instance arena ownership').replace('every host import','every internal handle lookup').replace('immutable host snapshots','immutable named-field snapshots')\\ne3='''### E.3 The dao.stream wasm binding\\n\\n**Owner supersession, 2026-10-19 (boundary clarification):** the former\\n\\\"'`yin.vm.engine.host.v1` service protocol (previous name `yin.register.host.v1`)\\nis dissolved, including control-block tables, operation envelopes, TLV tags,\\nexpanded-canonical-bytes comparator, service statuses and NEED_SPACE/refetch.\\nThe earlier performance exception is superseded. This section defines only a\\nphysical binding of [dao.stream](./dao.stream.md); it adds no stream outcomes,\\nsemantic operations, private codec or second ABI.\\n\\n#### E.3.1 Imported adapter surface\\n\\nUse import module `dao.stream` with the following proposed binding names.\\nAll parameters and single results are i32. `p,n` address one canonical Jing\\nCBOR value; `out,cap` denote a disjoint writable response region. The returned\\ni32 is the **encoded response byte length**, never a status number. Decode\\nthat response into the operation'\\\"'s exhaustive outcome map. Zero-copy views\\nmust not outlive the synchronous call or memory growth.\\n\\n| Import | Parameters → result | Existing stream operation |\\n|---|---|---|\\n| \\\"'`create` | `(p,n,out,cap) → length` | `create!` on a creation specification (VM make-stream adapter). |\\n| `attach` | `(p,n,out,cap) → length` | `attach!` on a portable descriptor. |\\n| `descriptor` | `(handle,out,cap) → length` | `descriptor`. |\\n| `cursor` | `(handle,p,n,out,cap) → length` | `cursor` with decoded anchor. |\\n| `next` | `(handle,p,n,out,cap) → length` | `next` with decoded cursor; the poll operation. |\\n| `append` | `(handle,p,n,out,cap) → length` | `append!` with decoded value. |\\n| `close` | `(handle,out,cap) → length` | `close!`. |\\n\\nMake/poll are VM-facing terminology, not additional stream operations. Park,\\nresume, resolve-var, primitive application, state reads, roots, collection and\\nmodule handling have **no imports**: they execute in-instance. Imports never\\nname arbitrary FFI operations. Memory/table provision and module instantiation\\nare runtime linkage/lifecycle mechanics, not semantic service operations.\\n\\nOperational stream handles cannot themselves be canonical data. The binding\\nrepresents the handle field in a successful create/attach outcome by a checked,\\ntask-local i32 slot, encoded as a Jing integer; decoding reconstructs the local\\nhandle resource before the engine consumes the outcome. This is the physical\\nrepresentation of an existing handle, not a guest stream identity or a portable\\ncapability. Keep this binding handle namespace distinct from ValueRefs, check\\nownership/lifetime, and never publish it in a stream value, continuation or\\nmodule payload. Portable descriptors/cursors and all other outcome fields use\\ntheir actual canonical data. Repeated reads remain non-destructive; the adapter\\nhas no privileged reader and introduces no callbacks.\\n\\n#### E.3.2 Outcome and buffer discipline\\n\\nThe exhaustive outcome sets and required keys are those of [dao.stream.md](./dao.stream.md),\\nCreating and Attaching, Cursors, Reading, Writing and Closing. Preserve optional\\nqualified diagnostics and retry declarations. In particular append returns\\nok/full/invalid-value/closed/refused/transport-error; next returns\\nok/blocked/end/gap/cursor-mismatch/invalid-cursor/refused/transport-error;\\nclose has only ok. Do not turn ordinary blocking or refusal into a trap.\\n\\nThe engine allocates request/response memory before invoking an adapter. The\\ncomposition declares supported payload/output bounds; W0 establishes a response\\nreservation sufficient for **every** admitted result, including diagnostic and\\nhandle outcomes. Validate pointers, lengths, overflow, overlap and reservation\\nbefore executing an operation. Invalid binding calls are implementation faults,\\nnot new stream statuses. Allocation failure is a qualified local resource limit\\nbefore invocation. No operation may commit and then ask the guest to resize and\\nfetch its response; no retained-response service or semantic replay exists.\\n\\nA full append buffer uses the stream'\\\"'s **full** outcome: nothing appended, keep\\nthe exact pending value and retry through the existing writer wait discipline.\\nResponse capacity is reserved separately; full is not invented for reads or\\nclose. Read-side inability to carry an oversized value follows the stream's\\ntransport-error outcome without advancing the immutable cursor. Admitted limits\\nmust make ordinary outcomes deliverable; test limit failures before mutation.\\nAn append transport-error can mean acceptance is unknown: do not automatically\\nretry unless that transport declares clean failure. Blocking waits stay as pure\\ndata in the in-instance engine; the composition later drives execution again,\\nwithout retaining a wasm activation or application callback.\\n\\n#### E.3.3 Canonical bytes and authority\\n\\nRequests and outcome data use exactly Jing's canonical CBOR (§E.2.2), with its\\nown ordering, numeric carriers, validation and decode/re-encode rules. Length\\nparameters delimit one CBOR value; no TLV or alternate framing wraps it.\\nCanonical maps/sets use repository ordering, never task handles or arena ids.\\nRegister image checksums still use the register codec. Buffer access grants no\\npermission to pass internal Values, private engine snapshots or trusted wrappers\\nacross custody gates. Stream history records effects/facts, not register loads,\\nroot materialization or other machine-internal traffic.\\n\\n'''\\ne4='''### E.4 Composition adapters and the single in-instance engine\\n\\n#### E.4.1 Execution and embedding responsibilities\\n\\nNode/browser, Wasmtime and Wasmer provide the same stream binding (§E.3).\\nThe composition hosts adapters and their actual transports/bridge interpreters;\\nit does not implement resolve-var, primitives, stores, GC, continuations or the\\nscheduler for wasm. A WASI-based composition may implement transports below the\\nbinding; the VM core imports no WASI. There is no JS dependency in native runs.\\nThe CLJS engine is the reference oracle, not a second production wasm engine.\\n\\nThe in-instance engine owns run-loop/scheduler-round, step budgets, ready/wait\\nqueues and terminal checks. A step performs exactly one guest instruction; run\\nkeeps the original ready-queue/terminal ordering and returns on the same blocked,\\nparked or halted states. Blocking is a normal result. Inspection/test snapshots\\nare immutable data, not an external engine service contract. Runtime start/drive\\nexports may only enter the in-instance driver; they are lifecycle controls, not\\napplication callbacks. No wasm stack survives a wait.\\n\\n#### E.4.2 Single-engine-port rule and Rust reconciliation\\n\\nThe production engine **is** the Rust design's \\\"'`yin-value`, `yin-scalar`,\\n`yin-image`, `yin-kernel`, `yin-prims`, `yin-engine`, `yin-wire` workspace\\n(`docs/design/yin.vm.rust-kernel.md:1134–1158`), compiled to\\n`wasm32-unknown-unknown` and linked with compiled register bodies. The internal\\nhandle facade adapts values, never reimplements semantics. Native wrappers\\nimplement only stream adapters and runtime mechanics. No parallel engine port\\nis permitted to bypass a crate'\\\"'s missing wasm support.\\n\\n“One transition implementation, two dispatchers” applies to the Rust interpreter,\\ncompiled wasm bodies and future LLVM helpers (\\\"'`docs/design/yin.vm.rust-kernel.md:991–995, 1308–1311`).\\nGenerated control flow uses shared transition helpers for call/return/restore/\\neffects; no wrapper duplicates them. CLJS necessarily has a language-port\\nimplementation: it remains the shared-kit oracle, not literally the same binary.\\nCheck-then-commit, refused-step equality and per-machine fault containment remain\\nmandatory (`docs/design/yin.vm.rust-kernel.md:639–713`). Panics are kernel defects,\\nnot success outcomes. W0/W1 must prove trap containment with this wasm profile;\\nnever rely on wasm exceptions or unwinding. Safe checked slices/Value APIs and\\nthe Rust design'\\\"'s reviewed boundary crates retain the unsafe-code boundary\\n(lines 1154–1158).\\n\\nRust revision 2's design-only status is unchanged. This wasm plan supersedes its\\nDEFERRED wasm artifact item (lines 1294–1300), not the K3 review gate for K4 or\\nunrelated standalone-node/transports work. The required K-phase library subset\\nis now a **wasm build dependency**, including Level-2 engine work by W3–W4.\\nIt does not authorize a separate full K5 node. A remains sole code identity;\\nR is executable checksum despite older Rust topology wording (lines 282–318,\\n997–1011). u32 Rust indices retain checked host limits (434–448, 1245–1246)\\nagainst 64-bit stored pc coordinates, never truncation. Rust public step still\\ninterprets (677–678); generated one-step mode is a separate dispatcher.\\nShared-reference traversal stabilization below remains an explicitly scoped\\nwasm prerequisite outside the Rust phases' CLJC-edit exclusion (93–95, 1164–1165).\\n\\n'''\\ntrav=between(old,'#### E.4.3 ','#### E.4.4 ')\\ntrav=trav.replace('service traversal','engine traversal').replace('native service','in-instance engine').replace('canonicalizing only the final ABI bytes','canonicalizing only final stream bytes')\\na=trav.index('For currently unordered traversal sites'); b=trav.index('| Site (existing symbols')\\ntrav=trav[:a]+'''For currently unordered traversal sites, use canonical **Jing CBOR bytes** for\\ncanonical keys/elements, never arena numbering or encoded resource-lifting output.\\nFor internal values outside Jing's domain, retain non-minting structural\\ncomparison within the value implementation; W0 must freeze its type/field order\\nand cross-language vectors before enabling those fixtures. It is an in-memory\\ncomparison, not a third serialized codec or a claim those values are portable.\\nThis replaces the dissolved private-byte comparator by the owner ruling.\\nPrepare ordering before id-minting traversal. Retain vector/list order, ordered\\nexports, wait/ready FIFO and first-occurrence deduplication. Preserve existing\\nexplicit \\\"'`sort-by str` cursor/module-store order and `sort-by key` image order,\\nwith cross-host fixtures; do not replace already-specified ordering.\\n\\n'\\\"'''+trav[b:]\\ne44=between(old,'#### E.4.4 ','### E.5 ')\\ne44=e44.replace('host rules','in-instance rules').replace('before adapter restore','before in-instance restore').replace('stale\\\\\\\\nservice-mailbox data','internal\\\\\\\\npending-operation scratch').replace(\\\\\\\"same host's interpreter\\\\\\\",\\\\\\\"in-instance register interpreter\\\\\\\")\\ne51=between(old,'### E.5 ','#### E.5.2 ')\\ne51=e51.replace('engine execution host','in-instance engine').replace('\\\"'`yin.vm.engine.host.v1`'\\\"\\\\\\\\ntransport','dao.stream wasm binding').replace('ABI-specific','Binding-specific').replace('serialized ABI\\\\\\\\ntraffic','canonical stream-binding\\\\\\\\ntraffic')\\ne51=e51.replace('One manifest drives **three execution lanes: R4 CLJS reference, Rust\\\\\\\\nkernel/interpreter, wasm**.', 'One manifest drives the **CLJS oracle and the in-wasm Rust engine under Node,\\\\\\\\nWasmtime and Wasmer**, plus the shared native Rust interpreter and planned LLVM\\\\\\\\nlanes as their phases become available.')\\ne51=e51.replace('Rust interpreter\\\\\\\\ncoverage advances with each W slice; it cannot be omitted because native wasm\\\\\\\\nhappens to pass.', 'The in-instance Rust interpreter and compiled dispatcher both pass each W\\\\\\\\nslice; native Rust phase gates remain independently required for their claims.')\\ne51=e51.replace('Test\\\\\\\\nprimitive implementations and scripted transports exist in both native and\\\\\\\\nCLJS compositions','Test\\\\\\\\nprimitive implementations exist in the in-instance crates and CLJS oracle;\\\\\\\\nscripted transports exist in every composition')\\ne52='''#### E.5.2 Codec, identity and traversal determinism gates\\n\\nBoth repository codecs (§E.2.2) must match frozen bytes/hashes across CLJS and\\nin-wasm Rust: R (including empty/combined images), owner, seal, primitive-profile\\nand manifest addresses are bit-identical for equal inputs/secrets. Reuse existing\\nJing/scalar/image corpora without regenerating goldens. W1 verifies load/profiles;\\nW2 recomputes continuation hashes; W3 checks minted seals; W4 checks admitted\\nmodules/layouts/slices. Jing alone governs binding canonicalization, numeric\\ncarriers, NaNs, negative zero, collision/refusal and decode/re-encode identity.\\nInternal noncanonical values keep their in-machine fidelity and existing wire\\nrefusals; no private transport codec is required to compare them structurally.\\n\\nTraversal gates (§E.4.3) compare exact \\\"'`:yin.k/c-N`, stream/cursor ids/seals,\\n`:yin.k/entries`, `:cells`, `:available`, counters/resource maps and first-error\\norder. Vary insertion history, small/large CLJS map representations, nested maps/\\nsets, repeated references, module stores and multiple invalid resource keys.\\nPrivate handle renaming never excuses a guest-visible difference.\\n\\n'\\\"'''\\npins=between(old,'| Tool | Pin / scope | Evidence |','W0 must build these pins together')\\npins=pins.replace('host service/wrapper builds, not guest wasm generation','in-instance engine target \\\"'`wasm32-unknown-unknown`, plus runtime wrappers'\\\"')\\ne53='''#### E.5.3 W0 oracle, toolchain, wasm32 and binding gates\\n\\n**Oracle first:** consume/extend the R4-exported K0 kit before trusting any new\\nengine or compiler. Preserve fixture schema and original goldens. Pin the\\nreference revision plus scoped traversal delta. W0 probes wasm32 compilability;\\nW1 cannot pass until the actual required Rust crates link and execute inside\\nwasm. An unavailable crate dependency blocks the lane; no host service fallback.\\n\\nThese are inherited reproducibility targets, not claims of installed/tested tools:\\n\\n'''+pins+'''W0 builds/probes the pins together, records checksums, target triples, Java 21\\nvendor/build and transitive lockfiles. Any changed pin requires an explicit\\nrecord and rerun. Binaryen is absent initially. Record browser versions on\\nqualification. The Rust/LLVM linker used for the engine must be pinned as part\\nof the Rust build; deterministic WAT emission/WABT remains the body toolchain.\\n\\nProbe core wasm 1.0 plus mutable globals and original function tables: imported\\nmemory, checked bounds/byte order, i32 calls, i64 memory fields, active segments,\\ncall_indirect, memory growth/view refresh and shared task memory across image\\ncomponents. Audit **engine dependencies and final linked artifacts**, not only\\nemitted bodies, against the allowlist. No WASI, reference types, bulk memory,\\nthreads, exceptions, tail-call extension or WasmGC may slip in via Rust defaults.\\nEvery extension is a separately probed profile/cache key. Test embedding\\nharnesses, not a bare CLI unable to supply imports.\\n\\nW0 binding probes run on Node and Wasmtime (Wasmer smoke probe where available;\\nfull suites mandatory by W5): canonical request/outcome round trips, all declared\\nstream outcomes, handle ownership, malformed CBOR, bounds/overlap/overflow,\\ninsufficient **pre-operation** reservation, full-before-append and no mutation\\non rejected binding calls. W3 repeats against real FFI/writer/reader paths and\\nproves once-only append after full. Test unknown-acceptance transport errors\\nwithout blind retry. No post-commit resize/refetch protocol exists.\\n\\nSnapshot gates cover all declared fields/extension keys, supplied-state\\nreplacement, old-state replay and immutable snapshots. GC gates force collection\\ninside a helper. Codec probes include both repository encodings, metadata,\\nfloat/integer carriers, canonical collisions, surrogate refusals, deep keys and\\npermuted handle allocation; frozen private-TLV tests are removed by supersession.\\n\\nW/K map: W0=K0 oracle/probes; W1=required K1 engine/value/image builds in wasm;\\nW2=K2 restore plus park subset; W3–W4=required Level-2 engine library coverage;\\nW5=wasm completion on Node/Wasmtime/Wasmer, not full K5; W6=codegen measurements\\non the shared K3 harness, without commissioning K4\\n(\\\"'`docs/design/yin.vm.rust-kernel.md:1167–1182, 1215–1228`). Full register slice\\nacceptance lives in [yin.vm.debruijn.register.md](./yin.vm.debruijn.register.md)\\n§10.5, compiler work in [yang.wasm.md](./yang.wasm.md) §6.1.\\n\\n#### E.5.4 Non-goals and risks\\n\\nNo second engine/value/codec port, new register contract, universal continuation\\ntransport, arbitrary FFI imports, WASI core, threads/shared memory, WasmGC,\\nNaN-boxing, cross-node artifact service or unrelated K4/full-K5 authorization.\\nImmediate JVM/Dart application integration and malicious network wasm are not\\ncompletion claims. W6 starts after correctness; engine-in-guest is not deferred.\\n\\nRisks: wasm32/dependency feature compliance is an early hard gate; shared\\nLevel-2 crates are substantial required work. Preserve persistent snapshots over\\nmutable memory and logical heap roots separately from arena retention. Traps or\\npanics must not poison another task; ambiguous external effects cannot be rolled\\nback/retried as if absent. Cross-runtime buffer/handle lifetimes and dynamic\\nmodule installation need physical-runtime probes. Predicate/codec drift is caught\\nby the oracle kit. Measure in-instance code size, dispatch and collection costs;\\nnone justifies restoring a second semantic service protocol.\\n\\n#### E.5.5 Reference conventions and open items\\n\\nCLJC helper names denote oracle semantics and shared Rust equivalents, not a\\nrequirement to load Clojure inside wasm. Rust-design references preserve the\\noriginal lines 1–1334. Adapter names in §E.3.1 are proposed physical bindings.\\n\\nThe owner ruling resolves the former compiled-call/service split: generated\\ncontrol invokes the one in-instance transition implementation. It also removes\\nthe former response-refetch sequencing question with that protocol. Remaining\\nW0 gates are wasm32 build/profile feasibility, bounded response reservations,\\nand a frozen internal structural ordering for noncanonical values (§E.4.3).\\nDo not invent a third codec to answer the last question.\\n'\\\"'''\\nengine=prefix+'''## Extension (2026-10-19, design only): the in-instance engine and wasm stream binding\\n\\nStatus: design, owner-authorized planning; not implemented.\\n\\nThis extension retains the five-round r5 consensus (gpt-6-astra author,\\ngemini-4-argon reviewer, APPROVE on r5, 2026-10-18) where not superseded.\\n**Owner boundary clarification, 2026-10-19:** engine-in-guest replaces the\\nbody-lane-first service architecture. §E.1/E.2 synchronization and ownership\\nnow operate inside wasm; §E.3 replaces the dissolved protocol; §E.4 hosts only\\nstream adapters outside; §E.5 retains the shared K0/W0 oracle method.\\nHistorical rename \\\"'`yin.register.host.v1` → `yin.vm.engine.host.v1` is provenance,\\nnot a live interface. The performance exception, private third codec/comparator,\\ntransport statuses and deferred W6 engine migration are superseded.\\n\\nAuthorities: [datom.world.md](./datom.world.md), [dao.stream.md](./dao.stream.md),\\n[yin.vm.rust-kernel.md](./yin.vm.rust-kernel.md), [yin.vm.linker.md](./yin.vm.linker.md).\\nCompiler plan: [yang.wasm.md](./yang.wasm.md). Register instance/fixtures/gates:\\n[yin.vm.debruijn.register.md](./yin.vm.debruijn.register.md) §10.\\n\\n'\\\"'''+e1+e21+e22+e23+e3+e4+trav+e44+e51+e52+e53\\npaths['datom.world'].write_text(m); paths['yin.vm.engine'].write_text(engine)\\nprint('wrote master and engine; remaining docs next')\\nPY\\\"\",\"aggregated_output\":\"\",\"exit_code\":null,\"status\":\"in_progress\"}}\n{\"type\":\"item.completed\",\"item\":{\"id\":\"item_11\",\"type\":\"command_execution\",\"command\":\"/bin/zsh -lc \\\"python3 - <<'PY'\\nfrom pathlib import Path\\nimport hashlib\\nD=Path('docs/design')\\npaths={n:D/(n+'.md') for n in ['datom.world','yang.wasm','yin.vm.engine','yin.vm.debruijn.register','yin.vm.rust-kernel']}\\no={n:p.read_text() for n,p in paths.items()}\\ndef between(t,a,b): return t[t.index(a):t.index(b)]\\ndef replaceblock(t,a,b,new): return t[:t.index(a)]+new+'\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\n'+t[t.index(b):]\\n# master authority\\nm=replaceblock(o['datom.world'],'**The system ABI.**','**Effects out.**','''**The system ABI.** \\\"'`dao.stream` is the one ABI of datom.world and `yin.vm`,\\nthe only contract crossing a trust domain. Nodes interoperate through canonical\\nstream values (Jing CBOR and content identities), exhaustive stream outcome maps,\\nand append/park/resume. A ws or UDP transport, or wasm imported functions over\\nlinear memory, is a **binding of this ABI**, never a second semantic contract.\\nThe wasm binding is specified once in [yin.vm.engine.md](./yin.vm.engine.md) §E.3.\\n\\nWhat crosses a trust domain is a strict subset of in-machine values: closures\\nand continuations cross only as admissible portable plain data; host types,\\nresources and code artifacts never do. The custody gates retain this distinction\\n([yin.vm.linker.md](./yin.vm.linker.md)); a physical binding grants no additional\\nportability or authority.\\n\\n**Owner ruling, strengthened 2026-10-19: the boundary is drawn, not found;\\none ABI is the principle.** The boundary between wasm and datom.world is\\n`dao.stream`; its implementation uses whatever wasm exposes to the outside\\nworld. The exposed functions are **adapters to dao.stream**, which is the ABI\\nto `yin.vm` and the whole ecosystem. Machine-internal work executes inside the\\nwasm instance: the Rust engine crates and compiled register bodies share memory\\nand direct calls. Only effects/facts cross through the stream adapters. World\\nfacts still require interaction with the world; moving engine implementation\\ninside the machine does not manufacture those facts.\\n\\n**Dated supersession (owner boundary clarification, 2026-10-19):** This replaces\\nthe earlier same-day “sole justification … performance” allowance for a second\\nprocess-local ABI and the service-protocol interpretation of\\n`yin.vm.engine.host.v1` (historically renamed from `yin.register.host.v1`). There\\nis no second ABI to justify. Its private operation vocabulary, TLV codec,\\nexpanded-canonical-bytes comparator and service-status protocol are dissolved.\\nPerformance work optimizes internal execution or the stream binding; it does\\nnot reintroduce a semantic service interface. [yang.wasm.md](./yang.wasm.md)\\nis the compiler implementation plan for this in-instance architecture.'\\\"''')\\n# engine preserve original body and semantic chunks\\nold=o['yin.vm.engine']; start=old.index('## Extension (2026-10-19')\\nprefix=old[:start].replace('\\\"'`yin.vm.engine.host.v1`'\\\"','the dao.stream wasm binding')\\ne1=between(old,'### E.1 ','### E.2 ')\\ne1=replaceblock(e1,\\\\\\\"A guest machine's execution state\\\\\\\",'The program store', '''A guest machine's execution state materializes as immutable named-field data\\ninside the instance. The task-owned Value arena and engine state live in wasm\\nlinear memory; compiled bodies are execution caches over that state. Separate\\ntasks have isolated arenas, even when they share cached compiled Modules. A\\nsupplied coremem must preserve allocation ownership and task isolation.\\n\\nThe CLJS oracle remains a persistent record with ordinary map keys. The Rust\\nengine has equivalent immutable named-field snapshots, not Clojure records.\\nThis is an **internal bodies↔engine synchronization discipline**, not a host\\nmessage or a production state-install import. Test/inspection entry points must\\nexercise old-state replay and supplied-state replacement without creating a\\nsecond system interface. The four-step discipline is:\\n\\n1. **Engine-to-body entry:** import all declared execution fields, layout and engine state from the supplied authoritative snapshot. W1 does so unconditionally. A later cache may skip only after proving all relevant keys agree; a private token is not proof. An externally \\\"'`assoc`-modified oracle fixture must have an equivalent replaced Rust snapshot. Copy or privately own the active shadow so old-state replay cannot mutate another snapshot.\\n2. **Body-to-engine boundary:** before any state-consuming engine helper, including effects, pinning, collection, scheduler, park/resume or modules, materialize all declared execution fields onto the engine Machine/state record. Do the same at step/run/restore/inspection returns. Equivalent `gc-roots` dispatch must inspect those current fields; synchronizing only the dense array is insufficient.\\n3. **Engine completion:** its returned state becomes authoritative, including every extension key. Apply only the opcode'\\\"'s specified post-helper changes, then reload locals/shadow. Never overlay a stale header. Preserve post-effect \\\"'`:value`, resources, heap/GC, stores, counters, queues and custody. A plain non-tail primitive updates destination/pc without automatically replacing `:value`.\\n4. **Site coordinate:** expose the absolute instruction site unless the source transition explicitly advances it first (FFI parking). Capture at that site; apply the opcode'\\\"'s pc update once. Internal pending-operation scratch is not a completed instruction.\\n\\nThis is also a GC barrier: \\\"'`:cell/new` may collect *inside* `handle-effect`.\\nExecution fields and roots must be current before entry. Arena retention cannot\\nsubstitute for the engine'\\\"'s exact logical heap tracing rules.''')\\ne1=e1.replace(\\\\\\\"embedder's persistent map\\\\\\\", \\\\\\\"in-instance engine's persistent map\\\\\\\").replace('the map and updates are addressable through ABI value\\\\\\\\ndefinitions/state messages on every runtime.','all access and updates use direct in-instance helpers.').replace('linear memory and host synchronization','compiled-body and engine synchronization')\\ne21='''### E.2 The in-instance value arena and two repository codecs\\n\\n#### E.2.1 Task-owned handles and the internal handle↔Value facade\\n\\nCompiled operands are \\\"'`i32` ValueRefs: 0=nil, 1=false, 2=true; every other\\ninteger, float, string, symbol, keyword, collection, primitive, closure,\\ncontinuation and sealed stream/cursor reference occupies a checked arena slot.\\nOnly nil and false are false. No NaN boxing or externref is used. Numeric\\nsemantics, metadata, equality and exact carriers come from the shared engine\\ncrates and match the CLJS oracle, not Rust or JS default coercions.\\n\\nThe arena is **inside wasm linear memory**, owned by the in-instance engine.\\nThe descriptor table holds kind, generation, task-local slot and optional\\nmachine-record offset. The internal handle↔Value facade indexes the shared\\n`yin-value` Values consumed by `yin-kernel`; it is not a second Value port.\\nK-D2 specifies the 16-byte `#[repr(C, u8)]` enum with safe `Rc` ownership,\\nexplicitly not NaN-boxed (`docs/design/yin.vm.rust-kernel.md:324–392, 1238–1244`).\\nW0/W1 prove the target layout/build rather than assuming native layout works\\nunchanged on wasm32. Safe Clone/Drop owns retention and release; no copied enum\\nbytes or Rust pointers cross adapters. Task ownership, bounds, kind and generation\\nare checked before safe engine APIs. These Values remain machine-local, not\\nSend/Sync; immutable admitted Images retain owned literals and Arc sharing,\\nmaterializing constants per task (`docs/design/yin.vm.rust-kernel.md:394–432, 553–563`).\\n\\nTrusted constructors alone mint Closure/Continuation/effect identities. A\\nliteral map is data regardless of keys. Returned effects pass `module/effect?`\\nand `engine/check-callee-effect!`. Stream/cursor references are sealed plain\\nmaps; actual operational handles stay in private resources. Primitive functions\\nrun in-instance; external bridge handlers observe streams in the composition.\\nNeither becomes a serialized function pointer. Pointer equality is not guest\\nequality. Round trips cover metadata, exact integers, float bits, nested values\\nand trusted wrappers.\\n\\nInternal representation may hold values that neither repository codec admits\\n(e.g. live primitives or lone-surrogate strings); retain or refuse them under\\nexisting predicates. The binding never makes them portable. Canonical Jing\\nencoding decides wire carriers, NaNs, integral floats and negative zero; no\\nprivate TLV framing or private normalization rule survives the owner ruling.\\n\\n'\\\"'''\\ne22=between(old,'#### E.2.2 ','#### E.2.3 ')\\ne22=replaceblock(e22,'#### E.2.2 ','Port executable framing exactly:', '''#### E.2.2 Two repository codecs and mandatory hashing\\n\\nThe same in-instance crates implement both existing repository codecs. Neither\\nmay be replaced by the other or by a binding-specific encoding.\\n\\n| Domain | Authority / obligation | Observable products |\\n|---|---|---|\\n| Register scalar/image encoding | \\\"'`debruijn_code.cljc` `scalar-class`, `encode-scalar`, `framed`; `debruijn_register_code.cljc` `encode-register-image`, `descriptor-hash`, `register-hash` | R, layout checks, continuation self-consistency. |\\n| Jing canonical CBOR | `dao/jing/cbor.cljc` `encode`/`decode`; `dao/jing.cljc` `canonical-bytes`, `content-hash`, `segment-key` | Stream-binding bytes, owner, seals, profile, module/semantic addresses. |'\\\"''')\\ne22=e22.replace('or hash the ABI node bytes','or hash a handle representation').replace('The private ABI may carry a lone UTF-16 surrogate\\\\\\\\nfor in-machine fidelity; that does not make it hashable by either repository\\\\\\\\ncodec.','In-machine values may retain a lone UTF-16 surrogate; it remains unhashable\\\\\\\\nby either repository codec.').replace('native\\\\\\\\nservice trust','in-instance engine trust')\\ne22=e22.replace('The\\\\\\\\nfacade alone owns private ABI framing/reference expansion; it calls these\\\\\\\\ncrates for identity bytes and hashes.','The\\\\\\\\ninternal facade calls these same crates for identity bytes and hashes.').replace('W0\\\\\\\\nports/verifies these codecs and hashing primitives before native execution\\\\\\\\ngates.','W0\\\\\\\\nfreezes/verifies their oracle vectors before execution gates; W1 proves the\\\\\\\\nshared implementations run inside wasm.').replace('native\\\\\\\\nservice obligations independent of the ABI codec implementation.','in-instance obligations independent of physical stream transport.')\\ne23=between(old,'#### E.2.3 ','### E.3 ')\\ne23=e23.replace('host service','in-instance engine helper').replace('mandatory map barrier','mandatory state-record barrier').replace('embedder arena ownership','in-instance arena ownership').replace('every host import','every internal handle lookup').replace('immutable host snapshots','immutable named-field snapshots')\\ne3='''### E.3 The dao.stream wasm binding\\n\\n**Owner supersession, 2026-10-19 (boundary clarification):** the former\\n\\\"'`yin.vm.engine.host.v1` service protocol (previous name `yin.register.host.v1`)\\nis dissolved, including control-block tables, operation envelopes, TLV tags,\\nexpanded-canonical-bytes comparator, service statuses and NEED_SPACE/refetch.\\nThe earlier performance exception is superseded. This section defines only a\\nphysical binding of [dao.stream](./dao.stream.md); it adds no stream outcomes,\\nsemantic operations, private codec or second ABI.\\n\\n#### E.3.1 Imported adapter surface\\n\\nUse import module `dao.stream` with the following proposed binding names.\\nAll parameters and single results are i32. `p,n` address one canonical Jing\\nCBOR value; `out,cap` denote a disjoint writable response region. The returned\\ni32 is the **encoded response byte length**, never a status number. Decode\\nthat response into the operation'\\\"'s exhaustive outcome map. Zero-copy views\\nmust not outlive the synchronous call or memory growth.\\n\\n| Import | Parameters → result | Existing stream operation |\\n|---|---|---|\\n| \\\"'`create` | `(p,n,out,cap) → length` | `create!` on a creation specification (VM make-stream adapter). |\\n| `attach` | `(p,n,out,cap) → length` | `attach!` on a portable descriptor. |\\n| `descriptor` | `(handle,out,cap) → length` | `descriptor`. |\\n| `cursor` | `(handle,p,n,out,cap) → length` | `cursor` with decoded anchor. |\\n| `next` | `(handle,p,n,out,cap) → length` | `next` with decoded cursor; the poll operation. |\\n| `append` | `(handle,p,n,out,cap) → length` | `append!` with decoded value. |\\n| `close` | `(handle,out,cap) → length` | `close!`. |\\n\\nMake/poll are VM-facing terminology, not additional stream operations. Park,\\nresume, resolve-var, primitive application, state reads, roots, collection and\\nmodule handling have **no imports**: they execute in-instance. Imports never\\nname arbitrary FFI operations. Memory/table provision and module instantiation\\nare runtime linkage/lifecycle mechanics, not semantic service operations.\\n\\nOperational stream handles cannot themselves be canonical data. The binding\\nrepresents the handle field in a successful create/attach outcome by a checked,\\ntask-local i32 slot, encoded as a Jing integer; decoding reconstructs the local\\nhandle resource before the engine consumes the outcome. This is the physical\\nrepresentation of an existing handle, not a guest stream identity or a portable\\ncapability. Keep this binding handle namespace distinct from ValueRefs, check\\nownership/lifetime, and never publish it in a stream value, continuation or\\nmodule payload. Portable descriptors/cursors and all other outcome fields use\\ntheir actual canonical data. Repeated reads remain non-destructive; the adapter\\nhas no privileged reader and introduces no callbacks.\\n\\n#### E.3.2 Outcome and buffer discipline\\n\\nThe exhaustive outcome sets and required keys are those of [dao.stream.md](./dao.stream.md),\\nCreating and Attaching, Cursors, Reading, Writing and Closing. Preserve optional\\nqualified diagnostics and retry declarations. In particular append returns\\nok/full/invalid-value/closed/refused/transport-error; next returns\\nok/blocked/end/gap/cursor-mismatch/invalid-cursor/refused/transport-error;\\nclose has only ok. Do not turn ordinary blocking or refusal into a trap.\\n\\nThe engine allocates request/response memory before invoking an adapter. The\\ncomposition declares supported payload/output bounds; W0 establishes a response\\nreservation sufficient for **every** admitted result, including diagnostic and\\nhandle outcomes. Validate pointers, lengths, overflow, overlap and reservation\\nbefore executing an operation. Invalid binding calls are implementation faults,\\nnot new stream statuses. Allocation failure is a qualified local resource limit\\nbefore invocation. No operation may commit and then ask the guest to resize and\\nfetch its response; no retained-response service or semantic replay exists.\\n\\nA full append buffer uses the stream'\\\"'s **full** outcome: nothing appended, keep\\nthe exact pending value and retry through the existing writer wait discipline.\\nResponse capacity is reserved separately; full is not invented for reads or\\nclose. Read-side inability to carry an oversized value follows the stream's\\ntransport-error outcome without advancing the immutable cursor. Admitted limits\\nmust make ordinary outcomes deliverable; test limit failures before mutation.\\nAn append transport-error can mean acceptance is unknown: do not automatically\\nretry unless that transport declares clean failure. Blocking waits stay as pure\\ndata in the in-instance engine; the composition later drives execution again,\\nwithout retaining a wasm activation or application callback.\\n\\n#### E.3.3 Canonical bytes and authority\\n\\nRequests and outcome data use exactly Jing's canonical CBOR (§E.2.2), with its\\nown ordering, numeric carriers, validation and decode/re-encode rules. Length\\nparameters delimit one CBOR value; no TLV or alternate framing wraps it.\\nCanonical maps/sets use repository ordering, never task handles or arena ids.\\nRegister image checksums still use the register codec. Buffer access grants no\\npermission to pass internal Values, private engine snapshots or trusted wrappers\\nacross custody gates. Stream history records effects/facts, not register loads,\\nroot materialization or other machine-internal traffic.\\n\\n'''\\ne4='''### E.4 Composition adapters and the single in-instance engine\\n\\n#### E.4.1 Execution and embedding responsibilities\\n\\nNode/browser, Wasmtime and Wasmer provide the same stream binding (§E.3).\\nThe composition hosts adapters and their actual transports/bridge interpreters;\\nit does not implement resolve-var, primitives, stores, GC, continuations or the\\nscheduler for wasm. A WASI-based composition may implement transports below the\\nbinding; the VM core imports no WASI. There is no JS dependency in native runs.\\nThe CLJS engine is the reference oracle, not a second production wasm engine.\\n\\nThe in-instance engine owns run-loop/scheduler-round, step budgets, ready/wait\\nqueues and terminal checks. A step performs exactly one guest instruction; run\\nkeeps the original ready-queue/terminal ordering and returns on the same blocked,\\nparked or halted states. Blocking is a normal result. Inspection/test snapshots\\nare immutable data, not an external engine service contract. Runtime start/drive\\nexports may only enter the in-instance driver; they are lifecycle controls, not\\napplication callbacks. No wasm stack survives a wait.\\n\\n#### E.4.2 Single-engine-port rule and Rust reconciliation\\n\\nThe production engine **is** the Rust design's \\\"'`yin-value`, `yin-scalar`,\\n`yin-image`, `yin-kernel`, `yin-prims`, `yin-engine`, `yin-wire` workspace\\n(`docs/design/yin.vm.rust-kernel.md:1134–1158`), compiled to\\n`wasm32-unknown-unknown` and linked with compiled register bodies. The internal\\nhandle facade adapts values, never reimplements semantics. Native wrappers\\nimplement only stream adapters and runtime mechanics. No parallel engine port\\nis permitted to bypass a crate'\\\"'s missing wasm support.\\n\\n“One transition implementation, two dispatchers” applies to the Rust interpreter,\\ncompiled wasm bodies and future LLVM helpers (\\\"'`docs/design/yin.vm.rust-kernel.md:991–995, 1308–1311`).\\nGenerated control flow uses shared transition helpers for call/return/restore/\\neffects; no wrapper duplicates them. CLJS necessarily has a language-port\\nimplementation: it remains the shared-kit oracle, not literally the same binary.\\nCheck-then-commit, refused-step equality and per-machine fault containment remain\\nmandatory (`docs/design/yin.vm.rust-kernel.md:639–713`). Panics are kernel defects,\\nnot success outcomes. W0/W1 must prove trap containment with this wasm profile;\\nnever rely on wasm exceptions or unwinding. Safe checked slices/Value APIs and\\nthe Rust design'\\\"'s reviewed boundary crates retain the unsafe-code boundary\\n(lines 1154–1158).\\n\\nRust revision 2's design-only status is unchanged. This wasm plan supersedes its\\nDEFERRED wasm artifact item (lines 1294–1300), not the K3 review gate for K4 or\\nunrelated standalone-node/transports work. The required K-phase library subset\\nis now a **wasm build dependency**, including Level-2 engine work by W3–W4.\\nIt does not authorize a separate full K5 node. A remains sole code identity;\\nR is executable checksum despite older Rust topology wording (lines 282–318,\\n997–1011). u32 Rust indices retain checked host limits (434–448, 1245–1246)\\nagainst 64-bit stored pc coordinates, never truncation. Rust public step still\\ninterprets (677–678); generated one-step mode is a separate dispatcher.\\nShared-reference traversal stabilization below remains an explicitly scoped\\nwasm prerequisite outside the Rust phases' CLJC-edit exclusion (93–95, 1164–1165).\\n\\n'''\\ntrav=between(old,'#### E.4.3 ','#### E.4.4 ')\\ntrav=trav.replace('service traversal','engine traversal').replace('native service','in-instance engine').replace('canonicalizing only the final ABI bytes','canonicalizing only final stream bytes')\\na=trav.index('For currently unordered traversal sites'); b=trav.index('| Site (existing symbols')\\ntrav=trav[:a]+'''For currently unordered traversal sites, use canonical **Jing CBOR bytes** for\\ncanonical keys/elements, never arena numbering or encoded resource-lifting output.\\nFor internal values outside Jing's domain, retain non-minting structural\\ncomparison within the value implementation; W0 must freeze its type/field order\\nand cross-language vectors before enabling those fixtures. It is an in-memory\\ncomparison, not a third serialized codec or a claim those values are portable.\\nThis replaces the dissolved private-byte comparator by the owner ruling.\\nPrepare ordering before id-minting traversal. Retain vector/list order, ordered\\nexports, wait/ready FIFO and first-occurrence deduplication. Preserve existing\\nexplicit \\\"'`sort-by str` cursor/module-store order and `sort-by key` image order,\\nwith cross-host fixtures; do not replace already-specified ordering.\\n\\n'\\\"'''+trav[b:]\\ne44=between(old,'#### E.4.4 ','### E.5 ')\\ne44=e44.replace('host rules','in-instance rules').replace('before adapter restore','before in-instance restore').replace('stale\\\\\\\\nservice-mailbox data','internal\\\\\\\\npending-operation scratch').replace(\\\\\\\"same host's interpreter\\\\\\\",\\\\\\\"in-instance register interpreter\\\\\\\")\\ne51=between(old,'### E.5 ','#### E.5.2 ')\\ne51=e51.replace('engine execution host','in-instance engine').replace('\\\"'`yin.vm.engine.host.v1`'\\\"\\\\\\\\ntransport','dao.stream wasm binding').replace('ABI-specific','Binding-specific').replace('serialized ABI\\\\\\\\ntraffic','canonical stream-binding\\\\\\\\ntraffic')\\ne51=e51.replace('One manifest drives **three execution lanes: R4 CLJS reference, Rust\\\\\\\\nkernel/interpreter, wasm**.', 'One manifest drives the **CLJS oracle and the in-wasm Rust engine under Node,\\\\\\\\nWasmtime and Wasmer**, plus the shared native Rust interpreter and planned LLVM\\\\\\\\nlanes as their phases become available.')\\ne51=e51.replace('Rust interpreter\\\\\\\\ncoverage advances with each W slice; it cannot be omitted because native wasm\\\\\\\\nhappens to pass.', 'The in-instance Rust interpreter and compiled dispatcher both pass each W\\\\\\\\nslice; native Rust phase gates remain independently required for their claims.')\\ne51=e51.replace('Test\\\\\\\\nprimitive implementations and scripted transports exist in both native and\\\\\\\\nCLJS compositions','Test\\\\\\\\nprimitive implementations exist in the in-instance crates and CLJS oracle;\\\\\\\\nscripted transports exist in every composition')\\ne52='''#### E.5.2 Codec, identity and traversal determinism gates\\n\\nBoth repository codecs (§E.2.2) must match frozen bytes/hashes across CLJS and\\nin-wasm Rust: R (including empty/combined images), owner, seal, primitive-profile\\nand manifest addresses are bit-identical for equal inputs/secrets. Reuse existing\\nJing/scalar/image corpora without regenerating goldens. W1 verifies load/profiles;\\nW2 recomputes continuation hashes; W3 checks minted seals; W4 checks admitted\\nmodules/layouts/slices. Jing alone governs binding canonicalization, numeric\\ncarriers, NaNs, negative zero, collision/refusal and decode/re-encode identity.\\nInternal noncanonical values keep their in-machine fidelity and existing wire\\nrefusals; no private transport codec is required to compare them structurally.\\n\\nTraversal gates (§E.4.3) compare exact \\\"'`:yin.k/c-N`, stream/cursor ids/seals,\\n`:yin.k/entries`, `:cells`, `:available`, counters/resource maps and first-error\\norder. Vary insertion history, small/large CLJS map representations, nested maps/\\nsets, repeated references, module stores and multiple invalid resource keys.\\nPrivate handle renaming never excuses a guest-visible difference.\\n\\n'\\\"'''\\npins=between(old,'| Tool | Pin / scope | Evidence |','W0 must build these pins together')\\npins=pins.replace('host service/wrapper builds, not guest wasm generation','in-instance engine target \\\"'`wasm32-unknown-unknown`, plus runtime wrappers'\\\"')\\ne53='''#### E.5.3 W0 oracle, toolchain, wasm32 and binding gates\\n\\n**Oracle first:** consume/extend the R4-exported K0 kit before trusting any new\\nengine or compiler. Preserve fixture schema and original goldens. Pin the\\nreference revision plus scoped traversal delta. W0 probes wasm32 compilability;\\nW1 cannot pass until the actual required Rust crates link and execute inside\\nwasm. An unavailable crate dependency blocks the lane; no host service fallback.\\n\\nThese are inherited reproducibility targets, not claims of installed/tested tools:\\n\\n'''+pins+'''W0 builds/probes the pins together, records checksums, target triples, Java 21\\nvendor/build and transitive lockfiles. Any changed pin requires an explicit\\nrecord and rerun. Binaryen is absent initially. Record browser versions on\\nqualification. The Rust/LLVM linker used for the engine must be pinned as part\\nof the Rust build; deterministic WAT emission/WABT remains the body toolchain.\\n\\nProbe core wasm 1.0 plus mutable globals and original function tables: imported\\nmemory, checked bounds/byte order, i32 calls, i64 memory fields, active segments,\\ncall_indirect, memory growth/view refresh and shared task memory across image\\ncomponents. Audit **engine dependencies and final linked artifacts**, not only\\nemitted bodies, against the allowlist. No WASI, reference types, bulk memory,\\nthreads, exceptions, tail-call extension or WasmGC may slip in via Rust defaults.\\nEvery extension is a separately probed profile/cache key. Test embedding\\nharnesses, not a bare CLI unable to supply imports.\\n\\nW0 binding probes run on Node and Wasmtime (Wasmer smoke probe where available;\\nfull suites mandatory by W5): canonical request/outcome round trips, all declared\\nstream outcomes, handle ownership, malformed CBOR, bounds/overlap/overflow,\\ninsufficient **pre-operation** reservation, full-before-append and no mutation\\non rejected binding calls. W3 repeats against real FFI/writer/reader paths and\\nproves once-only append after full. Test unknown-acceptance transport errors\\nwithout blind retry. No post-commit resize/refetch protocol exists.\\n\\nSnapshot gates cover all declared fields/extension keys, supplied-state\\nreplacement, old-state replay and immutable snapshots. GC gates force collection\\ninside a helper. Codec probes include both repository encodings, metadata,\\nfloat/integer carriers, canonical collisions, surrogate refusals, deep keys and\\npermuted handle allocation; frozen private-TLV tests are removed by supersession.\\n\\nW/K map: W0=K0 oracle/probes; W1=required K1 engine/value/image builds in wasm;\\nW2=K2 restore plus park subset; W3–W4=required Level-2 engine library coverage;\\nW5=wasm completion on Node/Wasmtime/Wasmer, not full K5; W6=codegen measurements\\non the shared K3 harness, without commissioning K4\\n(\\\"'`docs/design/yin.vm.rust-kernel.md:1167–1182, 1215–1228`). Full register slice\\nacceptance lives in [yin.vm.debruijn.register.md](./yin.vm.debruijn.register.md)\\n§10.5, compiler work in [yang.wasm.md](./yang.wasm.md) §6.1.\\n\\n#### E.5.4 Non-goals and risks\\n\\nNo second engine/value/codec port, new register contract, universal continuation\\ntransport, arbitrary FFI imports, WASI core, threads/shared memory, WasmGC,\\nNaN-boxing, cross-node artifact service or unrelated K4/full-K5 authorization.\\nImmediate JVM/Dart application integration and malicious network wasm are not\\ncompletion claims. W6 starts after correctness; engine-in-guest is not deferred.\\n\\nRisks: wasm32/dependency feature compliance is an early hard gate; shared\\nLevel-2 crates are substantial required work. Preserve persistent snapshots over\\nmutable memory and logical heap roots separately from arena retention. Traps or\\npanics must not poison another task; ambiguous external effects cannot be rolled\\nback/retried as if absent. Cross-runtime buffer/handle lifetimes and dynamic\\nmodule installation need physical-runtime probes. Predicate/codec drift is caught\\nby the oracle kit. Measure in-instance code size, dispatch and collection costs;\\nnone justifies restoring a second semantic service protocol.\\n\\n#### E.5.5 Reference conventions and open items\\n\\nCLJC helper names denote oracle semantics and shared Rust equivalents, not a\\nrequirement to load Clojure inside wasm. Rust-design references preserve the\\noriginal lines 1–1334. Adapter names in §E.3.1 are proposed physical bindings.\\n\\nThe owner ruling resolves the former compiled-call/service split: generated\\ncontrol invokes the one in-instance transition implementation. It also removes\\nthe former response-refetch sequencing question with that protocol. Remaining\\nW0 gates are wasm32 build/profile feasibility, bounded response reservations,\\nand a frozen internal structural ordering for noncanonical values (§E.4.3).\\nDo not invent a third codec to answer the last question.\\n'\\\"'''\\nengine=prefix+'''## Extension (2026-10-19, design only): the in-instance engine and wasm stream binding\\n\\nStatus: design, owner-authorized planning; not implemented.\\n\\nThis extension retains the five-round r5 consensus (gpt-6-astra author,\\ngemini-4-argon reviewer, APPROVE on r5, 2026-10-18) where not superseded.\\n**Owner boundary clarification, 2026-10-19:** engine-in-guest replaces the\\nbody-lane-first service architecture. §E.1/E.2 synchronization and ownership\\nnow operate inside wasm; §E.3 replaces the dissolved protocol; §E.4 hosts only\\nstream adapters outside; §E.5 retains the shared K0/W0 oracle method.\\nHistorical rename \\\"'`yin.register.host.v1` → `yin.vm.engine.host.v1` is provenance,\\nnot a live interface. The performance exception, private third codec/comparator,\\ntransport statuses and deferred W6 engine migration are superseded.\\n\\nAuthorities: [datom.world.md](./datom.world.md), [dao.stream.md](./dao.stream.md),\\n[yin.vm.rust-kernel.md](./yin.vm.rust-kernel.md), [yin.vm.linker.md](./yin.vm.linker.md).\\nCompiler plan: [yang.wasm.md](./yang.wasm.md). Register instance/fixtures/gates:\\n[yin.vm.debruijn.register.md](./yin.vm.debruijn.register.md) §10.\\n\\n'\\\"'''+e1+e21+e22+e23+e3+e4+trav+e44+e51+e52+e53\\npaths['datom.world'].write_text(m); paths['yin.vm.engine'].write_text(engine)\\nprint('wrote master and engine; remaining docs next')\\nPY\\\"\",\"aggregated_output\":\"wrote master and engine; remaining docs next\\n\",\"exit_code\":0,\"status\":\"completed\"}}\n{\"type\":\"item.completed\",\"item\":{\"id\":\"item_12\",\"type\":\"agent_message\",\"text\":\"The master ruling and engine extension now put the Rust engine inside wasm and replace the service protocol with stream adapters. Next I’m updating the compiler plan and slice gates, while preserving the opcode rows, fixture schema, and continuation restore semantics.\\n\"}}\n{\"type\":\"item.started\",\"item\":{\"id\":\"item_13\",\"type\":\"command_execution\",\"command\":\"/bin/zsh -lc \\\"python3 - <<'PY'\\nfrom pathlib import Path\\nD=Path('docs/design'); p=D/'yang.wasm.md'; y=p.read_text(); original=y\\nrpath=D/'yin.vm.debruijn.register.md'; r=rpath.read_text(); ro=r\\nrustpath=D/'yin.vm.rust-kernel.md'; rust=rustpath.read_text(); rusthead=''.join(rust.splitlines(True)[:1334])\\ndef block(t,a,b,new): return t[:t.index(a)]+new+'\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\n'+t[t.index(b):]\\ndef part(t,a,b): return t[t.index(a):t.index(b)]\\n# Yang targeted replacements preserve opcode table, structs, payloads and most attach law.\\ny=y.replace('# yang.wasm: lowering register images to WebAssembly modules','# yang.wasm: compiler implementation plan — register bytecode to WebAssembly')\\ny=block(y,'2026-10-19 decomposition, owner-ruling, and fold notes:','## Authorities','''**Owner supersession, 2026-10-19 (boundary clarification):** this is the\\ncompiler implementation plan for register bytecode → wasm. The earlier\\nbody-lane-first architecture and \\\"'`yin.vm.engine.host.v1` service target\\n(historically renamed from `yin.register.host.v1`) are superseded. Compile the\\nshared Rust engine crates into the instance; imports are only the dao.stream\\nbinding defined in [yin.vm.engine.md](./yin.vm.engine.md) §E.3. Its former\\noperation vocabulary, TLV codec/comparator, service statuses and response-refetch\\nmachinery are deleted. Engine-in-guest is mandatory, not a W6 experiment.\\n\\nThe 2026-10-19 decomposition remains: engine representation/binding/conformance\\nmethod live in [yin.vm.engine.md](./yin.vm.engine.md) §E; register state,\\nfixtures and acceptance gates in [yin.vm.debruijn.register.md](./yin.vm.debruijn.register.md)\\n§10. This plan owns compiler mechanics, not host service implementation.\\nRetired standalone wasm/kit documents remain retired. Unsuperseded r5 semantic\\nlaws, opcode rows, fixture obligations and artifact rules remain binding.'\\\"''')\\ny=y.replace('compiler and target-ABI half of the frozen r5 consensus','compiler half of the r5 consensus, subject to the owner supersession below').replace('the guest-agnostic execution host, \\\"'`yin.vm.engine.host.v1` service protocol, and K0/W0 conformance method'\\\"','the in-instance engine, dao.stream wasm binding, and K0/W0 conformance method')\\ny=block(y,'wasm activation. Target host services','The mandatory feature profile', '''wasm activation. Link the shared Rust engine crates into the instance;\\ncompiled bodies use direct internal calls and shared linear memory (§4).\\nOnly effects/facts cross through the dao.stream binding. Node/browser,\\nWasmtime and Wasmer run the same engine/body binary; the CLJS implementation\\nis the conformance oracle, not the engine of the Node wasm lane.\\n\\nThe body toolchain is a JVM Clojure emitter producing deterministic WAT,\\nassembled with WABT. The engine uses the pinned Rust toolchain targeting\\n\\\"'`wasm32-unknown-unknown`; W0/W1 must demonstrate linking the engine and bodies\\nunder the baseline profile. This is not ClojureScript-to-wasm. Toolchain pins\\nand probes live in [yin.vm.engine.md](./yin.vm.engine.md) §E.5.3; they are\\nacceptance targets, not results claimed here. Wasmtime joins Node from W1;\\nWasmer passes the full suite by W5. JVM/Dart remain reference/regression lanes;\\napplication integration is separate.'\\\"''')\\ny=y.replace('transports/FFI below the same ABI','transports/FFI below the same stream binding').replace('Binaryen is optional after parity; no wasm-ld pipeline is\\\\\\\\nrequired for generated guest code. Native embedder builds have their own pinned native\\\\\\\\ntoolchain.','Binaryen is optional after parity. Engine linking uses the pinned Rust/LLVM\\\\\\\\nlinker; W0 records that build and final-module feature audit.').replace('direct reuse in CLJS and equivalently tested native\\\\\\\\ntransitions','oracle behavior in CLJS and equivalently tested in-instance Rust\\\\\\\\ntransitions')\\ny=y.replace('native wasm embedder needs Level-2 engine services','in-instance wasm engine needs Level-2 engine services').replace('native standalone wasm parity','in-instance wasm parity').replace('native facade checks','internal facade checks')\\ny=block(y,'reference is an arena entry. Numeric computation','### 2.2 Private linear-memory layout', '''reference is an arena entry. Numeric operations run in the linked \\\"'`yin-prims`\\nand shared Value implementation, matching the CLJS oracle'\\\"'s declared carriers,\\nfloat behavior, equality and metadata. Only references 0/1 are false.\\n\\nThe per-task arena is inside wasm linear memory. The descriptor table has kind,\\ngeneration, task-local slot and optional machine-record offset. The internal\\nhandle↔Value facade owns the shared Rust Values through safe Clone/Drop; it does\\nnot serialize them between body and engine. No JS/native pointer is a ValueRef.\\nSee [yin.vm.engine.md](./yin.vm.engine.md) §E.2.1–§E.2.3 for ownership, collection,\\ntarget-layout gates and the **two repository codecs**. Internal values retain\\ntheir fidelity even when existing custody/encoding predicates refuse transport.\\n\\nHandles keep codegen register locals uniform and avoid externref or i64 host-call\\narguments. The cost is arena indirection, not a service crossing per primitive.\\nA scalar fast path remains a measured optimization with explicit numeric laws;\\nit cannot change the public image contract.''')\\ny=y.replace('ABI version, run status','Internal layout version, run status').replace('host-state token','engine-state token').replace('in the host payload','in the materialized payload').replace('Kind/generation/host slot','Kind/generation/arena slot').replace('Host slot contains','Arena slot contains')\\ny=y.replace('embedder’s persistent map','in-instance engine’s persistent map').replace('the map and updates are addressable through ABI value\\\\\\\\ndefinitions/state messages on every runtime.','the map and updates use direct internal helpers on every runtime.')\\ny=block(y,'The instance exports conceptual','Emission is byte-deterministic:', '''The linked instance exposes lifecycle run/drive entry points and test/inspection\\nstep/read-state/snapshot-install hooks, not semantic service imports. Its external\\nfunctions are the stream adapters specified once in\\n[yin.vm.engine.md](./yin.vm.engine.md) §E.3. Components share one task-owned,\\nnon-shared wasm32 memory and original function table, with immutable i32 base\\noffsets where needed. Memory/table provision and internal component helper\\nimports are linkage mechanics. Final external semantic imports are allowlisted\\nto the stream binding. No host-call signature passes i64, externref, Rust\\npointers, JS values, strings or multi-value results; i64 pc/numeric bytes live\\nin memory and core i64 computation remains allowed inside wasm.\\n\\nTRANSFER/YIELDED/BLOCKED/PARKED/HALTED/ERROR below denote internal driver control,\\nnot serialized service statuses or adapter results. Public state preserves exact\\nflags, value and queues. **Blocking is a normal return, never a trap.** Memory\\nor validation traps are backend faults, not blocked or FFI error envelopes.''')\\ny=y.replace('emitter/ABI version','emitter/internal-layout version')\\ny=y.replace('retained as a W6 optimization axis with services moved into wasm if serialized crossing cost dominates.','available as the in-instance interpreter fallback and a W6 codegen comparison; engine placement is already fixed.')\\ny=block(y,'is an implementation dispatch, not the guest call stack. On HOST_SERVICE','Consequently flattening', '''is an implementation dispatch, not the guest call stack. State-consuming helper\\nsites return to a shared **internal helper thunk** after materialization. The\\nthunk directly calls the linked engine using the same memory, then reloads the\\ncommitted state and continues or returns blocked/error. This single replaceable\\nsite centralizes synchronization and lets later codegen inline proven-safe calls;\\nit has no serialized mailbox or external service protocol. No guest activation\\nremains across an asynchronous wait. The same body/engine binaries run on every\\nembedder.''')\\ny=y.replace('several host service transitions implement it','several internal engine transitions implement it')\\ny=block(y,'Here \\\"'`r` means ValueRef local'\\\"','| Opcode | Lowering and observable behavior |', '''Here \\\"'`r` means ValueRef local and `next` means pc+1. The retained r5 rows use\\n“Host”/“host fn” to name the oracle'\\\"'s kernel-helper/primitive cases: in this\\narchitecture these **all execute inside the linked Rust engine**, through the\\ninternal thunk (§4), never through an imported engine service. Pending scratch\\nretains already-read operands so continuation/re-entry cannot repeat effects.\\nThe rows specify transitions; generated dispatch invokes shared helpers rather\\nthan copying semantic transition implementations.''')\\ny=y.replace('### 3.4 Exact host-function effect completion','### 3.4 Exact primitive (oracle host-function) effect completion').replace('Host-side payload materialization','In-instance payload materialization')\\ny=block(y,'## 4. Target interface specification:','## 5. Per-image-row compilation', '''## 4. Target linkage: in-instance engine and dao.stream binding\\n\\nThe compiler links \\\"'`yin-value`, `yin-scalar`, `yin-image`, `yin-kernel`,\\n`yin-prims`, `yin-engine` and `yin-wire` for `wasm32-unknown-unknown`. The\\nbinding'\\\"'s operations, signatures, Jing payloads and stream outcomes have one\\nhome: [yin.vm.engine.md](./yin.vm.engine.md) §E.3. The compiler emits no private\\nservice protocol, codec, transport-status decoder or response-refetch loop.\\nThe owner boundary clarification of 2026-10-19 supersedes the former target\\ninterface and its earlier performance-based exception.\\n\\nCompiled bodies and engine functions reach each other by **direct calls and\\nshared linear memory**, internal linkage within the task's instance group.\\nThe shared helper thunk is the one replaceable generated call site. It flushes\\nthe eight register fields, calls the owning crate's transition, and adopts the\\nreturned state before opcode-specific completion (§3.4). Full fields and barriers\\nare owned by [yin.vm.debruijn.register.md](./yin.vm.debruijn.register.md) §10.1\\nand [yin.vm.engine.md](./yin.vm.engine.md) §E.1; they are not import messages.\\n\\nThe compiler owns pc dispatch, operand access, branch structure and entry maps.\\nShared helpers own call binding/return/restore, ownership, primitives, stores,\\ncollection and effects. This resolves the old compiled-call/service division\\nwithout duplicating transitions. The in-instance interpreter reaches those same\\nhelpers. An imported helper between separately instantiated linked components\\nis private bundle linkage, not a composition-provided semantic operation.\\n\\nOnly the in-instance engine's stream adapter layer calls external semantic\\nimports. It encodes canonical Jing data and consumes the exact stream outcomes.\\nResolve-var, frame/root materialization and scheduler operations never become\\nstream history. The compiler must preserve the thunk barrier on every path\\nthat can collect, park, attach or return a snapshot. External bindings carry no\\nlive stack frames, engine records or ValueRef definitions.''')\\ny=y.replace('the same \\\"'`yin.vm.engine.host.v1` ABI memory and services.'\\\"','the same task memory/table and linked in-instance engine helpers.')\\ny=block(y,'Dynamic instantiation/attachment occurs outside an active exchange','The same component bytes', '''Dynamic code instantiation uses the runtime's module lifecycle at a quiescent,\\nmaterialized boundary, never a reentrant semantic service call. Core wasm cannot\\ninstantiate new modules itself: the composition installs derived cached body\\nfunctions via the embedding API; this physical linkage does not perform guest\\nattachment/admission. The in-instance engine owns that transaction and its\\nstate publication. Without a ready artifact, execute the attached image in the\\nsame in-instance interpreter; cache preparation cannot change guest semantics.\\nArtifact preparation/availability is node-local, never a portable module field.\\n''') if 'The same component bytes' in y else y\\n# previous replacement introduced paragraph spacing only; preserve remainder\\ny=y.replace('Host-side \\\"'`spawn-module`'\\\"','In-instance \\\"'`spawn-module`'\\\"').replace('wasm ABI/emitter version','wasm layout/emitter version').replace('translator and ABI versions','translator and internal-layout versions').replace(\\\\\\\"same host’s register interpreter\\\\\\\",\\\\\\\"same in-instance register interpreter\\\\\\\")\\ny=block(y,'For the lowering pipeline specifically:','### 6.2 Lowering non-goals and risks', '''For the lowering pipeline specifically (acceptance detail is in register §10.5):\\n\\n- **W0 — Oracle and feasibility:** export/extend K0 from CLJS first; freeze admitted images/R/traces/refusals; probe crate wasm32 compilation, linker/profile, internal layout and stream-binding bounds. Pin deterministic emitter/assembler. No host-service fallback.\\n- **W1 — In-instance pure execution:** link the required Rust engine/primitive/value/image crates and compiled body dispatcher; prove actual wasm32 execution and single-step parity under Node + Wasmtime.\\n- **W2 — Continuations:** compile sparse capture/restore, internal continuation calls and park/resume against the linked engine; retain layout/custody/refusal and snapshot gates.\\n- **W3 — Effects:** use Jing stream adapters with the in-instance scheduler, GC, custody and two-step FFI; test full/blocked and once-only retry on Node + Wasmtime.\\n- **W4 — Modules:** compile per-image rows, relocate once, install artifacts at materialized boundaries, preserve attach/spawn/store/GC law and CLJS↔wasm module-slice parity.\\n- **W5 — Complete lane:** all 23 opcodes and complete W1–W5 full traces on Node, Wasmtime and Wasmer; deterministic binaries and external import/profile audit. Wasmer's formerly W4 full-suite gate is superseded by this owner-directed schedule, not removed.\\n- **W6 — Codegen optimization only:** measure compile latency, binary size, dispatch/indirection, stack depth, arena/heap retention, call/suspension, stream-byte/copy time and end-to-end time. Compare compiled bodies with the same in-instance interpreter; coalesce/structure blocks, reduce redundant spills or add pinned Binaryen only with full three-runtime parity. No engine migration or architecture-selection experiment remains. K3 may use the same benchmark harness; K4 still requires owner review.\\n\\nThe ruling supersedes the former W0–W5 private-mailbox work and W6 service\\nmigration. Correctness gates survive in their in-instance or stream-binding\\nform; two repository codecs and the shared oracle remain mandatory.''')\\ny=y.replace('restore/call helper names are existing source semantics, whereas exchange/fetch-response, ABI\\\\\\\\nstatuses and all native implementation entry names are proposed protocol vocabulary.','restore/call helper names are oracle semantics; planned internal Rust helper and\\\\\\\\nphysical binding names are not claims of implemented symbols.').replace('New ABI names and native entry names','New binding names and internal entry names')\\ny=y[:y.index('## Open items')]+'''## Open items\\n\\nThe owner ruling resolves the former compiled-call/service split (§4) and\\nremoves the response-refetch question with the dissolved protocol. W0 must\\nprove crate/linker wasm32 feasibility and the baseline feature audit. The\\nengine's remaining binding-reservation and noncanonical internal-ordering gates\\nare in [yin.vm.engine.md](./yin.vm.engine.md) §E.5.5. No new protocol is a fallback.\\n'''\\n# Register extension only\\nidx=r.index('## 10. Wasm execution instance'); prefix=r[:idx]; ext=r[idx:]\\next=block(ext,'> Owner rulings and decomposition lineage','Authorities and companion specifications', '''> Owner boundary clarification, 2026-10-19, supersedes the body-lane-first\\n> architecture and historical \\\"'`yin.vm.engine.host.v1` service protocol (formerly\\n> `yin.register.host.v1`). The Rust engine now executes inside wasm; eight-field\\n> synchronization is internal, and external imports bind only dao.stream.\\n> Private TLV/comparator/status/refetch machinery and W6 engine migration are\\n> deleted. Fixtures and register transition semantics survive unchanged.\\n> Wasmer'\\\"'s full-suite gate moves from W4 to W5 under the revised schedule.\\n>\\n> Earlier decomposition/fold provenance remains: engine method/binding live in\\n> engine §E; this section holds register state, fixtures and gates. The dated\\n> correction of historical \\\"'`:store-updates` remains: ready entries merge\\n> `:resource-updates` into private `:resources`, refusing non-keyword keys with\\n> `{:rule :resource-update-key}`.'\\\"''')\\next=ext.replace('the guest-agnostic execution host, \\\"'`yin.vm.engine.host.v1` service protocol'\\\"','the in-instance engine and dao.stream wasm binding').replace('lowerings targeting \\\"'`yin.vm.engine.host.v1`'\\\"','lowerings linked to the in-instance engine')\\next=block(ext,'This section defines the **register-machine instance**','#### 10.1.1 ', '''This is the register instance (contract version 4 / \\\\\\\"r2\\\\\\\", 23 opcodes) of the\\nin-instance engine in [yin.vm.engine.md](./yin.vm.engine.md) §E.1–§E.5.\\n[yang.wasm.md](./yang.wasm.md) compiles admitted register images and links the\\nsame Rust engine into wasm under Node/Wasmtime/Wasmer. The CLJS kernel is the\\noracle. Arena/Value facade and both repository codecs live inside the instance;\\nonly dao.stream adapters (§E.3) cross into the composition. The single-port rule\\nand Rust reconciliations remain §E.4.''')\\next=ext.replace('on\\\\\\\\nthe VM state map:','on\\\\\\\\nthe authoritative in-instance state record (the oracle uses a VM map):')\\next=ext.replace('**Public entry / host-to-wasm:**','**Engine-to-body entry:**').replace('accept the supplied map as authoritative','accept the supplied named-field snapshot as authoritative').replace('**Wasm-to-host:**','**Body-to-engine:**').replace('**Host completion:** the returned host state','**Engine completion:** the returned engine state').replace('current opcode\\\\\\\\'s specified post-service changes','current opcode\\\\\\\\'s specified post-helper changes').replace('pre-service header','pre-helper header').replace('before a host service','before an engine helper').replace('after service completion','after helper completion').replace('before the adapter\\\\\\\\nreceives its result','before the caller\\\\\\\\nreceives its result').replace('before every return to the host','before every return to the engine driver').replace('map-based\\\\\\\\n\\\"'`:registers`'\\\"','state-record\\\\\\\\n\\\"'`:registers`'\\\"').replace('Keep the record\\\\\\\\'s protocol implementations so \\\"'`module/gc-roots` can inspect the current map. Dense memory synchronization without map synchronization is insufficient.'\\\"','Preserve equivalent \\\"'`module/gc-roots` dispatch over the current record. Dense shadow synchronization without state-record synchronization is insufficient.'\\\"')\\next=ext.replace('IDs or tag-\\\"'`13` arena references'\\\"','IDs or arena references').replace('Expose one adapter restore','Implement one in-instance restore')\\next=block(ext,'Same-layout native payloads with transportable values','If a future authorized transport', '''Same-layout native payloads with admissible values round-trip between reference\\nand wasm execution through conformance/inspection facilities. Inside one task,\\ntrusted wrappers remain arena Values; there is no serialized handle service.\\nThis grants no external portability. The actual module/linker path retains\\n\\\"'`:yin.k/non-portable` / `:non-canonicalizable` continuation refusal in\\n`engine/encoder`. Only admitted canonical stream values use\\n[yin.vm.engine.md](./yin.vm.engine.md) §E.3.'\\\"''')\\next=block(ext,\\\\\\\"table are specified in [\\\"'`yang.wasm.md`\\\",'\\\"'Under the \\\"'`spawn-module` isolation rules'\\\"', '''table are specified in [yang.wasm.md](./yang.wasm.md) §5. The composition's\\nruntime lifecycle installs derived body functions only at materialized boundaries;\\nthe **in-instance engine** owns admission/attachment and publishes its layout.\\nMissing artifacts use its own register interpreter. This is runtime linkage,\\nnot an imported engine service. \\\"'`reset` preserves offset rows; `load-image`\\nreplaces code space and resets execution, so old membership checks can fail.'\\\"''')\\next=ext.replace('\\\"'`yin.vm.engine.host.v1` ABI/emitter version'\\\"','internal layout/emitter version').replace(\\\\\\\"same host's register interpreter\\\\\\\",\\\\\\\"in-instance register interpreter\\\\\\\")\\n# fixture section byte preserved\\nfixture=part(ro,'### 10.4 ','### 10.5 ')\\next=block(ext,'### 10.5 Register-specific','#### 10.5.1 ', '''### 10.5 Register-specific W0–W6 implementation slices and acceptance gates\\n\\nThe node-lane schedule replaces private-service-first staging by owner ruling,\\n2026-10-19. Toolchain pins, oracle-first K0/W0, two-codec determinism, wasm32\\ncompilability and dao.stream binding probes live in\\n[yin.vm.engine.md](./yin.vm.engine.md) §E.5.2–§E.5.3. Compiler work lives in\\n[yang.wasm.md](./yang.wasm.md) §6.1. Each gate below retains the existing\\nregister acceptance assertions, now against the in-instance engine.''')\\next=ext.replace('#### 10.5.1 W0 — Register admission, codec, and eight-field synchronization gates','#### 10.5.1 W0 — Oracle first, admission, wasm32 feasibility and internal state')\\next=ext.replace('Acceptance: all semantic goldens retain','First export/extend K0 from the CLJS oracle; probe the shared crates on\\\\\\\\n\\\"'`wasm32-unknown-unknown` with the final-module feature allowlist and adapter'\\\"\\\\\\\\nreservation/handle/CBOR tests. No service-library substitute is allowed.\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\nAcceptance: all semantic goldens retain',1).replace('byte-message equivalents','internal snapshot equivalents')\\next=ext.replace('step/ABI traces; provide those services through the shared Rust crates and\\\\\\\\nrequire the Rust interpreter to pass the same pure fixtures.','step/stream traces. The required shared Rust crates must actually compile, link\\\\\\\\nand run inside wasm; require their interpreter dispatcher to pass the same pure\\\\\\\\nfixtures. Failure of wasm32/profile feasibility blocks W1.').replace('scheduler/event/ABI traces','scheduler/event/stream-binding traces').replace('native scheduler, custody, collection and\\\\\\\\ntwo-step FFI services','in-instance scheduler, custody, collection and\\\\\\\\ntwo-step FFI').replace('typed effect mailbox completion','typed effect completion inside the engine')\\next=block(ext,'W3 also forces \\\"'`NEED_SPACE`'\\\"','#### 10.5.5 ', '''W3 repeats W0 binding probes against real effects: full-before-append, response\\nreservation before execution, all stream terminal/error outcomes, bounds/CBOR/\\nhandle failures with no accidental mutation, and no automatic retry after an\\nunknown-acceptance transport error. Full writer wake converts once to the FFI\\nreader: no second append/id/GC/resource update. Compare resulting records and\\ncanonical stream bytes on Node and Wasmtime; Wasmer repeats by W5. There is\\nno response-refetch protocol. Internal gensym/park/GC never cross adapters.''')\\next=ext.replace('module/state/ABI traces','module/state/stream traces').replace('native admission, module stores and resource/closure transport. Bring up Wasmer\\\\\\\\nwith the same native service library and run W1–W4 suites before W5.','in-instance admission, module stores and resource/closure transport. Prepare\\\\\\\\nthe Wasmer binding; its complete suite is required by W5.').replace('**W4 does not pass until Wasmer passes the entire W1–W4 full-trace suite** on\\\\\\\\nthe identical binaries and its native service, alongside Node and Wasmtime.','Node and Wasmtime must pass the identical binaries now; Wasmer must pass the\\\\\\\\nentire W1–W4 suite as part of W5 (dated owner schedule supersession above).')\\next=ext.replace('native↔CLJS module-slice','in-wasm↔CLJS module-slice').replace('receiving native hosts','receiving in-instance engines')\\next=ext.replace('with Node/CLJS, Wasmtime and\\\\\\\\nWasmer adapters','with Node, Wasmtime and\\\\\\\\nWasmer stream adapters').replace('\\\"'`yin.vm.engine.host.v1` ABI versioning'\\\"','the dao.stream binding/profile and internal linkage versioning').replace('byte-identical artifacts, \\\"'`yin.vm.engine.host.v1`'\\\"\\\\\\\\nimport allowlist, malformed ABI/overflow/\\\"'`NEED_SPACE`/repeated-fetch handling,'\\\"','byte-identical artifacts, dao.stream external\\\\\\\\nimport allowlist, malformed CBOR/bounds/overflow/reservation/full handling,')\\next=block(ext,'Keep Node + Wasmtime + Wasmer full-trace parity mandatory for every\\\\\\\\noptimization.','Register-specific non-goals', '''Keep Node + Wasmtime + Wasmer full-trace parity mandatory for every\\noptimization. Measure compilation latency, binary size, dispatch/indirection,\\nnative stack depth, arena/heap retention, call/suspension, stream bytes/copy/codec\\ncost and end-to-end time against the same in-instance register interpreter.\\nOnly then coalesce/structure blocks, reduce redundant spills or use pinned\\nBinaryen passes ([yang.wasm.md](./yang.wasm.md) §6.1). Re-run all parity and\\nprofile gates. Engine placement is settled; W6 is **codegen optimization only**,\\nnot a service-migration or alternative architecture experiment. K3 may measure\\nwasm on the shared harness; this does not commission K4. Making wasm work is\\nnot evidence it is faster.''')\\next=ext[:ext.index('**Reference conventions and open items:**')]+'''**Reference conventions and open items:** follow\\n[yin.vm.engine.md](./yin.vm.engine.md) §E.5.5 and\\n[yang.wasm.md](./yang.wasm.md), Reference conventions / Open items. The former\\nservice-call split and response-refetch questions are superseded by the owner\\nruling. Remaining W0 feasibility/binding/ordering gates do not change the\\nregister fixture schema or restore semantics.\\n'''\\nassert part(ext,'### 10.4 ','### 10.5 ')==fixture\\nassert part(y,'| Opcode | Lowering','### 3.4 ')==part(original,'| Opcode | Lowering','### 3.4 ')\\nassert part(y,'### 3.2 ','### 3.3 ').replace('internal engine','host service')==part(original,'### 3.2 ','### 3.3 ')\\nr=prefix+ext\\nrust=rust.replace('§E.1–§E.5, guest-agnostic execution host, \\\"'`yin.vm.engine.host.v1` ABI, and K0/W0 conformance method'\\\"','§E.1–§E.5, in-instance engine, dao.stream wasm binding, and K0/W0 conformance method')\\nrust+='2026-10-19 owner boundary clarification: the wasm plan now depends on compiling the K-phase Rust crates to wasm32-unknown-unknown as its in-instance engine; the composition supplies only dao.stream adapters. This pointer supersedes the earlier footer’s service-protocol description; revision 2 design-only status, file name and coordinates 1–1334 remain unchanged.\\\\\\\\n'\\nassert ''.join(rust.splitlines(True)[:1334])==rusthead\\np.write_text(y); rpath.write_text(r); rustpath.write_text(rust)\\nprint('wrote compiler/register/rust pointers; asserted exact 23 opcode rows, fixture schema, rust prefix')\\nPY\\\"\",\"aggregated_output\":\"\",\"exit_code\":null,\"status\":\"in_progress\"}}\n{\"type\":\"item.completed\",\"item\":{\"id\":\"item_13\",\"type\":\"command_execution\",\"command\":\"/bin/zsh -lc \\\"python3 - <<'PY'\\nfrom pathlib import Path\\nD=Path('docs/design'); p=D/'yang.wasm.md'; y=p.read_text(); original=y\\nrpath=D/'yin.vm.debruijn.register.md'; r=rpath.read_text(); ro=r\\nrustpath=D/'yin.vm.rust-kernel.md'; rust=rustpath.read_text(); rusthead=''.join(rust.splitlines(True)[:1334])\\ndef block(t,a,b,new): return t[:t.index(a)]+new+'\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\n'+t[t.index(b):]\\ndef part(t,a,b): return t[t.index(a):t.index(b)]\\n# Yang targeted replacements preserve opcode table, structs, payloads and most attach law.\\ny=y.replace('# yang.wasm: lowering register images to WebAssembly modules','# yang.wasm: compiler implementation plan — register bytecode to WebAssembly')\\ny=block(y,'2026-10-19 decomposition, owner-ruling, and fold notes:','## Authorities','''**Owner supersession, 2026-10-19 (boundary clarification):** this is the\\ncompiler implementation plan for register bytecode → wasm. The earlier\\nbody-lane-first architecture and \\\"'`yin.vm.engine.host.v1` service target\\n(historically renamed from `yin.register.host.v1`) are superseded. Compile the\\nshared Rust engine crates into the instance; imports are only the dao.stream\\nbinding defined in [yin.vm.engine.md](./yin.vm.engine.md) §E.3. Its former\\noperation vocabulary, TLV codec/comparator, service statuses and response-refetch\\nmachinery are deleted. Engine-in-guest is mandatory, not a W6 experiment.\\n\\nThe 2026-10-19 decomposition remains: engine representation/binding/conformance\\nmethod live in [yin.vm.engine.md](./yin.vm.engine.md) §E; register state,\\nfixtures and acceptance gates in [yin.vm.debruijn.register.md](./yin.vm.debruijn.register.md)\\n§10. This plan owns compiler mechanics, not host service implementation.\\nRetired standalone wasm/kit documents remain retired. Unsuperseded r5 semantic\\nlaws, opcode rows, fixture obligations and artifact rules remain binding.'\\\"''')\\ny=y.replace('compiler and target-ABI half of the frozen r5 consensus','compiler half of the r5 consensus, subject to the owner supersession below').replace('the guest-agnostic execution host, \\\"'`yin.vm.engine.host.v1` service protocol, and K0/W0 conformance method'\\\"','the in-instance engine, dao.stream wasm binding, and K0/W0 conformance method')\\ny=block(y,'wasm activation. Target host services','The mandatory feature profile', '''wasm activation. Link the shared Rust engine crates into the instance;\\ncompiled bodies use direct internal calls and shared linear memory (§4).\\nOnly effects/facts cross through the dao.stream binding. Node/browser,\\nWasmtime and Wasmer run the same engine/body binary; the CLJS implementation\\nis the conformance oracle, not the engine of the Node wasm lane.\\n\\nThe body toolchain is a JVM Clojure emitter producing deterministic WAT,\\nassembled with WABT. The engine uses the pinned Rust toolchain targeting\\n\\\"'`wasm32-unknown-unknown`; W0/W1 must demonstrate linking the engine and bodies\\nunder the baseline profile. This is not ClojureScript-to-wasm. Toolchain pins\\nand probes live in [yin.vm.engine.md](./yin.vm.engine.md) §E.5.3; they are\\nacceptance targets, not results claimed here. Wasmtime joins Node from W1;\\nWasmer passes the full suite by W5. JVM/Dart remain reference/regression lanes;\\napplication integration is separate.'\\\"''')\\ny=y.replace('transports/FFI below the same ABI','transports/FFI below the same stream binding').replace('Binaryen is optional after parity; no wasm-ld pipeline is\\\\\\\\nrequired for generated guest code. Native embedder builds have their own pinned native\\\\\\\\ntoolchain.','Binaryen is optional after parity. Engine linking uses the pinned Rust/LLVM\\\\\\\\nlinker; W0 records that build and final-module feature audit.').replace('direct reuse in CLJS and equivalently tested native\\\\\\\\ntransitions','oracle behavior in CLJS and equivalently tested in-instance Rust\\\\\\\\ntransitions')\\ny=y.replace('native wasm embedder needs Level-2 engine services','in-instance wasm engine needs Level-2 engine services').replace('native standalone wasm parity','in-instance wasm parity').replace('native facade checks','internal facade checks')\\ny=block(y,'reference is an arena entry. Numeric computation','### 2.2 Private linear-memory layout', '''reference is an arena entry. Numeric operations run in the linked \\\"'`yin-prims`\\nand shared Value implementation, matching the CLJS oracle'\\\"'s declared carriers,\\nfloat behavior, equality and metadata. Only references 0/1 are false.\\n\\nThe per-task arena is inside wasm linear memory. The descriptor table has kind,\\ngeneration, task-local slot and optional machine-record offset. The internal\\nhandle↔Value facade owns the shared Rust Values through safe Clone/Drop; it does\\nnot serialize them between body and engine. No JS/native pointer is a ValueRef.\\nSee [yin.vm.engine.md](./yin.vm.engine.md) §E.2.1–§E.2.3 for ownership, collection,\\ntarget-layout gates and the **two repository codecs**. Internal values retain\\ntheir fidelity even when existing custody/encoding predicates refuse transport.\\n\\nHandles keep codegen register locals uniform and avoid externref or i64 host-call\\narguments. The cost is arena indirection, not a service crossing per primitive.\\nA scalar fast path remains a measured optimization with explicit numeric laws;\\nit cannot change the public image contract.''')\\ny=y.replace('ABI version, run status','Internal layout version, run status').replace('host-state token','engine-state token').replace('in the host payload','in the materialized payload').replace('Kind/generation/host slot','Kind/generation/arena slot').replace('Host slot contains','Arena slot contains')\\ny=y.replace('embedder’s persistent map','in-instance engine’s persistent map').replace('the map and updates are addressable through ABI value\\\\\\\\ndefinitions/state messages on every runtime.','the map and updates use direct internal helpers on every runtime.')\\ny=block(y,'The instance exports conceptual','Emission is byte-deterministic:', '''The linked instance exposes lifecycle run/drive entry points and test/inspection\\nstep/read-state/snapshot-install hooks, not semantic service imports. Its external\\nfunctions are the stream adapters specified once in\\n[yin.vm.engine.md](./yin.vm.engine.md) §E.3. Components share one task-owned,\\nnon-shared wasm32 memory and original function table, with immutable i32 base\\noffsets where needed. Memory/table provision and internal component helper\\nimports are linkage mechanics. Final external semantic imports are allowlisted\\nto the stream binding. No host-call signature passes i64, externref, Rust\\npointers, JS values, strings or multi-value results; i64 pc/numeric bytes live\\nin memory and core i64 computation remains allowed inside wasm.\\n\\nTRANSFER/YIELDED/BLOCKED/PARKED/HALTED/ERROR below denote internal driver control,\\nnot serialized service statuses or adapter results. Public state preserves exact\\nflags, value and queues. **Blocking is a normal return, never a trap.** Memory\\nor validation traps are backend faults, not blocked or FFI error envelopes.''')\\ny=y.replace('emitter/ABI version','emitter/internal-layout version')\\ny=y.replace('retained as a W6 optimization axis with services moved into wasm if serialized crossing cost dominates.','available as the in-instance interpreter fallback and a W6 codegen comparison; engine placement is already fixed.')\\ny=block(y,'is an implementation dispatch, not the guest call stack. On HOST_SERVICE','Consequently flattening', '''is an implementation dispatch, not the guest call stack. State-consuming helper\\nsites return to a shared **internal helper thunk** after materialization. The\\nthunk directly calls the linked engine using the same memory, then reloads the\\ncommitted state and continues or returns blocked/error. This single replaceable\\nsite centralizes synchronization and lets later codegen inline proven-safe calls;\\nit has no serialized mailbox or external service protocol. No guest activation\\nremains across an asynchronous wait. The same body/engine binaries run on every\\nembedder.''')\\ny=y.replace('several host service transitions implement it','several internal engine transitions implement it')\\ny=block(y,'Here \\\"'`r` means ValueRef local'\\\"','| Opcode | Lowering and observable behavior |', '''Here \\\"'`r` means ValueRef local and `next` means pc+1. The retained r5 rows use\\n“Host”/“host fn” to name the oracle'\\\"'s kernel-helper/primitive cases: in this\\narchitecture these **all execute inside the linked Rust engine**, through the\\ninternal thunk (§4), never through an imported engine service. Pending scratch\\nretains already-read operands so continuation/re-entry cannot repeat effects.\\nThe rows specify transitions; generated dispatch invokes shared helpers rather\\nthan copying semantic transition implementations.''')\\ny=y.replace('### 3.4 Exact host-function effect completion','### 3.4 Exact primitive (oracle host-function) effect completion').replace('Host-side payload materialization','In-instance payload materialization')\\ny=block(y,'## 4. Target interface specification:','## 5. Per-image-row compilation', '''## 4. Target linkage: in-instance engine and dao.stream binding\\n\\nThe compiler links \\\"'`yin-value`, `yin-scalar`, `yin-image`, `yin-kernel`,\\n`yin-prims`, `yin-engine` and `yin-wire` for `wasm32-unknown-unknown`. The\\nbinding'\\\"'s operations, signatures, Jing payloads and stream outcomes have one\\nhome: [yin.vm.engine.md](./yin.vm.engine.md) §E.3. The compiler emits no private\\nservice protocol, codec, transport-status decoder or response-refetch loop.\\nThe owner boundary clarification of 2026-10-19 supersedes the former target\\ninterface and its earlier performance-based exception.\\n\\nCompiled bodies and engine functions reach each other by **direct calls and\\nshared linear memory**, internal linkage within the task's instance group.\\nThe shared helper thunk is the one replaceable generated call site. It flushes\\nthe eight register fields, calls the owning crate's transition, and adopts the\\nreturned state before opcode-specific completion (§3.4). Full fields and barriers\\nare owned by [yin.vm.debruijn.register.md](./yin.vm.debruijn.register.md) §10.1\\nand [yin.vm.engine.md](./yin.vm.engine.md) §E.1; they are not import messages.\\n\\nThe compiler owns pc dispatch, operand access, branch structure and entry maps.\\nShared helpers own call binding/return/restore, ownership, primitives, stores,\\ncollection and effects. This resolves the old compiled-call/service division\\nwithout duplicating transitions. The in-instance interpreter reaches those same\\nhelpers. An imported helper between separately instantiated linked components\\nis private bundle linkage, not a composition-provided semantic operation.\\n\\nOnly the in-instance engine's stream adapter layer calls external semantic\\nimports. It encodes canonical Jing data and consumes the exact stream outcomes.\\nResolve-var, frame/root materialization and scheduler operations never become\\nstream history. The compiler must preserve the thunk barrier on every path\\nthat can collect, park, attach or return a snapshot. External bindings carry no\\nlive stack frames, engine records or ValueRef definitions.''')\\ny=y.replace('the same \\\"'`yin.vm.engine.host.v1` ABI memory and services.'\\\"','the same task memory/table and linked in-instance engine helpers.')\\ny=block(y,'Dynamic instantiation/attachment occurs outside an active exchange','The same component bytes', '''Dynamic code instantiation uses the runtime's module lifecycle at a quiescent,\\nmaterialized boundary, never a reentrant semantic service call. Core wasm cannot\\ninstantiate new modules itself: the composition installs derived cached body\\nfunctions via the embedding API; this physical linkage does not perform guest\\nattachment/admission. The in-instance engine owns that transaction and its\\nstate publication. Without a ready artifact, execute the attached image in the\\nsame in-instance interpreter; cache preparation cannot change guest semantics.\\nArtifact preparation/availability is node-local, never a portable module field.\\n''') if 'The same component bytes' in y else y\\n# previous replacement introduced paragraph spacing only; preserve remainder\\ny=y.replace('Host-side \\\"'`spawn-module`'\\\"','In-instance \\\"'`spawn-module`'\\\"').replace('wasm ABI/emitter version','wasm layout/emitter version').replace('translator and ABI versions','translator and internal-layout versions').replace(\\\\\\\"same host’s register interpreter\\\\\\\",\\\\\\\"same in-instance register interpreter\\\\\\\")\\ny=block(y,'For the lowering pipeline specifically:','### 6.2 Lowering non-goals and risks', '''For the lowering pipeline specifically (acceptance detail is in register §10.5):\\n\\n- **W0 — Oracle and feasibility:** export/extend K0 from CLJS first; freeze admitted images/R/traces/refusals; probe crate wasm32 compilation, linker/profile, internal layout and stream-binding bounds. Pin deterministic emitter/assembler. No host-service fallback.\\n- **W1 — In-instance pure execution:** link the required Rust engine/primitive/value/image crates and compiled body dispatcher; prove actual wasm32 execution and single-step parity under Node + Wasmtime.\\n- **W2 — Continuations:** compile sparse capture/restore, internal continuation calls and park/resume against the linked engine; retain layout/custody/refusal and snapshot gates.\\n- **W3 — Effects:** use Jing stream adapters with the in-instance scheduler, GC, custody and two-step FFI; test full/blocked and once-only retry on Node + Wasmtime.\\n- **W4 — Modules:** compile per-image rows, relocate once, install artifacts at materialized boundaries, preserve attach/spawn/store/GC law and CLJS↔wasm module-slice parity.\\n- **W5 — Complete lane:** all 23 opcodes and complete W1–W5 full traces on Node, Wasmtime and Wasmer; deterministic binaries and external import/profile audit. Wasmer's formerly W4 full-suite gate is superseded by this owner-directed schedule, not removed.\\n- **W6 — Codegen optimization only:** measure compile latency, binary size, dispatch/indirection, stack depth, arena/heap retention, call/suspension, stream-byte/copy time and end-to-end time. Compare compiled bodies with the same in-instance interpreter; coalesce/structure blocks, reduce redundant spills or add pinned Binaryen only with full three-runtime parity. No engine migration or architecture-selection experiment remains. K3 may use the same benchmark harness; K4 still requires owner review.\\n\\nThe ruling supersedes the former W0–W5 private-mailbox work and W6 service\\nmigration. Correctness gates survive in their in-instance or stream-binding\\nform; two repository codecs and the shared oracle remain mandatory.''')\\ny=y.replace('restore/call helper names are existing source semantics, whereas exchange/fetch-response, ABI\\\\\\\\nstatuses and all native implementation entry names are proposed protocol vocabulary.','restore/call helper names are oracle semantics; planned internal Rust helper and\\\\\\\\nphysical binding names are not claims of implemented symbols.').replace('New ABI names and native entry names','New binding names and internal entry names')\\ny=y[:y.index('## Open items')]+'''## Open items\\n\\nThe owner ruling resolves the former compiled-call/service split (§4) and\\nremoves the response-refetch question with the dissolved protocol. W0 must\\nprove crate/linker wasm32 feasibility and the baseline feature audit. The\\nengine's remaining binding-reservation and noncanonical internal-ordering gates\\nare in [yin.vm.engine.md](./yin.vm.engine.md) §E.5.5. No new protocol is a fallback.\\n'''\\n# Register extension only\\nidx=r.index('## 10. Wasm execution instance'); prefix=r[:idx]; ext=r[idx:]\\next=block(ext,'> Owner rulings and decomposition lineage','Authorities and companion specifications', '''> Owner boundary clarification, 2026-10-19, supersedes the body-lane-first\\n> architecture and historical \\\"'`yin.vm.engine.host.v1` service protocol (formerly\\n> `yin.register.host.v1`). The Rust engine now executes inside wasm; eight-field\\n> synchronization is internal, and external imports bind only dao.stream.\\n> Private TLV/comparator/status/refetch machinery and W6 engine migration are\\n> deleted. Fixtures and register transition semantics survive unchanged.\\n> Wasmer'\\\"'s full-suite gate moves from W4 to W5 under the revised schedule.\\n>\\n> Earlier decomposition/fold provenance remains: engine method/binding live in\\n> engine §E; this section holds register state, fixtures and gates. The dated\\n> correction of historical \\\"'`:store-updates` remains: ready entries merge\\n> `:resource-updates` into private `:resources`, refusing non-keyword keys with\\n> `{:rule :resource-update-key}`.'\\\"''')\\next=ext.replace('the guest-agnostic execution host, \\\"'`yin.vm.engine.host.v1` service protocol'\\\"','the in-instance engine and dao.stream wasm binding').replace('lowerings targeting \\\"'`yin.vm.engine.host.v1`'\\\"','lowerings linked to the in-instance engine')\\next=block(ext,'This section defines the **register-machine instance**','#### 10.1.1 ', '''This is the register instance (contract version 4 / \\\\\\\"r2\\\\\\\", 23 opcodes) of the\\nin-instance engine in [yin.vm.engine.md](./yin.vm.engine.md) §E.1–§E.5.\\n[yang.wasm.md](./yang.wasm.md) compiles admitted register images and links the\\nsame Rust engine into wasm under Node/Wasmtime/Wasmer. The CLJS kernel is the\\noracle. Arena/Value facade and both repository codecs live inside the instance;\\nonly dao.stream adapters (§E.3) cross into the composition. The single-port rule\\nand Rust reconciliations remain §E.4.''')\\next=ext.replace('on\\\\\\\\nthe VM state map:','on\\\\\\\\nthe authoritative in-instance state record (the oracle uses a VM map):')\\next=ext.replace('**Public entry / host-to-wasm:**','**Engine-to-body entry:**').replace('accept the supplied map as authoritative','accept the supplied named-field snapshot as authoritative').replace('**Wasm-to-host:**','**Body-to-engine:**').replace('**Host completion:** the returned host state','**Engine completion:** the returned engine state').replace('current opcode\\\\\\\\'s specified post-service changes','current opcode\\\\\\\\'s specified post-helper changes').replace('pre-service header','pre-helper header').replace('before a host service','before an engine helper').replace('after service completion','after helper completion').replace('before the adapter\\\\\\\\nreceives its result','before the caller\\\\\\\\nreceives its result').replace('before every return to the host','before every return to the engine driver').replace('map-based\\\\\\\\n\\\"'`:registers`'\\\"','state-record\\\\\\\\n\\\"'`:registers`'\\\"').replace('Keep the record\\\\\\\\'s protocol implementations so \\\"'`module/gc-roots` can inspect the current map. Dense memory synchronization without map synchronization is insufficient.'\\\"','Preserve equivalent \\\"'`module/gc-roots` dispatch over the current record. Dense shadow synchronization without state-record synchronization is insufficient.'\\\"')\\next=ext.replace('IDs or tag-\\\"'`13` arena references'\\\"','IDs or arena references').replace('Expose one adapter restore','Implement one in-instance restore')\\next=block(ext,'Same-layout native payloads with transportable values','If a future authorized transport', '''Same-layout native payloads with admissible values round-trip between reference\\nand wasm execution through conformance/inspection facilities. Inside one task,\\ntrusted wrappers remain arena Values; there is no serialized handle service.\\nThis grants no external portability. The actual module/linker path retains\\n\\\"'`:yin.k/non-portable` / `:non-canonicalizable` continuation refusal in\\n`engine/encoder`. Only admitted canonical stream values use\\n[yin.vm.engine.md](./yin.vm.engine.md) §E.3.'\\\"''')\\next=block(ext,\\\\\\\"table are specified in [\\\"'`yang.wasm.md`\\\",'\\\"'Under the \\\"'`spawn-module` isolation rules'\\\"', '''table are specified in [yang.wasm.md](./yang.wasm.md) §5. The composition's\\nruntime lifecycle installs derived body functions only at materialized boundaries;\\nthe **in-instance engine** owns admission/attachment and publishes its layout.\\nMissing artifacts use its own register interpreter. This is runtime linkage,\\nnot an imported engine service. \\\"'`reset` preserves offset rows; `load-image`\\nreplaces code space and resets execution, so old membership checks can fail.'\\\"''')\\next=ext.replace('\\\"'`yin.vm.engine.host.v1` ABI/emitter version'\\\"','internal layout/emitter version').replace(\\\\\\\"same host's register interpreter\\\\\\\",\\\\\\\"in-instance register interpreter\\\\\\\")\\n# fixture section byte preserved\\nfixture=part(ro,'### 10.4 ','### 10.5 ')\\next=block(ext,'### 10.5 Register-specific','#### 10.5.1 ', '''### 10.5 Register-specific W0–W6 implementation slices and acceptance gates\\n\\nThe node-lane schedule replaces private-service-first staging by owner ruling,\\n2026-10-19. Toolchain pins, oracle-first K0/W0, two-codec determinism, wasm32\\ncompilability and dao.stream binding probes live in\\n[yin.vm.engine.md](./yin.vm.engine.md) §E.5.2–§E.5.3. Compiler work lives in\\n[yang.wasm.md](./yang.wasm.md) §6.1. Each gate below retains the existing\\nregister acceptance assertions, now against the in-instance engine.''')\\next=ext.replace('#### 10.5.1 W0 — Register admission, codec, and eight-field synchronization gates','#### 10.5.1 W0 — Oracle first, admission, wasm32 feasibility and internal state')\\next=ext.replace('Acceptance: all semantic goldens retain','First export/extend K0 from the CLJS oracle; probe the shared crates on\\\\\\\\n\\\"'`wasm32-unknown-unknown` with the final-module feature allowlist and adapter'\\\"\\\\\\\\nreservation/handle/CBOR tests. No service-library substitute is allowed.\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\nAcceptance: all semantic goldens retain',1).replace('byte-message equivalents','internal snapshot equivalents')\\next=ext.replace('step/ABI traces; provide those services through the shared Rust crates and\\\\\\\\nrequire the Rust interpreter to pass the same pure fixtures.','step/stream traces. The required shared Rust crates must actually compile, link\\\\\\\\nand run inside wasm; require their interpreter dispatcher to pass the same pure\\\\\\\\nfixtures. Failure of wasm32/profile feasibility blocks W1.').replace('scheduler/event/ABI traces','scheduler/event/stream-binding traces').replace('native scheduler, custody, collection and\\\\\\\\ntwo-step FFI services','in-instance scheduler, custody, collection and\\\\\\\\ntwo-step FFI').replace('typed effect mailbox completion','typed effect completion inside the engine')\\next=block(ext,'W3 also forces \\\"'`NEED_SPACE`'\\\"','#### 10.5.5 ', '''W3 repeats W0 binding probes against real effects: full-before-append, response\\nreservation before execution, all stream terminal/error outcomes, bounds/CBOR/\\nhandle failures with no accidental mutation, and no automatic retry after an\\nunknown-acceptance transport error. Full writer wake converts once to the FFI\\nreader: no second append/id/GC/resource update. Compare resulting records and\\ncanonical stream bytes on Node and Wasmtime; Wasmer repeats by W5. There is\\nno response-refetch protocol. Internal gensym/park/GC never cross adapters.''')\\next=ext.replace('module/state/ABI traces','module/state/stream traces').replace('native admission, module stores and resource/closure transport. Bring up Wasmer\\\\\\\\nwith the same native service library and run W1–W4 suites before W5.','in-instance admission, module stores and resource/closure transport. Prepare\\\\\\\\nthe Wasmer binding; its complete suite is required by W5.').replace('**W4 does not pass until Wasmer passes the entire W1–W4 full-trace suite** on\\\\\\\\nthe identical binaries and its native service, alongside Node and Wasmtime.','Node and Wasmtime must pass the identical binaries now; Wasmer must pass the\\\\\\\\nentire W1–W4 suite as part of W5 (dated owner schedule supersession above).')\\next=ext.replace('native↔CLJS module-slice','in-wasm↔CLJS module-slice').replace('receiving native hosts','receiving in-instance engines')\\next=ext.replace('with Node/CLJS, Wasmtime and\\\\\\\\nWasmer adapters','with Node, Wasmtime and\\\\\\\\nWasmer stream adapters').replace('\\\"'`yin.vm.engine.host.v1` ABI versioning'\\\"','the dao.stream binding/profile and internal linkage versioning').replace('byte-identical artifacts, \\\"'`yin.vm.engine.host.v1`'\\\"\\\\\\\\nimport allowlist, malformed ABI/overflow/\\\"'`NEED_SPACE`/repeated-fetch handling,'\\\"','byte-identical artifacts, dao.stream external\\\\\\\\nimport allowlist, malformed CBOR/bounds/overflow/reservation/full handling,')\\next=block(ext,'Keep Node + Wasmtime + Wasmer full-trace parity mandatory for every\\\\\\\\noptimization.','Register-specific non-goals', '''Keep Node + Wasmtime + Wasmer full-trace parity mandatory for every\\noptimization. Measure compilation latency, binary size, dispatch/indirection,\\nnative stack depth, arena/heap retention, call/suspension, stream bytes/copy/codec\\ncost and end-to-end time against the same in-instance register interpreter.\\nOnly then coalesce/structure blocks, reduce redundant spills or use pinned\\nBinaryen passes ([yang.wasm.md](./yang.wasm.md) §6.1). Re-run all parity and\\nprofile gates. Engine placement is settled; W6 is **codegen optimization only**,\\nnot a service-migration or alternative architecture experiment. K3 may measure\\nwasm on the shared harness; this does not commission K4. Making wasm work is\\nnot evidence it is faster.''')\\next=ext[:ext.index('**Reference conventions and open items:**')]+'''**Reference conventions and open items:** follow\\n[yin.vm.engine.md](./yin.vm.engine.md) §E.5.5 and\\n[yang.wasm.md](./yang.wasm.md), Reference conventions / Open items. The former\\nservice-call split and response-refetch questions are superseded by the owner\\nruling. Remaining W0 feasibility/binding/ordering gates do not change the\\nregister fixture schema or restore semantics.\\n'''\\nassert part(ext,'### 10.4 ','### 10.5 ')==fixture\\nassert part(y,'| Opcode | Lowering','### 3.4 ')==part(original,'| Opcode | Lowering','### 3.4 ')\\nassert part(y,'### 3.2 ','### 3.3 ').replace('internal engine','host service')==part(original,'### 3.2 ','### 3.3 ')\\nr=prefix+ext\\nrust=rust.replace('§E.1–§E.5, guest-agnostic execution host, \\\"'`yin.vm.engine.host.v1` ABI, and K0/W0 conformance method'\\\"','§E.1–§E.5, in-instance engine, dao.stream wasm binding, and K0/W0 conformance method')\\nrust+='2026-10-19 owner boundary clarification: the wasm plan now depends on compiling the K-phase Rust crates to wasm32-unknown-unknown as its in-instance engine; the composition supplies only dao.stream adapters. This pointer supersedes the earlier footer’s service-protocol description; revision 2 design-only status, file name and coordinates 1–1334 remain unchanged.\\\\\\\\n'\\nassert ''.join(rust.splitlines(True)[:1334])==rusthead\\np.write_text(y); rpath.write_text(r); rustpath.write_text(rust)\\nprint('wrote compiler/register/rust pointers; asserted exact 23 opcode rows, fixture schema, rust prefix')\\nPY\\\"\",\"aggregated_output\":\"Traceback (most recent call last):\\n  File \\\"<stdin>\\\", line 244, in <module>\\nAssertionError\\n\",\"exit_code\":1,\"status\":\"failed\"}}\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
