roken links []\nyin.vm.debruijn.register lines 1711 broken links []\nyin.vm.rust-kernel lines 1339 broken links []\n  File \"/Applications/Xcode.app/Contents/Developer/Library/Frameworks/Python3.framework/Versions/3.9/lib/python3.9/subprocess.py\", line 424, in check_output\n    return run(*popenargs, stdout=PIPE, timeout=timeout, check=True,\n  File \"/Applications/Xcode.app/Contents/Developer/Library/Frameworks/Python3.framework/Versions/3.9/lib/python3.9/subprocess.py\", line 528, in run\n    raise CalledProcessError(retcode, process.args,\nsubprocess.CalledProcessError: Command '['git', 'show', 'HEAD:docs/design/yang.wasm.md']' returned non-zero exit status 128.\n15:body-lane-first architecture and `yin.vm.engine.host.v1` service target\n16:(historically renamed from `yin.register.host.v1`) are superseded. Compile the\n19:operation vocabulary, TLV codec/comparator, service statuses and response-refetch\n25:§10. This plan owns compiler mechanics, not host service implementation.\n90:defaults are not the feature contract. Uneven native-runtime support for post-MVP features is\n96:WASI stays outside the VM core and its imports. A WASI-based embedder may implement stream\n101:Read authorities (source symbols are semantic specifications for every embedder; calls to\n103:transitions, not a requirement for native hosts to load Clojure):\n110:- The October amendments to the register and semantic designs make **A the code identity**. R is the register image checksum. Existing `:hash`, `:image`, and offset rows still use R for native compatibility. Do not publish wasm binaries as a new code identity, fetch code by a wasm checksum, or bypass A-to-R admission. Retain access to the Universal AST through existing provenance/addressing.\n112:- Current `vm/machine-data?`, used by register restore and payload validation, recursively accepts kernel-minted Closure/Continuation wrappers by their payloads, as well as exact-integer carriers. It is not identical to `plain-data?`. Arbitrary host functions/errors remain invalid. Preserve the predicate, not an approximate JSON test.\n113:- The current module-slice encoder in `engine/encoder` explicitly refuses reified and parked continuations with `:yin.k/status :yin.k/non-portable`, kind `:non-canonicalizable`. Thus universal linker transport of every register continuation is not already implemented. This lane must support the existing native payload where admissible, and preserve qualified refusals elsewhere; it must not silently promise a new universal migration mechanism.\n118:the native implementation: status and subordination at lines 3–35, crates at 1134–1158,\n122:they neither commission K4 LLVM nor the unrelated standalone Rust node/transports. Where native\n133:- Rust K-D2b uses u32 indices and qualified host limits (lines 434–448, 1245–1246); wasm keeps 64-bit **wire pc fields**, not a promise of unrestricted native execution. The internal facade checks every pc/operand/attachment against the Rust domain and declared limits before conversion or mutation and returns the same qualified `:host-limit`. W0 records the common accepted profile; tests outside it require declared refusals, never truncation or a second widened Rust Machine. Wide register tests above 65,535 still fit u32.\n134:- Rust phases exclude existing CLJC edits (lines 93–95, 1164–1165). The deterministic service traversal policy in [`yin.vm.engine.md`](./yin.vm.engine.md) §E.4.3 (r5 §4.1.2) is a separately identified **wasm-driven shared-reference stabilization prerequisite**, outside that K-phase restriction; it remains an explicit scoped amendment for implementation review, not permission for a second native ordering policy. Export a versioned extension of the common kit after that shared change, preserving original goldens/history. This specification changes no source.\n136:- The in-instance wasm engine needs Level-2 engine services in W2–W4 even though the Rust standalone-node roadmap leaves K5 last (§6.3, lines 748–781; §10, lines 1170–1182). Develop/reuse those **same crates** to the slice's required coverage ([`yin.vm.engine.md`](./yin.vm.engine.md) §E.4.2); a Level-1 offload service cannot satisfy in-instance wasm parity. This schedules the necessary library subset for wasm, not the full K5 node or a new Rust network client.\n162:not serialize them between body and engine. No JS/native pointer is a ValueRef.\n167:Handles keep codegen register locals uniform and avoid externref or i64 host-call\n168:arguments. The cost is arena indirection, not a service crossing per primitive.\n197:host integer value and advances only through engine operations.\n214:step/read-state/snapshot-install hooks, not semantic service imports. Its external\n220:to the stream binding. No host-call signature passes i64, externref, Rust\n225:not serialized service statuses or adapter results. Public state preserves exact\n236:### 3.1 Choice among the alternatives\n240:| Fully structured body functions with direct guest `call` | Small fast code for ordinary functions; conventional optimizer input | Captured continuations require every caller activation to be materialized and abandoned; arbitrary resume PCs need secondary entries; suspension cannot retain native stack frames. A shadow stack alone does not unwind native callers. |\n242:| **Compiled body dispatch plus trampoline** | Static register locals and operands, arbitrary entry PCs, appendable bodies, constant native stack depth, explicit continuations | More generated code than interpreter; indirect dispatch and spills at transfers; per-body branch tables may be large. |\n255:it has no serialized mailbox or external service protocol. No guest activation\n257:embedder.\n262:walk, Asyncify, native-stack copying, wasm exception unwinding, or retained JS Promise\n266:the active lexical chain/register file. This gives bounded native stack depth on engines without\n275:entry-pc dispatch, live sets as spill maps and data guest calls; their native control\n278:service implementation. An artifact may be discarded at a materialized boundary and execution\n293:“Host”/“host fn” to name the oracle's kernel-helper/primitive cases: in this\n295:internal thunk (§4), never through an imported engine service. Pending scratch\n326:### 3.4 Exact primitive (oracle host-function) effect completion\n345:changed. A non-effect host result follows the separate plain-result arm of `register.cljc`,\n394:service protocol, codec, transport-status decoder or response-refetch loop.\n408:collection and effects. This resolves the old compiled-call/service division\n452:section. The baseline preallocates table capacity (W0 test profile: 65,536 slots) and uses host\n454:refusal. No guest `table.grow`/`table.set` instruction is required. Optional host-API growth\n457:materialized boundary, never a reentrant semantic service call. Core wasm cannot\n501:- **W0 — Oracle and feasibility:** export/extend K0 from CLJS first; freeze admitted images/R/traces/refusals; probe crate wasm32 compilation, linker/profile, internal layout and stream-binding bounds. Pin deterministic emitter/assembler. No host-service fallback.\n509:The ruling supersedes the former W0–W5 private-mailbox work and W6 service\n515:Non-goals for the lowering: no new opcode/contract version, alternate A→R allocator, native\n517:portable continuation schema, direct arbitrary host FFI imports, WASI imports in the VM core,\n518:threads/shared memory, WasmGC dependency, numeric NaN boxing, cross-node artifact service, or\n523:Lowering-specific risks (retained from r5 §7; host/runtime risks in\n526:- **Large bodies and dispatch size.** One instruction per case can create deep block nesting and large binaries. W0 should pin practical limits; W6 can shard dispatch into bounded pages/helpers while keeping entry-pc precision. Such helpers must never hold suspended guest state in their native stack.\n559:The owner ruling resolves the former compiled-call/service split (§4) and\nExecution fields and roots must be current before entry. Arena retention cannot\nsubstitute for the engine's exact logical heap tracing rules.\n\nThe program store is logically an immutable map, usually name-keyed but **not\nrestricted to names**: `:store-get`/`:store-put` accept `:data` keys,\nincluding keywords and numbers in the corpus. Its linear-memory\nrepresentation is a root `ValueRef` to the in-instance engine's persistent map, not a\nsecond wasm hash map; all access and updates use direct in-instance helpers. All writes go through\n`engine/put-active` → `engine/store-put`. Non-nil `store-of` selects only\nthat module's store, with no ambient fallback. Private `:resources` and heap\nare distinct from the program store.\n\nThe engine state roots retained across compiled-body and engine synchronization\ninclude `ValueRef`s/tokens for `store`, `module-stores`, `free-env`,\n`primitives`, `modules`, `wait-set`, `ready-queue`, `parked`, `resources`,\n`heap`/`gc`, `owner`, `origin`/`ancestry`, `id-counter`, `:yin.k/gate`,\n`:yin.k/issued`, and `:yin.k/closes`, preserving all other extension keys as\nwell. The actual issue-counter key is `:yin.k/issued`, distinct from\n`id-counter`; do not invent `:yin.k/next-issue`. These are state/arena\nretention roots, not an expansion of the engine's logical heap-GC root set.\nThe full existing state map is authoritative for engine extension keys.\n\n### E.2 The in-instance value arena and two repository codecs\n\n#### E.2.1 Task-owned handles and the internal handle↔Value facade\n\ndecimal/rational forms, collection ordering, metadata stripping/validation,\nequality-collapse and duplicate checks, depth limits, surrogate refusals and\ndecode/re-encode identity. In-machine values may retain a lone UTF-16 surrogate; it remains unhashable\nby either repository codec. Keep that domain distinction and the existing qualified refusal.\n`jing/content-hash` digests canonical CBOR bytes directly and returns\nlowercase hex; `jing/segment-key` applies the exact\nnamespace/algorithm/digest spelling. The default here is BLAKE3; any\nadvertised alternative algorithm must reproduce the repository registry's\nbehavior rather than aliasing BLAKE3.\n\nRequired formulas are the actual inputs in source: owner = content-hash of\n`[:yin.k/owner secret]` (nil secret → nil owner); seal = content-hash of\n`[:yin.k/seal secret kind id]` with `issue-ref`'s missing-secret refusal;\nprimitive profile = content-hash of the exact description in\n`vm/primitive-profile`, followed by the `:yin.k.pp/blake3-…` keyword\nconstruction; module address = segment-key of the manifest. Preserve A's\nexisting canonical construction as well. Equal test secrets, identities and\ninput data must yield **bit-identical** R, owner, seal, profile and address\nacross CLJS and Rust. Do not normalize these fields away or let the in-instance engine trust supplied digest strings instead of recomputing them.\n\nAssign executable scalar encoding to `yin-scalar`, image/descriptor/R and\nadmission to `yin-image`, Clojure values/equality to `yin-value`, primitive\nprofiles to `yin-prims` with engine composition, and Jing payload/content\nserialization to `yin-wire` using the canonical Jing implementation. The\ninternal facade calls these same crates for identity bytes and hashes. These are the crate responsibilities in\n`docs/design/yin.vm.rust-kernel.md:1136–1145`, extended for the current\ncontracts; never duplicate codec ports inside each runtime wrapper. W0\nfreezes/verifies their oracle vectors before execution gates; W1 proves the\nshared implementations run inside wasm. Reuse `test/resources/dao/jing/cbor-v1.json` and its README/errata plus\n`test/dao/jing/cbor_conformance_test.cljc`,\n`test/dao/jing/hash_registry_contract_test.cljc`,\n`test/yin/vm/debruijn_code_test.cljc` and\n`test/yin/vm/debruijn_register_code_test.cljc`; do not regenerate existing\ngoldens. Add cross-embedder vectors for descriptor-hash/R (including empty and\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
