{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_30","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/zsh -lc \"git ls-files docs/design/{yang.wasm,yin.vm.rust-kernel}.md; sed -n '100,123p' docs/design/dao.stream.md; sed -n '130,150p' docs/design/yin.vm.rust-kernel.md; sed -n '420,438p' docs/design/yang.wasm.md; rg -n 'local-arena|host service|service state|native service|three encoding|host implementation|native embedder|ABI' docs/design/yin.vm.engine.md\"","aggregated_output":"## Result Convention\n\nEvery DaoStream operation returns data.\n\n- Each operation's set of outcomes is **exhaustive**: a transport may produce\n  fewer of them, never any outside the set. Which subset it produces is part of\n  its declared nature (see Surfaces). An outcome is a map whose\n  `:dao.stream/outcome` key names the outcome with a qualified keyword. Every\n  operation returns such a map — including projections like `descriptor` and\n  `cursor`, whose success map carries the projected value.\n- `:dao.stream/outcome` is present in every result map and is never repeated\n  in the per-outcome tables below; the keys those tables name are required in\n  addition to it.\n- Result maps are **open**: the contract fixes which keys are required per\n  outcome, and a consumer must ignore keys it does not understand. This is\n  what allows future extensions (for example readiness notification) to be\n  added without breaking existing consumers.\n- Each outcome set is exhaustive **for this version of the contract**. A\n  consumer that receives an outcome it does not recognize treats it as: not\n  `ok`; nothing observed; for a write, effect unknown and no automatic\n  retry; and not retryable unless the result map carries\n  `:dao.stream/retry? true`. It does not throw, because an unrecognized\n  outcome is a newer contract, not a defect in this host's assembly. A\n  transport may carry `:dao.stream/retry? true` on any non-`ok` outcome to\n    |                          | values (5.4). Native code never calls a   |\n    |                          | yin closure on the native stack (7.3).    |\n    | No callbacks             | The kernel invokes no supplied function   |\n    |                          | except pure primitives and `dao.stream`   |\n    |                          | handle operations. The LLVM worker is     |\n    |                          | reached by a request datum on a stream    |\n    |                          | and answers on a stream (8.3); no         |\n    |                          | compile-finished callback exists.         |\n    | No shared mutable state  | A `Machine` is owned by exactly one       |\n    |                          | driver. Values are reference-counted with |\n    |                          | `Rc`, which is neither `Send` nor `Sync`: |\n    |                          | the compiler refuses to share a machine's |\n    |                          | values across threads (4.6).              |\n    | No layer collapsing      | Decode/validate (image crate), execute    |\n    |                          | (machine), schedule (engine), translate   |\n    |                          | to IR (translator) and generate machine   |\n    |                          | code (LLVM worker) are five components    |\n    |                          | with data between each (3, 7.1).          |\n    | No assumed graphs        | The pc-to-body index, the basic-block     |\n    |                          | graph and the offset table are built by   |\n    |                          | explicit passes over validated tuples at  |\n## 5. Per-image-row compilation, relocation, and artifact-cache law\n\nCompile each independently admitted image into a wasm Module whose body entry functions import\nthe same task memory/table and linked in-instance engine helpers. Instantiate it for the receiving task\nand install its functions into the task's imported function table using the runtime embedding\nAPI. This table maps implementation body ids to uniform functions; it is not the guest offset\ntable.\n\nAttach is a staged, append-only transaction:\n\n1. Apply the same contract/validator gates as `attach-image`. Empty images are no-ops; duplicate component R is a no-op. Production admission retains the existing A-to-R derivation check before executable attachment.\n2. Compute offset = held instruction count. Relocate every operand whose opcode-table kind is `:pc`; shift body start/end. Compute the exact combined segment and `register-hash`, and append `[component-R offset length]`.\n3. Compile/instantiate the new component, reserve directory/table capacity, install its body entries, then publish the new code-layout generation atomically. Prefer component-relative constants plus an instance base offset so the same compiled Module can serve different task layouts. Every pc generated from a component-relative coordinate adds the instance base exactly once before entering public state: header/exit pc, payload site/next pc, return-frame site/return pc, and minted closure body-pc. Incoming absolute restore/return pcs are already absolute and must not receive another base. Resolve absolute pcs through the combined body directory and subtract the selected body start for local dispatch.\n4. Preserve pc, registers, frames, K, all pending payloads, stores, and queues. No existing function slot or instruction pc is renumbered; old compiled instances remain reachable. Failure before publish leaves the old task executable. Attach only while wasm is at a materialized boundary.\n\nFor every boundary payload, fetch the instruction as `(nth (:instructions (:segment vm)) (:pc\nvm))` from the current relocated **combined** segment at the materialized absolute site pc. Pass\nthat instruction and current segment/hash/images to `effects/continuation-payload`; never pair\ncomponent-relative site metadata or a component-only segment with absolute payload pcs. The\n553:semantic operations, private codec or second ABI.\n897:wasm. An unavailable crate dependency blocks the lane; no host service fallback.\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
